OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026", "title": "Square-lattice FK interfaces and nested loops for 1 <= q < 4", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "223", "family_title": "Random-cluster interfaces: critical, disordered, thermal, and natural-time scaling", "abstract_html": "For every fixed \\(1\\le q\\lt 4\\), critical square-lattice random-cluster Dobrushin interfaces converge as ordered curves to chordal \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{SLE}}\\nolimits _{\\kappa(q)}\\), where \\(\\kappa(q)=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)\\), confirming the Rohde–Schramm prediction in this parameter range. This holds in every bounded Jordan domain under uniform marked boundary approximation. The complete nested plane loop collections converge to whole-plane \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{CLE}}\\nolimits _{\\kappa(q)}\\) in a spherical matching topology retaining multiplicities and traversals. At <i>q</i> = 1 we obtain Cardy's formula for square-lattice bond percolation with free boundary edges.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1, “Interfaces and all nested loops.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The strands \\(\\eta_n\\) converge in law in \\(d_{\\mathrm{curv}}\\) to chordal \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_{\\kappa(q)}\\) in \\((D;a,b)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), and set\n  \\[\n  p_q=\\frac{\\sqrt q}{1+\\sqrt q},\n  \\qquad\n  \\kappa(q)=\\frac{4\\pi}{\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)}\\in(4,6].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite graph \\(G=(V,E)\\) and a boundary partition \\(\\xi\\), the random-cluster law is\n  \\[\n  \\phi^\\xi_{G,q}(A)=\n  \\frac{1}{Z^\\xi_{G,q}}\n  p_q^{|A|}(1-p_q)^{|E|-|A|}q^{k_\\xi(A)},\n  \\qquad A\\subseteq E.\n  \\]\n  Here \\(k_\\xi(A)\\) counts components after identification within each block of \\(\\xi\\), including isolated vertices. An actual open path uses edges of \\(G\\), not boundary identifications.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a bounded Jordan domain with distinct boundary marks \\(a,b\\). At mesh \\(\\delta_n\\downarrow0\\), let \\(D_n\\) be the polygonal domain bounded by a simple nearest-neighbor polygon on \\(\\delta_n\\mathbb Z^2\\), with all lattice vertices and edges prescribed in Subsection 1.1; boundary edges are included.</p><p class=\"li\">Require counterclockwise boundary parametrizations \\(\\beta_n,\\beta:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb C\\), injective on \\([0,1)\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\beta_n(0)=a_n,\\quad \\beta_n(1/2)=b_n,\\qquad\n  \\beta(0)=a,\\quad\\beta(1/2)=b,\n  \\qquad\n  \\sup_t|\\beta_n(t)-\\beta(t)|\\longrightarrow0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The counterclockwise arc \\(F_n:a_n\\to b_n\\) is free. All vertices of the other arc \\(W_n:b_n\\to a_n\\), including its endpoints, are wired together. Its boundary edges are removed from sampling and declared open for interface drawing; this leaves the law of all remaining edges unchanged.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\eta_n\\) be the medial Dobrushin strand oriented from \\(a_n\\) to \\(b_n\\), with occurrence order at double visits retained. For oriented curves,\n  \\[\n  d_{\\mathrm{curv}}(\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2)\n  =\\inf_{\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2}\n  \\sup_{t\\in[0,1]}\n  |\\gamma_1(\\varphi_1(t))-\\gamma_2(\\varphi_2(t))|,\n  \\]\n  where the infimum is over increasing homeomorphisms.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi_q^0\\) be the free infinite-volume critical square-lattice law. Let \\(\\Gamma_\\delta\\) be its complete medial loop collection after scaling by \\(\\delta\\), including every nesting level.</p><p class=\"li\">Individual loops are unrooted, unoriented continuous maps of the circle into \\(\\widehat{\\mathbb C}\\), modulo zero uniform spherical distance under circular reparametrization and reversal. Distinct collection elements are retained even if their images coincide.</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(\\varepsilon\\)-matching is a partial bijection covering every loop of spherical diameter greater than \\(\\varepsilon\\) in both collections, matching loops within distance \\(\\varepsilon\\), and leaving only loops of diameter at most \\(\\varepsilon\\) unmatched.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The strands \\(\\eta_n\\) converge in law in \\(d_{\\mathrm{curv}}\\) to chordal \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_{\\kappa(q)}\\) in \\((D;a,b)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The collections \\(\\Gamma_\\delta\\) converge in law, in the spherical matching topology, to nested whole-plane \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_{\\kappa(q)}\\). The limiting law is Möbius invariant.</p><p>Both conclusions hold along every mesh sequence. No uniformity in \\(q\\) is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 14 proves part (i), with exactly the marked Jordan approximations and ordered medial drawing prescribed in the theorem.</p><p>For part (ii), Propositions 15–17 identify the joint target tree under actual-wall peeling. Proposition 18 identifies its original loop elements, winding labels, complete traces, traversal order, and multiplicities away from a distant square wall. Proposition 19 transfers this identification to the specified free infinite-volume law and upgrades local convergence to spherical one-to-one matching, including every nesting generation and Möbius invariance. All identifications hold for every subsequential limit, hence along every mesh sequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2, “Cardy’s formula for square-lattice bond percolation.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\mathbb P(C_n) = \\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_0^x[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,dt} {\\displaystyle\\int_0^1[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,dt}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a bounded Jordan domain with four distinct counterclockwise boundary marks \\(a,b,c,d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((D_n;a_n,b_n,c_n,d_n)\\) be nearest-neighbor square-lattice polygon approximations with uniformly convergent counterclockwise boundary parametrizations placing the four marks at \\(0,1/4,1/2,3/4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sample every edge of the polygon graph independently with probability \\(1/2\\), including every boundary edge, with no identifications or external connections.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C_n\\) be the event of an actual open path joining the closed counterclockwise arcs \\([a_n,b_n]\\) and \\([c_n,d_n]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\psi:D\\to\\mathbb H\\) send \\(a,c,d\\) to \\(0,1,\\infty\\), and put \\(x=\\psi(b)\\in(0,1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\mathbb P(C_n)\n=\n\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_0^x[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,dt}\n{\\displaystyle\\int_0^1[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,dt}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 10 proves holomorphicity of the four-change disk observable, so Proposition 6’s exact boundary-tree calculation and Schwarz–Christoffel integral apply. Proposition 11 transfers the actual crossing probability from balanced rational tile polygons to arbitrary uniformly approximated marked Jordan quadrilaterals.</p><p>At \\(q=1\\), the boundary partition does not affect the independently sampled edges, \\(d=1\\), and \\(\\rho=1/3\\). Thus Proposition 11’s specialization, originally Corollary 10.2, gives precisely the stated Cardy integral for the fully free-boundary event \\(C_n\\). The proof compares actual edge paths and does not infer crossing-event continuity from a curve limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 1.3, “Square-bond arm and thermodynamic exponents.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For each fixed \\(k\\ge1\\) and every sufficiently large fixed admissible \\(r\\), \\( \\pi_1(R)=R^{-5/48+o(1)}, \\qquad \\pi_{2k}(r,R)=R^{-(4k^2-1)/12+o(1)} \\quad(R\\to\\infty). \\) Moreover …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\mathbb P_p^\\square\\) for independent bond percolation on \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\), and \\(B_R=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|&lt;R\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\pi_1(R)=\\mathbb P_{1/2}^\\square(0\\leftrightarrow\\mathbb Z^2\\setminus B_R).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(k\\ge1\\), let \\(\\pi_{2k}(r,R)\\) be the probability of \\(2k\\) disjoint actual primal-open and dual-open paths, cyclically alternating in color, from \\(\\overline B_r\\) to \\(\\mathbb C\\setminus B_R\\). A dual-open edge crosses a closed primal edge.</p><p class=\"li\">For the open cluster \\(C(0)\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\theta(p)=\\mathbb P_p^\\square(|C(0)|=\\infty),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\chi(p)=\\mathbb E_p^\\square[|C(0)|;\\ |C(0)|&lt;\\infty],\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\xi(p)^2=\n  \\frac1{\\chi(p)}\n  \\sum_{x\\in\\mathbb Z^2}|x|^2\n  \\mathbb P_p^\\square(0\\leftrightarrow x,\\ |C(0)|&lt;\\infty),\n  \\qquad p\\ne\\tfrac12.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each fixed \\(k\\ge1\\) and every sufficiently large fixed admissible \\(r\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_1(R)=R^{-5/48+o(1)},\n\\qquad\n\\pi_{2k}(r,R)=R^{-(4k^2-1)/12+o(1)}\n\\quad(R\\to\\infty).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\theta(p)=(p-\\tfrac12)^{5/36+o(1)}\n\\quad(p\\downarrow\\tfrac12),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\chi(p)=|p-\\tfrac12|^{-43/18+o(1)},\n\\qquad\n\\xi(p)=|p-\\tfrac12|^{-4/3+o(1)}\n\\quad(p\\to\\tfrac12,\\ p\\ne\\tfrac12).\n\\]\nThe arm assertion concerns even cyclically alternating words only. These exponent assertions are made only for \\(q=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Theorem 1(ii) at \\(q=1\\) through Proposition 20’s buffered annular comparisons.</p><p>For one arm and each fixed even arm number \\(j\\), square-bond arm separation, localization, and quasi-multiplicativity give constants \\(0&lt;c_j\\le C_j&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\nc_j\\pi_j(r,R)\n\\le\\pi_j(r,\\varrho)\\pi_j(\\varrho,R)\n\\le C_j\\pi_j(r,R)\n\\]\nat separated radii. Fixed-shape comparisons transfer these estimates to circular annuli.</p><p>Proposition 20 says that at each fixed large ratio \\(M\\), the lower and upper mesh-limit envelopes have logarithmic cost\n\\[\n\\alpha_j\\log M+o(\\log M),\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_1=5/48,\\quad\n\\alpha_j=(j^2-1)/12\\quad(j\\text{ even}).\n\\]\nChoose \\(M\\) first, then sufficiently large inner radii so every ratio-\\(M\\) probability lies between these envelopes with arbitrarily small logarithmic error. Iterate quasi-multiplicativity over ratio-\\(M\\) annuli. Its cost per annulus is bounded by\n\\(\\max(|\\log c_j|,|\\log C_j|)\\). Let the number of annuli grow, divide by \\(\\log M\\), then let \\(M\\to\\infty\\). Monotonicity handles the final partial annulus and quasi-multiplicativity handles any fixed admissible inner radius. A fixed finite inner connection compares annular one-arm probability with connection from \\(0\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\pi_1(R)=R^{-5/48+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\pi_{2k}(r,R)=R^{-(4k^2-1)/12+o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(L(p)\\) be a finite-size crossing length with a sufficiently small fixed RSW-admissible threshold, using the subcritical color on either side of \\(1/2\\). The square-bond near-critical scaling relations give\n\\[\n|p-\\tfrac12|L(p)^2\\pi_4(r_2,L(p))\\asymp1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta(p)\\asymp\\pi_1(L(p))\\quad(p&gt;\\tfrac12),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\chi(p)\\asymp L(p)^2\\pi_1(L(p))^2,\\qquad\n\\xi(p)\\asymp L(p).\n\\]\nHere \\(r_2\\) is any fixed admissible four-arm inner radius, and Euclidean and \\(\\ell^\\infty\\) second-moment lengths are comparable.</p><p>Substituting the four-arm exponent \\(5/4\\) yields\n\\[\nL(p)=|p-\\tfrac12|^{-4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\beta=(4/3)(5/48)=5/36,\\qquad\n\\gamma=(4/3)(2-10/48)=43/18,\n\\]\nand \\(\\nu=4/3\\), proving all thermodynamic assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The capped loop weights are positive FK laws.", "statement": "<p>Positive loop laws, localization, and local weights; collecting the results of Section 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), and write\n  \\[\n  d=\\sqrt q=2\\cos\\lambda,\\qquad\n  \\rho=\\lambda/\\pi,\\qquad 0&lt;\\lambda\\le\\pi/3.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A square tile has alternating primal and dual corner sites and one of the two noncrossing pairings of its four side-midpoint ports. Pairing around dual corners declares the primal diagonal open; the other pairing declares the dual diagonal open.</p><p class=\"li\">Actual color paths use open diagonals and never boundary identifications.</p><p class=\"li\">A wall cuts connections at ports. Distinct sectors at a corner have distinct copies of that corner. A finite slit tip has one sector of angle \\(2\\pi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Local caps pair successive eligible ports on each designated wire-color interval. A genuine wire-color change leaves one unmatched port. A positive completion joins unmatched ports around primal intervals, equivalently wiring each primal interval separately.</p><p class=\"li\">All geometries below have fixed shapes, fixed buffers, and only boundedly many designated changes or through-wire blocks.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The capped loop weights are positive FK laws. Their plane strands are finite; remote marginals admit bounded and relative collar comparisons; raw passage counts have all fixed exponential moments; local nest tilts have bounded scale increments and controlled outer moments; macroscopic passage errors have arbitrarily small polynomial cost; and separated slit partition functions factor up to bounded ratios, as specified in Lemmas 1.1–1.8.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 identifies the positive laws. Lemma 1.2 supplies finite plane loops and finite-surgery laws. Lemmas 1.3–1.4 provide the bounded and relative comparisons. Lemmas 1.5–1.7 control passages, local tilts, and passage errors. Lemma 1.8 supplies the surface partition comparisons. These are precisely the positive estimates used in subsequent complex-observable arguments.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “The positive loop law.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The switch weight \\(d^\\ell\\) is proportional to the critical FK weight with partition \\(\\xi\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the tile and cap conventions of Proposition 1 on a genus-zero tile surface.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\ell\\) count completed medial loops, and let \\(\\xi\\) be the corresponding primal boundary partition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The switch weight \\(d^\\ell\\) is proportional to the critical FK weight with partition \\(\\xi\\). Conditional laws on common subdomains are FK laws with induced partitions. The assertion remains true after interchanging colors. On a torus without caps, the two weights differ by a factor bounded in terms of \\(q\\) alone.</p>", "proof": "<p>Draw noncrossing exterior primal trees realizing the designated identifications and thicken the resulting open primal graph. Its perimeter is the medial loop collection, apart from a fixed number of exterior components. Euler’s formula gives the configuration-dependent identity\n\\[\n\\ell=2k_\\xi(A)+|A|+\\text{constant}.\n\\]\nSince \\(d^2=q\\) and \\(p_q/(1-p_q)=d\\), the weights agree up to a configuration-independent constant. Domain Markov gives the conditional statement.</p><p>On a torus,\n\\[\n|V|-|A|=2k-\\ell-2g,\\qquad g\\le1,\n\\]\nso the additional genus factor is bounded.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, “Finiteness in the plane.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every medial strand is almost surely a finite loop.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work under the specified free infinite-volume critical square-lattice FK law.</p><p class=\"li\">A finite deterministic surgery changes finitely many tile connections at a fixed mesh.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every medial strand is almost surely a finite loop. Finite deterministic surgeries admit positive normalized loop laws whose conditional laws are those of Lemma 1.1.</p>", "proof": "<p>The buffered bulk box-crossing theorem for canonical doubly periodic isoradial FK weights applies for \\(1\\le q\\le4\\), including the square lattice. It supplies a uniformly positive conditional probability of an actual primal circuit around a fixed box in each of successively separated annuli. Missing the first \\(m\\) screens has probability at most \\((1-c)^m\\), regardless of the exterior partition. The same holds for dual circuits. These two colors of screens prevent a medial strand meeting the box from escaping. Take the countable union over boxes.</p><p>A finite surgery changes finitely many loops. Its density relative to the original law is \\(d^{\\Delta\\ell}\\), bounded above and below by constants depending on the finite surgery. Normalization defines its positive law. Alternatively exhaust by planar domains; the same circuits remove dependence on remote completion. Lemma 1.1 supplies the conditional law.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3, “Bounded densities across a collar.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The marginal densities beyond the fixed side are mutually bounded by a constant independent of scale and of the far graph.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Two positive laws have the same graph in the collar between radii \\(r\\) and \\(16r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The collar is a full annulus or a bounded number of sectors separated by straight coordinate walls; coincident slit banks are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Their deterministic wire portions agree in the collar. Only boundedly many wire blocks traverse it, and no other identifications jump it.</p><p class=\"li\">One radial side faces arbitrary unknown partition data; the graph and deterministic wires toward the other side are fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The marginal densities beyond the fixed side are mutually bounded by a constant independent of scale and of the far graph. Equivalently, expectations of every nonnegative remote function are comparable. The same holds for a fixed-shape rectangular bulk collar, with a shape-dependent constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>First use a finite graph and a finite remote edge set. Split each through wire transversally, preserving its connections on each side and one representative connection across the cut. The unknown data induce a partition at this section.</p><p>For ordered partitions, the likelihood ratio of the coarser law to the finer law is increasing: the rank of the additional identifications decreases when open components merge. If \\(R\\) is this ratio and \\(E_0\\) is the remote edge set, its marginal ratio is\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{fine}}[R\\mid\\omega|_{E_0}].\n\\]\nOpening remote edges increases both \\(R\\) and the conditional law of the remaining edges. Thus the maximum marginal ratio is attained at all-open remote edges and the minimum at all-closed remote edges. It suffices to compare these events for maximal and minimal partitions.</p><p>Actual primal crosscuts in sectors, or a primal annular circuit, screen the remote graph with uniformly positive probability. The up-to-wall square-FK crossing theorem is used here only for \\(q&lt;4\\). Discover screens from the unknown side without examining edges strictly beyond them: explore actual dual paths from the unknown-facing arc. If a primal blocking crossing exists, complementary crossing duality places such a path among the queried edges. The annular version is the alternative between a primal circuit and a radial dual connection.</p><p>Under the minimal law, condition on successful primal searches. Joining the screens and boundedly many through-wire representatives takes only boundedly many block identifications, each with unnormalized weight ratio in \\([q^{-1},1]\\). The resulting partition dominates every partition transmitted from the unknown side. This compares all-open probabilities.</p><p>Under the maximal law, use dual screens. No actual primal path crosses them; only boundedly many through-wire ties remain, and removing these has bounded cost. The resulting free cut is stochastically smaller than any transmitted partition, comparing all-closed probabilities. The extremal likelihood-ratio argument proves the result for every remote nonnegative function. Exhaustion gives infinite exteriors. Enclosing-circuit searches give rectangular collars.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, “Relative comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After \\(m\\) further collars, expectations of every fixed nonnegative remote function under two unknown-side laws have ratio \\( 1+O((1-c)^m), \\) or both vanish.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use successive collars satisfying Lemma 1.3, each with a sufficiently large fixed radius ratio.</p><p class=\"li\">Their geometry is either:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. a full annulus, or one sector whose through wires are continuous primal wires along its side rays; or</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. two sectors, one with primal wires on both side rays and the other with no through primal wires, with no additional fixed contacts between sectors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After \\(m\\) further collars, expectations of every fixed nonnegative remote function under two unknown-side laws have ratio\n\\[\n1+O((1-c)^m),\n\\]\nor both vanish. This holds in either radial direction and after positive modifications or tilts supported on the unknown side.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a radius ratio such as \\(256\\), leaving two subcollars. Lemma 1.3 on the first gives a common marginal part of uniformly positive mass on the second. Couple this part identically.</p><p>In the first geometry, additionally impose a primal screen meeting and joining every through wire. In the second, impose a primal screen on the wired side and a dual screen on the other. Conditional crossing bounds give uniformly positive probability. The transmitted partition then depends only on the shared configuration.</p><p>Largest and smallest remote expectations over section partitions form nested intervals. The shared part contracts their difference by \\(1-c\\); Lemma 1.3 bounds the initial ratio. Iteration gives the relative estimate. An unknown-side positive tilt is a mixture over the same partitions. Connections made or lost inside a gap remain part of that unknown partition, requiring both colors of screen in the second geometry.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma 2.5, “Exponential passage moments.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(B\\ge0\\), \\( \\mathbb E[e^{BX}\\mid\\text{exterior}]\\le C_B \\) uniformly in scale and exterior state.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A raw strand uses switches and stops at wall ports.</p><p class=\"li\">In a fixed buffered annulus or sector of Proposition 1, let \\(X\\) count disjoint raw passages through prescribed inner and outer rims, with cropping allowed in the buffers.</p><p class=\"li\">Condition on exterior data inducing the prescribed graph and an arbitrary boundary partition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(B\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[e^{BX}\\mid\\text{exterior}]\\le C_B\n\\]\nuniformly in scale and exterior state. The same holds for a fixed finite family of macroscopic passage counts in a fixed geometry.</p>", "proof": "<p>Disjoint radial strand subarcs split a cropped annulus into ordered strips. An actual primal crossing runs beside each passage. Distinct strips cannot communicate across the medial separators. Thus \\(X\\) passages force at least \\(X/2-O(1)\\) distinct actual crossing components.</p><p>Explore complete actual components from one rim, ignoring external identifications when deciding component membership. After removing a discovered component, its incident unexplored edges are closed. The remaining law is dominated by full wiring at the original rims and walls. Even under full wiring, an actual dual blocker has probability at least \\(c&gt;0\\). Therefore the number of crossing components has a geometric tail, giving one positive exponential rate.</p><p>For any fixed rate, divide the radial distance into \\(J\\) separated slices of thickness comparable to \\(r/J\\). Cover a middle rim of each slice by \\(O(J)\\) small buffered boxes, with wall-centered boxes near walls and smaller bulk boxes elsewhere. Their overlap graph has a bounded coloring independent of \\(J\\). Every original passage crosses a small annulus in every slice, so\n\\[\nJX\\le\\sum_i X_i,\n\\]\nwith \\(O(J^2)\\) counts satisfying the first exponential bound. Conditional iteration within colors and Hölder across colors give rate \\(c'J\\). Choose fixed \\(J\\) arbitrarily large. Before those boxes fit in the mesh, use a deterministic finite-size bound. A finite bulk, wall, and corner cover proves the macroscopic assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "Lemma 2.6, “Local tilt bounds.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The ratios \\(F_j/F_{j-1}\\) are uniformly bounded above and below.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N_j\\) count raw nests wholly inside dyadic radius \\(r_j\\), in a local sector extending past the largest radius with a buffer.</p><p class=\"li\">A nest is a surrounding bulk loop or a straddling arc around a gap, corner, change, or slit tip.</p><p class=\"li\">Passage counts \\(X_j\\) can be chosen so that\n  \\[\n  0\\le N_j-N_{j-1}\\le X_j,\n  \\]\n  and the buffer of \\(X_j\\) is disjoint from \\(B_{r_{j-k}}\\) for fixed \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For fixed \\(b&gt;0\\), put\n  \\[\n  F_j=\\mathbb E b^{N_j},\n  \\qquad\n  d\\nu_j=F_j^{-1}b^{N_j}\\,d\\mathbb P.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ratios \\(F_j/F_{j-1}\\) are uniformly bounded above and below. Under \\(\\nu_j\\), the counts \\(X_i\\), \\(i\\ge j\\), have every fixed exponential moment uniformly bounded. These assertions remain conditional on compatible exterior data and allow a fixed family of additional passage counts with buffers at or beyond the outer tilt level.</p>", "proof": "<p>The increment inequality follows because a newly included nest either travels a fixed fraction of its radius or goes around the point inside that band. At a slit tip, connecting the other bank without approaching the tip traverses the full-angle sector. Start at a fixed mesh radius.</p><p>For \\(b=1\\), use Lemma 1.5. For \\(0&lt;b&lt;1\\), set \\(D_i=\\log(F_{i-1}/F_i)\\ge0\\). Jensen and the passage estimate give, for sufficiently small fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nD_i\\le|\\log b|\\,\\mathbb E_{\\nu_{i-1}}X_i\n\\le C_\\varepsilon+\\varepsilon\\sum_{i-k&lt;l&lt;i}D_l.\n\\]\nDrop decreasing factors back to level \\(i-k\\); the remaining tilt is disjoint from the passage buffer. Choose \\(\\varepsilon(k-1)&lt;1\\) and induct. Dropping these boundedly many levels also proves all asserted moments.</p><p>For \\(b&gt;1\\), choose \\(B&gt;k\\log b\\) as large as desired and induct that\n\\(\\mathbb E_{\\nu_j}e^{BX_i}\\le C_*\\) for \\(i\\ge j\\). If \\(i\\ge j+k\\), supports are disjoint. Otherwise set \\(j_0=i-k&lt;j\\). Since the normalizing increment is at least one,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_j}e^{BX_i}\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_{j_0}}\n\\exp\\!\\left(BX_i+(\\log b)\\sum_{j_0&lt;l\\le j}X_l\\right).\n\\]\nGive each sum term Hölder weight \\(\\log b/B\\). The inductive contribution is at most \\(C_*^{k\\log b/B}\\); the remaining factor is a larger fixed-rate moment disjoint from the tilt. Thus the bound closes for sufficiently large \\(C_*\\). The moment bounds and the increment inequality give the ratio bounds.</p><p>Consequently local weights and their fixed powers have logarithms \\(O(\\log n)\\) up to polynomially large mesh radii. Fixed-factor cutoff changes have bounded cost, separated tilts can be treated conditionally, and increasing a power by \\(\\varepsilon\\) costs only \\(n^{O(\\varepsilon)}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.7", "orig": "Lemma 2.7, “Absorbing macroscopic errors.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(C_0\\ge1\\) and every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), \\( C_\\varepsilon^{-1}n^{-\\varepsilon} \\le \\frac{\\mathbb E[Y_nC_0^{\\pm X_n}]}{\\mathbb EY_n} \\le C_\\varepsilon n^\\varepsilon. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y_n,X_n\\ge0\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb EY_n\\ge C^{-1}n^{-A},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathbb EY_n^2\\le Cn^A.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X_n\\) has uniformly bounded exponential moments at every fixed rate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For fixed \\(C_0\\ge1\\) and every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nC_\\varepsilon^{-1}n^{-\\varepsilon}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\mathbb E[Y_nC_0^{\\pm X_n}]}{\\mathbb EY_n}\n\\le C_\\varepsilon n^\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) with \\(\\eta\\log C_0&lt;\\varepsilon\\), and split at \\(X_n=\\eta\\log n\\). On the first event the multiplicative factor lies between \\(n^{-\\varepsilon}\\) and \\(n^\\varepsilon\\). Cauchy–Schwarz, the polynomial second moment, and an arbitrarily large fixed exponential rate make both tail contributions smaller than any prescribed inverse power. Choose that power larger than \\(A+1\\), divide by the lower bound for \\(\\mathbb EY_n\\), and enlarge constants for bounded \\(n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.8", "orig": "Lemma 2.8, “Surface partition comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly in mesh, \\( Z_{\\mathrm{all}}\\asymp\\prod_s Z_s. \\) Reversing all cap-wire polarities on both banks of one straight isolated segment with boundedly many changes changes \\(Z_s\\) by a bounded factor.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Finitely many straight finite wall segments have fixed macroscopic shapes and positive mutual separation.</p><p class=\"li\">Complete each as a positive isolated boundary slit.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z_s\\) be its partition function relative to the unmodified plane law, and \\(Z_{\\mathrm{all}}\\) the relative partition function with all slits.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly in mesh,\n\\[\nZ_{\\mathrm{all}}\\asymp\\prod_s Z_s.\n\\]\nReversing all cap-wire polarities on both banks of one straight isolated segment with boundedly many changes changes \\(Z_s\\) by a bounded factor. A segment and its lattice-symmetry image, with cap data transported and colors exchanged when necessary, satisfy the same comparison.</p>", "proof": "<p>Enclose each segment in a separate patch with a vacant bulk collar. Let \\(X_s\\) count passages through that collar. Compare its loop-count change \\(\\Delta\\ell_s\\) with \\(\\Delta\\ell_s^\\circ\\), computed after closing patch ports by the same arbitrary pairing before and after surgery. Their difference is \\(O(X_s)\\): only ends whose strands traverse the collar can have externally determined partners, and changing those partners changes cycle count by at most their number.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nG_s=d^{\\Delta\\ell_s^\\circ}\\mathbf1_{\\{X_s\\le K\\}}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large fixed \\(K\\), simultaneous truncation has uniformly positive probability under the completed-slit law by Lemma 1.5. On truncation the true and local surgery weights are comparable. Hence\n\\[\nZ_{\\mathrm{all}}\\asymp\\mathbb E_0\\prod_sG_s,\n\\qquad\nZ_s\\asymp\\mathbb E_0G_s.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 1.3 successively to separated patches.</p><p>Translate one isolated segment by one tile step along its tangent. Plane symmetry, with duality if needed, preserves the law. The shifted caps agree with reversed caps except at boundedly many ports near tips and changes, so the cycle-count discrepancy is bounded. This compares partition functions, not pointwise densities for changing every pairing along a wall.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.2, “Three logarithmic comparisons.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Suppressing \\(n\\), \\( 2P+2D_*+4(C_0+C_1)\\le o(\\log n), \\) \\( C_0+C_1+L_0+L_1\\le o(\\log n), \\) and, for every finite admissible charge list with \\(\\sum_j a_j=1\\), \\( P+\\sum_j\\log\\frac{F_-(a_j)}{F_+(a_j)} \\le o(\\log n). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), \\(d=2\\cos\\lambda=\\sqrt q\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\Delta=\\frac{\\pi}{2\\lambda}-\\frac32,\\qquad\n  s=\\Delta+1,\\qquad t=-\\Delta,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(g)\n  =\\frac{\\sin((2-g)\\lambda)\\sin((1+g)\\lambda)}\n  {\\sin(2\\lambda)\\sin\\lambda}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Local experiments have cutoff radius comparable to \\(n\\) in mesh units, fixed buffered geometry, and a compatible positive exterior completion.</p><p class=\"li\">At a flat wire-color change, \\(P(n)\\) is the logarithm of the probability of an actual arm of the gap vertex’s color to the cutoff. If \\(N\\) counts raw straddling arcs, put\n  \\[\n  D_*(n)=\\log\\mathbb E d^N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At a corner without a wire flip, type \\(i=0\\) means gap color agrees with wire color, and \\(i=1\\) means disagreement. Define\n  \\[\n  C_i(n)=\\log\\mathbb E f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(1/2)^{N/2},\n  \\qquad\n  L_i(n)=\\log\\mathbb E f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(-1/2)^{N/2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At a bulk point, \\(N\\) counts surrounding raw nests. For fixed \\(a\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  0&lt;a&lt;1,\\qquad (1+a)\\lambda&lt;\\pi/2,\n  \\]\n  put\n  \\[\n  u_\\pm(a)=\\frac{\\cos((1\\pm a)\\lambda)}{\\cos\\lambda},\n  \\qquad F_\\pm(a;n)=\\mathbb E u_\\pm(a)^N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A one-sided \\(o(\\log n)\\) upper error means \\(\\varepsilon\\log n+O_\\varepsilon(1)\\) for every fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Suppressing \\(n\\),\n\\[\n2P+2D_*+4(C_0+C_1)\\le o(\\log n),\n\\]\n\\[\nC_0+C_1+L_0+L_1\\le o(\\log n),\n\\]\nand, for every finite admissible charge list with \\(\\sum_j a_j=1\\),\n\\[\nP+\\sum_j\\log\\frac{F_-(a_j)}{F_+(a_j)}\n\\le o(\\log n).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first inequality, use a rectangle of side lengths comparable to \\(n\\), with changes at the middle of its top and bottom and base drops \\(s,t\\). Since \\(s+t=1\\), the boundary factors are periodic. In the positive expansion every separating arc has \\(w(s)=w(t)=1\\); cap elimination bounds the spin partition by a constant times its positive cap partition.</p><p>In the negative expansion the changes have drops \\(-s,-t\\), and every convex corner contributes \\(1/2\\). An arc separating changes and containing the \\(s\\)-mark and \\(k\\) corners has numerator \\(\\sin(k\\lambda/2)\\), nonnegative and zero exactly when \\(k=0\\). On the event \\(E\\) that the \\(s\\)-gap has an actual arm to another rectangle side, all factors are positive. Relative to complementary caps the partition is at least\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_-\\!\\left[\n\\mathbf1_Ed^{N_t}\n\\prod_v f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(1/2)^{N_v/2}\nC^{-(1+X)}\n\\right].\n\\]\nSeparated-patch comparison and an arm extension give the lower bound\n\\(\\exp(P+D_*+\\sum_vC_{i(v)})\\), up to \\(n^\\varepsilon\\) from Lemma 1.7. Choose odd horizontal and even vertical tile lengths so all four corner types agree. Repeat with every wire reversed. The cap-partition ratios cancel, yielding the first inequality.</p><p>For the second inequality, use a cylinder with spatial period having two positive coordinate components of order \\(n\\), even sum, staircase boundaries with one convex and one concave corner each, and transverse height \\(Kn\\log n\\). Same-boundary arcs span less than one period, since otherwise they interleave a translate. Contractible loops weigh \\(d\\); essential loops have turn zero and weigh \\(2\\). In the empty middle strip put\n\\[\nM=(2/d)^{k_0}\\ge1.\n\\]\nPassage moments give \\(\\mathbb EM^p\\le n^{O_p(K)}\\). If \\(T\\) is the event of a raw arc joining the two boundaries, then \\(M=1\\) on \\(T\\), and actual noncontractible circuits in successive transverse bands give\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_\\pm(T)\\le Cn^{-cK}.\n\\]\nThe positive expansion is bounded by \\(Z_+^{\\mathrm{cap}}\\mathbb E_+[MC^{1+X}]\\). Off \\(T\\), the negative expansion is positive and bounded below by\n\\[\nM\\prod_v f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(g_v)^{N_v/2}C^{-(1+X)},\n\\qquad g_v\\in\\{1/2,-1/2\\}.\n\\]\nOn \\(T\\), its absolute contribution has the same local costs without \\(M\\). Choose fixed \\(K\\) large enough to suppress this signed error, then remove passage factors at \\(n^\\varepsilon\\) cost. Reversing both wire colors cancels the cap and middle-factor ratios. The two comparisons count twice each corner cost, proving the second inequality.</p><p>For the third inequality, use a straight cylinder of even period comparable to \\(n\\), with bottom changes \\(s,t\\), no top change, and separated bulk charges \\(a_j\\) in the bottom band. Draw charge-\\(-a_j\\) strings from the \\(s\\)-gap to the endpoints and use bottom base drops \\(s-1,t\\). The positive effective drops are \\(s,t\\). In the negative expansion, a boundary disk containing total endpoint charge \\(a\\) has numerators\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{enclosed flips}&amp;\\text{numerators}\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;\\sin((2-a)\\lambda),\\ \\sin((1+a)\\lambda)\\\\\n1&amp;\\sin(a\\lambda)\\ [s],\\ \\sin((2-a)\\lambda)\\ [t]\\\\\n2&amp;\\sin((3-a)\\lambda),\\ \\sin(a\\lambda).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThey are nonnegative for \\(0\\le a\\le1\\). Every zero cuts off \\(s\\), including the additional \\(q=1\\) zero. Local closed nests give precisely \\(u_\\pm(a_j)\\).</p><p>Let \\(E\\) connect the \\(s\\)-gap by its actual color to a noncontractible cycle near the bottom. Conditional on endpoint patches and the middle strip,\n\\(\\mathbb P_-(E\\mid\\cdot)\\gtrsim e^P\\). On \\(E\\) no disk cuts off \\(s\\), and no through arc occurs. With \\(U_\\pm=\\prod_j u_\\pm(a_j)^{N_j}\\), the expansion integrands satisfy\n\\[\n|Y_+|\\le MU_+C^{1+X},\\qquad\nY_-\\ge MU_-C^{-(1+X)}\\ \\text{on }E,\\qquad\nY_-\\ge0\\ \\text{off }T.\n\\]\nThe through contribution is polynomially weighted times \\(n^{-cK}\\), and is negligible for sufficiently large fixed \\(K\\). Separate local factors, remove passage errors, and repeat after global wire reversal. The cap and middle-factor ratios cancel, giving\n\\[\n2P+2\\sum_j\\log(F_-(a_j)/F_+(a_j))\\le o(\\log n).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Local conventions and strict arm decay.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fixed-factor cutoff changes, compatible exterior changes, tile symmetries, and simultaneous exchange of color names and wire designations change these logarithms by \\(O(1)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the local experiments and logarithms of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Fixed-factor cutoff changes, compatible exterior changes, tile symmetries, and simultaneous exchange of color names and wire designations change these logarithms by \\(O(1)\\). Local weights, their fixed powers, and the arm indicator have logarithmic costs \\(O(\\log n)\\). For some \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nP(n)\\le-c\\log n+O(1).\n\\]\nAlso \\(D_*,C_i\\ge0\\) and \\(L_i\\le0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The nest assertions follow from Proposition 1. The signs follow from\n\\(f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(1/2)&gt;1\\) and \\(0&lt;f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(-1/2)&lt;1\\), obtained by comparing the sine products at their common mean.</p><p>For arms, boundary regions separated by raw boundary-to-boundary arcs are the actual color components touching boundary gaps. Closed interior loops do not split these boundary classes. In the separator tree, a wall visit absent from the outer-rim excursion is entered and left across the same separating edge. Thus failure of the gap region to reach the rim produces a straddling arc; conversely a straddling arc blocks its actual color paths.</p><p>Constant-factor arm extensions have uniformly positive cost: add a same-color wall-to-wall screen in an intermediate annulus and a crossing from it to the larger radius, using FKG and wall crossing estimates. Iteration gives a polynomial lower bound. Opposite-color screens in separated annuli obstruct the arm with uniformly positive conditional probability, proving strict polynomial decay. No identification is used as an arm edge.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, “Local cap elimination.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For arcs whose child boundary interval contains at most one wire flip, the positive cap factors are \\(d\\) for a zero-flip high start and \\(1\\) for a zero-flip low start or a one-flip arc, apart from bounded loop-count …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Orient switch strands with turn weight \\(e^{\\pm i\\rho W/2}\\), \\(\\rho=\\lambda/\\pi\\), and common tile coefficient \\(\\sin(\\lambda/2)/\\sin\\lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a boundary port \\(j\\), let \\(\\theta_j\\) be the lifted inward normal and\n  \\[\n  b_j(c)=(2\\sin\\lambda)^{-1/2}\n  e^{ic(\\rho\\theta_j/2+\\lambda h_j)},\\qquad c=\\pm1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(h_j=H_j+\\Delta\\mathbf1_{\\{j\\text{ high}\\}}\\), where “high” means the preceding gap vertex has the interval’s designated wire color.</p><p class=\"li\">For a disk arc with endpoint height drop \\(m\\),\n  \\[\n  w(m)=\\frac{\\cos((m-1/2)\\lambda)}{\\sin\\lambda}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For arcs whose child boundary interval contains at most one wire flip, the positive cap factors are \\(d\\) for a zero-flip high start and \\(1\\) for a zero-flip low start or a one-flip arc, apart from bounded loop-count error and a factor \\(d^{O(X)}\\) from macroscopic arcs. At a corner of turn \\(g\\pi\\) without a flip, a nest of \\(N\\) straddles has negative-expansion weight relative to caps comparable to \\(f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(g)^{N/2}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The two winding expansions give the same real arrow tensor. Adjacent incoming arrows permit one pairing with canceling turns; opposite incoming arrows give two contributions summing to \\(2\\cos(\\lambda/2)\\). Thus every finite contraction, with boundary spin factors and inserted matrices, agrees in the two expansions.</p><p>An arc from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) has turn \\(\\theta_j-\\theta_i-\\pi\\). Summing orientations gives \\(w(h_i-h_j)\\). In the negative expansion use \\(h'_j=-h_j-\\theta_j/\\pi\\). Endpoint high statuses are opposite for an even number of flips and equal for an odd number. The identities\n\\[\nw(g+\\Delta)=\\frac{\\sin((2-g)\\lambda)}{\\sin\\lambda},\n\\qquad\nw(g-\\Delta)=\\frac{\\sin((1+g)\\lambda)}{\\sin\\lambda}\n\\]\ngive staggered weights \\(d,1\\) at zero drop.</p><p>Remove an inside-closed family of disk arcs from the inside outward. A high-low arc creates a loop; a low-high arc transports the cap pattern. Across a flip an unmatched end is transported without a loop. Only boundedly many exceptional erasures of whole intervals are possible. Unremoved arcs are charged to a macroscopic passage count \\(X\\). Along a corner nest the starting parities alternate; the product of two relative weights is \\(f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(g)\\), with at most one bounded unmatched factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Corollary 3.4, “Balanced corner parities.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are whole-tile approximants differing by \\(O(1)\\) mesh units for which at least \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) corners of each relative wire type occur in both convex and concave sets, also after global wire reversal.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed-\\(q\\) local costs of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">A fixed simple rectilinear polygon has \\(m\\) concave and \\(m+4\\) convex true corners, and wire changes in side interiors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are whole-tile approximants differing by \\(O(1)\\) mesh units for which at least \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) corners of each relative wire type occur in both convex and concave sets, also after global wire reversal. For these approximants,\n\\[\n3P+D_*+\\sum_{v\\text{ convex}}C_{i(v)}\n+\\sum_{v\\text{ concave}}L_{i(v)}\n\\le \\tfrac12P-\\tfrac32D_*+o(\\log n).\n\\]\nEach finite admissible family of negative endpoint charges of total magnitude one has total wrong-to-correct logarithmic cost at most\n\\[\n-P+o(\\log n).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Prescribe the required corner types for one wire designation. At least four convex corners remain spare; use one to adjust the sum of absolute corner parities. If \\(b_i\\) is side-coordinate parity and \\(p_i\\) the corner parity between sides \\(i-1,i\\), then\n\\[\np_i=b_{i-1}+b_i\\pmod2.\n\\]\nThe cyclic system is solvable exactly when \\(\\sum_i p_i=0\\pmod2\\). Choose side coordinates of these parities within bounded mesh distance. Small independent shifts preserve the fixed polygon’s simplicity. Reversing wires exchanges relative types.</p><p>Pair balanced convex and concave terms and apply Proposition 2. Four convex terms remain if \\(m\\) is even; five convex and one nonpositive concave term remain if \\(m\\) is odd. Since \\(C_i\\ge0\\), its first inequality gives\n\\[\nP+D_*\\le o(\\log n),\\qquad\nC_i\\le-\\tfrac12(P+D_*)+o(\\log n).\n\\]\nThese pay for five convex terms even when only four remain, yielding the stated budget. The endpoint-charge assertion is Proposition 2’s third inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Theorem 4.1, “Angular transfer estimate.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Uniformly for \\(x,y\\in[-v,v]\\), \\( \\|B_{\\mathrm{out}}N(x)B_{\\mathrm{in}}\\|\\le Cn^{-1+\\rho}, \\) \\( \\|B_{\\mathrm{out}}\\{N(x)-e^{i\\tau(x-y)}N(y)\\}B_{\\mathrm{in}}\\| \\le Cn^{-1-\\rho}. \\) Boundedly many additional even pairs …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), \\(d=2\\cos\\lambda\\), \\(\\rho=\\lambda/\\pi\\), \\(v=\\pi/2\\), and \\(Q=-e^{i\\lambda}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the spin basis \\(+,-\\), set\n  \\[\n  J=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n  \\quad L_-=|-\\rangle\\langle+|,\n  \\quad L_+=|+\\rangle\\langle-|,\n  \\quad K(\\zeta)=\\operatorname{diag}(\\zeta,\\zeta^{-1}),\\quad|\\zeta|=1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In the ordered pair basis \\(++,+-,-+,--\\), set\n  \\[\n  R(u)=\n  \\begin{pmatrix}\n  a(u)&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\n  0&amp;b(u)&amp;1&amp;0\\\\\n  0&amp;1&amp;b(u)&amp;0\\\\\n  0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;a(u)\n  \\end{pmatrix},\n  \\quad\n  a(u)=\\frac{\\sin(\\lambda-u)}{\\sin\\lambda},\n  \\quad b(u)=\\frac{\\sin u}{\\sin\\lambda}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A row on an even spatial circle has transfer\n  \\[\n  T_N^D(\\alpha)=\\operatorname{Tr}_c\n  D_cR_{c0}(\\rho(\\omega_0-\\alpha))\\cdots\n  R_{c,N-1}(\\rho(\\omega_{N-1}-\\alpha)).\n  \\]\n  Divide by the untwisted zero-spin Perron eigenvalue \\(\\Lambda_N(\\alpha)\\). In the homogeneous transverse array \\(\\omega_j=v\\), denote the limiting normalized action by \\(A^D(z)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  P_\\zeta(z)=A^{K(\\zeta)}(z),\\quad\n  M(z)=e^{i\\tau\\rho z}A^D(z),\\quad\n  N(z)=M(z)P_\\zeta(z)+P_\\xi(z)M(z),\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  \\zeta/\\xi=Q^\\epsilon,\\quad \\epsilon=\\pm1,\\qquad\n  (D,\\tau)=(L_-,\\epsilon)\\ \\text{or }(L_+,-\\epsilon).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a compact interval \\(I\\subset(-v,v)\\). Let \\(B_{\\mathrm{in}}\\) be a product of \\(P_\\zeta\\)-rows and \\(B_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) a product of \\(P_\\xi\\)-rows, all at real angles in \\([-v,v]\\), each containing at least \\(n\\) angles in \\(I\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for \\(x,y\\in[-v,v]\\),\n\\[\n\\|B_{\\mathrm{out}}N(x)B_{\\mathrm{in}}\\|\\le Cn^{-1+\\rho},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|B_{\\mathrm{out}}\\{N(x)-e^{i\\tau(x-y)}N(y)\\}B_{\\mathrm{in}}\\|\n\\le Cn^{-1-\\rho}.\n\\]\nBoundedly many additional even pairs of before-twist rows may be inserted immediately to the right and after-twist rows immediately to the left. The two compared terms may use different such pairs.</p><p>The same estimates hold for diagonal slicing of the physical square array, whose time angles are \\(\\pm\\pi/4\\) and space angles alternate between \\(3\\pi/4,\\pi/4\\). A scalar contraction is bounded by the displayed rate times the two exterior reflection norms, including the squares centered on the cut in the reflection kernel.</p>", "proof": "<p>Map the strip to the right half-plane by \\(w=e^{iz}\\). Lemma 4.3 makes \\(f\\) inner, with analytic continuation through all finite nonzero imaginary-boundary points. Its zeros satisfy a uniform cone condition\n\\[\n\\Re w_j\\ge c|w_j|.\n\\]\nInner factorization gives\n\\[\nf(w)=\\eta e^{-aw-b/w}\\prod_j\\beta_j(w),\n\\quad a,b\\ge0,\\quad|\\eta|=1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\beta_j(w)\\) is the canonical phase multiple of\n\\((w-w_j)/(w+\\overline w_j)\\). No singular factors occur except atoms at \\(0,\\infty\\).</p><p>If \\(-\\log|f(1)|\\le s\\) for sufficiently small \\(s\\), evaluation at \\(1\\) and the cone condition give\n\\[\na+b+\\sum_j\\min(|w_j|,|w_j|^{-1})\\le Cs.\n\\]\nThus on\n\\[\nc_1^{-1}s&lt;|w|&lt;c_1/s,\\qquad |\\arg w|\\le\\pi/2,\n\\]\nboth \\(f\\) and \\(f^{-1}\\) are uniformly bounded, and on the unit semicircle\n\\(f(w)=f(1)+O(s)\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nH_\\zeta=-\\log|P_\\zeta(0)|,\\qquad\nE_\\zeta(s)=\\mathbf1_{[0,s]}(H_\\zeta),\n\\]\nand split by the sign \\(\\sigma=\\pm1\\) of the self-adjoint \\(P_\\zeta(0)\\). Then\n\\[\nP_\\zeta(w)E_\\zeta^\\sigma(s)\n=\\sigma E_\\zeta^\\sigma(s)+O(s).\n\\]</p><p>Endpoint swaps give the exact reversal\n\\[\nM(v)+P_\\xi(v)M(-v)P_\\zeta(v)=0,\n\\]\nand its counterpart at \\(-v\\). For \\(D=L_-\\),\n\\(K(\\xi)DK(\\zeta)=(\\zeta/\\xi)D\\), and\n\\(e^{-i\\tau\\rho v}(\\zeta/\\xi)=-e^{i\\tau\\rho v}\\); the raising case is analogous. Imaginary covariance extends the identities along the boundary lines.</p><p>Compress \\(M\\) to the two low bands and continue across the imaginary rays by\n\\[\nM_c(-w)=-P_\\xi(w)^{-1}M_c(w)P_\\zeta(w)^{-1}.\n\\]\nBoundary reversal gives holomorphic gluing on the full annulus and\n\\[\n\\|M_c(w)\\|\\le C|w|^{\\tau\\rho}.\n\\]\nFor its Laurent coefficients,\n\\[\n\\|M_{c,j}\\|\\le C(C's)^{|j-\\tau\\rho|}.\n\\]\nHence on \\(|w|=1\\),\n\\[\n\\|M_c(w)\\|=O(s^\\rho),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{M_c(w)-M_c(-w)}2\n=w^\\tau M_{c,\\tau}+O(s^{1+\\rho}),\n\\qquad\n\\|M_{c,\\tau}\\|=O(s^{1-\\rho}).\n\\]\nOn equal-sign blocks reversal makes the even part \\(O(s^{1+\\rho})\\); on opposite-sign blocks the two leading signs in \\(MP_\\zeta+P_\\xi M\\) cancel. Consequently\n\\[\n\\|E_\\xi(s)N(x)E_\\zeta(s)\\|\\le Cs^{1-\\rho},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|E_\\xi(s)\\{N(x)-e^{i\\tau(x-y)}N(y)\\}E_\\zeta(s)\\|\n\\le Cs^{1+\\rho}.\n\\]\nEven bounded row insertions are \\(I+O(s)\\), causing only \\(O(s^{1+\\rho})\\) error.</p><p>Schwarz–Pick gives, uniformly for \\(x\\in I\\),\n\\[\n1-|f(x)|\\ge c_I(1-|f(0)|).\n\\]\nDecompose both spectral energies into \\([0,n^{-1}]\\), dyadic bands, and a fixed high-energy remainder. The \\(n\\) strict rows damp a band at energy \\(u\\) by \\(e^{-cnu}\\). Summing\n\\[\nC\\max(u,u',n^{-1})^\\beta e^{-cn(u+u')},\n\\qquad \\beta=1-\\rho,\\ 1+\\rho,\n\\]\ngives \\(Cn^{-\\beta}\\); high bands are exponentially small.</p><p>For physical diagonal cuts, put \\(\\gamma_0=\\pi/4\\), \\(X=e^{-i\\gamma_0}\\), \\(Y=e^{i\\gamma_0}\\), \\(R_*=R(\\lambda/2)\\), and\n\\[\nG=\\prod_j(\\operatorname{swap}R_*)_{2j,2j+1}.\n\\]\nEach factor is positive definite, with middle eigenvalues \\(1\\pm b(\\lambda/2)\\). Collapsed tiles give\n\\[\nT_N^D(\\gamma_0)=D_{N-1}SG,\\qquad\nT_N^D(-\\gamma_0)=(JD^tJ)_0S^{-1}G.\n\\]\nThus \\(G\\) is exactly the reflection kernel, including squares centered on the cut.</p><p>The degenerate indexed arrays biject with physical square tori; collapsed duplicate sides create no free cycles and preserve flux projection. Their transverse period products are primitive: prescribed zero-sum boundary spins are height increments on two geodesic paths; for sufficiently large separation their combined heights are \\(1\\)-Lipschitz and extend by integer distance cones. Yang–Baxter commutation identifies their Perron data with strict transverse quantization. Hence physical seam-word Gram kernels are the homogeneous kernels.</p><p>The seam-generated subspace is reducing for untwisted rows; the no-zero assertion gives dense ranges. The identity\n\\(S^2P_1(-\\gamma_0)=P_1(\\gamma_0)\\) makes shifts by two preserve it. The row formulas then give every local site insertion, so all finite physical exterior states belong to this subspace. Gluing reflected states through \\(G\\) identifies their norms exactly. Restricting the homogeneous estimates proves the physical assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, “Plane kernels.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "All Gram limits \\(\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\langle h_N,g_N\\rangle\\) exist.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use homogeneous normalized strict-angle rows from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">A word \\(h=((D_1,\\alpha_1),\\ldots,(D_m,\\alpha_m))\\) defines\n  \\[\n  h_N=A_N^{D_m}(\\alpha_m)\\cdots A_N^{D_1}(\\alpha_1)\\Omega_N,\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\Omega_N\\) is the positive unit zero-spin Perron vector.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All Gram limits \\(\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\langle h_N,g_N\\rangle\\) exist. Quotienting null vectors and completing gives a separable Hilbert space \\(\\mathcal H\\), unit vacuum \\(\\Omega\\), and bounded left row actions\n\\[\n\\|A^D(\\alpha)\\|\\le2\\|D\\|.\n\\]\nBare vacuum coefficients are continuous in strict angles and invariant under common translation of all time angles while those angles remain in \\((-v,v)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The finite row identities are Yang–Baxter, inversion, and crossing:\n\\[\nR_{12}(x-y)R_{13}(x)R_{23}(y)\n=R_{23}(y)R_{13}(x)R_{12}(x-y),\n\\]\n\\[\nR(u)R(-u)=(1-b(u)^2)I,\\qquad\nJ_cR(u)^{t_c}J_c=R(\\lambda-u).\n\\]\nThe nontrivial one-minus block reduces to the three sine identities displayed in Section 4.2; spin reversal gives the other block. Sliding the intertwiner makes rows with common diagonal twist commute. For arbitrary insertions, exchange conjugates their tensor product by the two-spin intertwiner and expands it into finitely many matrix units. Strict untwisted rows are primitive on each total-spin sector: positive diagonal entries allow holding spins, and auxiliary swaps connect the sector. Thus commuting rows have common Perron data. Transposition and crossing give\n\\[\nA^D(\\alpha)^*=A^{J\\overline D J}(-\\alpha),\n\\]\nand equal left and right homogeneous vacua.</p><p>To identify the plane state, use periodic strict time and space angle lists in ordered, transversely separated direction ranges. The resulting rhombic grids are embedded bounded-angle doubly periodic isoradial grids: local cyclic order gives local injectivity, and uniformly transverse averages give properness.</p><p>Expand \\(R\\) into oriented loop pairings. Contractible loops weigh \\(d\\). Essential simple torus loops have turn zero, are mutually parallel, and occur in even number \\(k\\). Zero-spin projection on a quantum section leaves orientation count\n\\[\n\\chi=2^k\n\\quad\\text{if the essential class has zero intersection with the section},\n\\qquad\n\\chi=\\binom{k}{k/2}\\quad\\text{otherwise}.\n\\]\nSince \\(d&lt;2\\), \\(c(d)d^k\\le\\chi\\le2^k\\). Replacing \\(\\chi\\) by \\(d^k\\), up to the bounded genus factor, gives canonical isoradial FK odds\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{odds}}{\\sqrt q}\n=\\frac{\\sin(\\rho\\gamma)}{\\sin(\\rho(\\pi-\\gamma))}.\n\\]\nThe isoradial crossing and uniqueness theorem applies.</p><p>Let \\(r\\le R\\) be torus period sizes. Cover by \\(O(R/r)\\) buffered boxes at scale \\(r\\), including translated target annuli. If \\(Y\\) is the total passage count, then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E e^{cY}\\le e^{C_cR/r},\\qquad k\\le Y.\n\\]\nThe essential-orientation tilt consequently satisfies\n\\(\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{tilt}}Y\\le CR/r\\). Translation invariance makes the target-annulus count tight. With at most \\(K\\) exterior excursions, changing an inner filling alters only \\(O(K)\\) essential loops; changing their homology or projection type can occur only when no unaffected essential loop remains. Central-binomial ratios with indices differing by \\(O(K)\\) are bounded. Thus the conditional inner law is comparable to FK, with a \\(K,q\\)-dependent factor. Apply this comparison once to the entire escape event; circuit bounds make escape vanish as \\(r\\to\\infty\\), then let \\(K\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>With high probability all objects incident to a fixed query box lie in one injective disk. Every filling then changes only planar components and contractible loops, so the orientation and genus tilts remain fixed and the conditional law is ordinary FK. Free/wired uniqueness identifies the plane limit. Finitely many marked matrices have absolute orientation ratio at most \\(\\prod_i2\\|D_i\\|\\); marked strands that remain local do not encounter flux projection. Hence marked local limits also exist.</p><p>Taking longitudinal length first gives finite-circle Perron coefficients and then their plane limits. Transverse quantization turns seam marks into one tensor-product insertion:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\langle\\ell_L,D_Lr_L\\rangle}{\\langle\\ell_L,r_L\\rangle}.\n\\]\nCommon transverse Perron vectors make this independent of strict transverse angles. Rotating both direction families and restoring the transverse angle proves common time-angle translation invariance. Finite-box FK sandwiches, uniqueness, and circuit localization give continuity.</p><p>Finite-volume Gram positivity passes to the limit. For the formal positive operator \\(T=(A^D)^*A^D\\), marked expansion bounds\n\\(\\|T^jh\\|^2\\le C_h(2\\|D\\|)^{4j}\\). Iterated Cauchy–Schwarz followed by \\(j\\to\\infty\\) yields \\(\\|A^Dh\\|\\le2\\|D\\|\\|h\\|\\), preserving null vectors. Rational-angle, rational-matrix words give separability. Local site insertions and shifts can initially be included by the same argument; the endpoint formulas are\n\\[\nA^D(v)=SD_0,\\qquad\nA^D(-v)=(JD^tJ)_0S^{-1}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, “Angle generator.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a self-adjoint \\(B\\) such that \\(V_th=e^{tB}h\\) locally for every word.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V_th\\) translate every angle of a strict word \\(h\\) by \\(t\\), locally while all angles remain strict.</p><p class=\"li\">Work in the plane Hilbert space of Lemma 4.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a self-adjoint \\(B\\) such that \\(V_th=e^{tB}h\\) locally for every word. Ordinary strict-angle words form a graph core. Each row family extends to a bounded holomorphic operator function on \\(|\\Re z|&lt;v\\), with\n\\[\nA^D(x+ib)=U_bA^D(x)U_{-b},\n\\qquad U_b=e^{ibB}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Reflection negates angles in the Gram formula, and common translation invariance gives\n\\[\n\\langle V_th,V_sg\\rangle=\\langle h,V_{s+t}g\\rangle.\n\\]\nThus \\(f_h(t)=\\langle h,V_th\\rangle\\) is continuous and exponentially convex on a small interval. The interval Bernstein–Widder theorem represents it as a positive Laplace transform, hence makes it analytic. Finite differences give all Hilbert derivatives and\n\\[\n\\|h^{(k)}\\|^2=f_h^{(2k)}(0)\n\\le C_h(2k)!r_h^{-2k}.\n\\]\nThe Taylor series recovers the vector curve.</p><p>On the span of derivative vectors define \\(B_0h^{(k)}=h^{(k+1)}\\). Differentiated cross identities make this well defined and symmetric. Every such vector is analytic for \\(B_0\\). Nelson’s analytic-vector theorem gives essential self-adjointness. Finite differences of ordinary words approximate derivatives in graph norm, proving the core assertion and the local exponential formula.</p><p>The word identity\n\\[\nA^D(x+t)V_th=V_t(A^D(x)h)\n\\]\ngives weakly on the core\n\\[\n\\frac d{dx}\\langle g,A^D(x)h\\rangle\n=\\langle Bg,A^D(x)h\\rangle-\\langle g,A^D(x)Bh\\rangle.\n\\]\nExtend by graph-norm approximation. The proposed imaginary covariance satisfies Cauchy–Riemann equations between domain vectors. The uniform bound \\(2\\|D\\|\\) extends weak holomorphicity to all vectors and hence gives operator holomorphicity.</p><p>Middle-third words are dense by analyticity in each word argument. For a fixed twist, commuting rows and their adjoints lie in an abelian von Neumann algebra:\n\\[\n[P_\\zeta(z),P_\\zeta(w)]=0,\\qquad\nP_\\zeta(z)^*=P_\\zeta(-\\overline z).\n\\]\nA direct-integral representation gives scalar analytic spectral functions \\(f(z)\\), bounded by \\(2\\), with \\(f(-\\overline z)=\\overline{f(z)}\\). Countably many arguments and Taylor discs give a common exceptional null set. Imaginary covariance preserves the spectral measure class.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4, “Scalar continuation in a fixed band.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Almost every \\(f\\) extends analytically and without zeros to bands of one fixed positive width about \\(\\Re z=\\pm v\\), has modulus one on these lines, and satisfies \\(|f(z)|\\le1\\) in the strip.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(f\\) be a scalar spectral function of a fixed twisted row family from Lemma 4.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Almost every \\(f\\) extends analytically and without zeros to bands of one fixed positive width about \\(\\Re z=\\pm v\\), has modulus one on these lines, and satisfies \\(|f(z)|\\le1\\) in the strip. At every specified strip point, spectral functions vanishing there form a null set.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write temporarily \\(T_D(u)=T_N^D(v-u/\\rho)\\). In a fixed small disc about \\(u=0\\), both\n\\[\nT_D(u)T_I(u)^{-1},\n\\qquad\nT_I(u)^{-1}T_D(u)\n\\]\nare holomorphic and bounded by \\(C\\|D\\|\\), uniformly for large \\(N\\).</p><p>Multiply by the reversed-auxiliary transfer at \\(-u\\) and fold its auxiliary index. The local product is\n\\[\nG_j=R_{cj}(u)R_{c'j}(-u)^{t_{c'}}.\n\\]\nThe auxiliary line spanned by \\(|++\\rangle+|--\\rangle\\) is invariant, with action \\(r(u)I\\), \\(r(u)=1-b(u)^2\\). At zero the range is entirely on this line. In the common triangular decomposition, \\(G_j/r(u)\\) has identity on the line, complementary diagonal block \\(O(|u|)\\), and bounded off-diagonal block. Products remain bounded by a geometric series. Their unmarked partial trace differs from identity by \\(O((C|u|)^N)\\) and is uniformly invertible. Reverse order for the other quotient; crossing handles \\(u=\\lambda\\).</p><p>The vacuum is a common analytic eigenvector with nonzero eigenvalue in these discs, giving uniformly bounded analytic vacuum row vectors near \\(z=\\pm v\\). Normal families and interior uniqueness identify their plane continuations. Cauchy estimates compare the continued vectors with the endpoint swap vectors, identifying them strongly in the enlarged Gram space.</p><p>Commute an endpoint row through a middle-third word. Intertwiner inverses are analytic on one common endpoint disc, independent of word length; the resulting finite sum continues the vector on that disc. Endpoint rows preserve the original Hilbert space and are strong limits there.</p><p>Choose a countable dense middle-third core in a finite equivalent spectral measure. Cauchy estimates make each vector’s Taylor coefficients pointwise summable on smaller discs. On their interior these vectors are \\(f(z)h_j\\); analytic proportionality therefore extends a common scalar \\(f\\) to the entire disc. Endpoint swaps make \\(P_\\zeta(v)\\) and \\(P_\\zeta(-v)\\) unitary inverses. Strong convergence and imaginary covariance give modulus one along both boundary diameters. Schwarz reflection gives zero-free continuation. Rational imaginary translates cover the boundary lines by discs of the same radius.</p><p>The bounded strip maximum principle gives \\(|f|\\le1\\). Each nonzero analytic function has isolated zeros; Fubini along a vertical orbit and preservation of spectral measure class show that the zero set at any prescribed point is null.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 5.3, “Extraction in a clear slab.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The contraction is the corresponding low or high expression of Proposition 4 between exterior states independent of the stencil term, up to bounded compensating even row pairs and a common bounded scalar.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(Q=-e^{i\\lambda}\\), \\(\\rho=\\lambda/\\pi\\), and the matrices of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">For a simple directed tile-edge path \\(C\\), with continuously lifted tangent angle \\(\\alpha\\), its primitive \\(\\mathcal J(C)\\) sums over a marked step: insert \\(e^{i\\rho\\alpha}L_-\\) there, \\(K(Q)\\) before it, and identity after it.</p><p class=\"li\">A low stencil is a primitive increment on a straight two-step extension.</p><p class=\"li\">A high stencil is the difference of increments in directions \\(d_1,d_2\\), with coefficient \\(e^{i(\\arg d_1-\\arg d_2)}\\) on the subtracted term; extensions share one arriving prefix and lift.</p><p class=\"li\">A diagonal-time slab of macroscopic thickness around the stencil contains no other non-diagonal tensors or endpoints. Diagonal chains may cross it. The stencil has an intact neighborhood disjoint from other chains except its own prefix, with spare routing and cut margins.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The contraction is the corresponding low or high expression of Proposition 4 between exterior states independent of the stencil term, up to bounded compensating even row pairs and a common bounded scalar. With at least \\(n\\) physical steps on both clear sides, its absolute value is at most\n\\[\nCn^{-1+\\rho}\\quad\\text{for a low stencil},\n\\qquad\nCn^{-1-\\rho}\\quad\\text{for a high stencil},\n\\]\ntimes the two exterior reflection norms. Reflected stencils obey the same statement.</p>", "proof": "<p>Reflection in angle \\(\\varphi\\), with conjugation and arrow reversal, changes the primitive to the \\(L_+,K(Q^{-1})\\) type with lifted direction \\(2\\varphi-\\alpha\\), up to a common scalar. Its angular parameter remains \\(\\tau=1\\), and reflected high coefficients are the reflected direction coefficients.</p><p>Let \\(a,b\\) be the two positive-time steps. Rewrite a negative increment as the negative forward increment ending at the original point, using Lemma 5.1 inside the intact neighborhood. For high directions \\(a,b\\), append two after-twist steps in the other direction to reach one common endpoint. For \\(-a,-b\\), prepend two before-twist steps from one common start. For \\(a,-b\\), use the trajectory \\(b,b,a,a\\) from \\(z-2b\\); interchange \\(a,b\\) for the other mixed case. These are precisely the compensating even pairs.</p><p>Approach the common start by one prefix with a final positive-direction segment, bending in spare lattice corridors if necessary. No additional full turn is introduced.</p><p>Consolidate all background diagonal chains into twists \\(\\zeta\\) before and \\(\\xi\\) after the start, with \\(\\zeta/\\xi=Q^\\epsilon\\). Route the \\(\\xi\\)-background on one common skirt outside the entire marker rectangle, on the same spin side of every possible source. Ice conservation moves it onto either compared path, multiplying the source matrix on one fixed side by one common scalar.</p><p>The old-to-new routing difference is a closed tile-edge chain, hence a weighted sum of face boundaries. Contributions cancel except at the source spin jump. All prospective source edges lie in the same winding region of this routing difference, since other chains avoid the intact box and the skirt avoids the whole marker rectangle. Thus the source phase is common to all stencil terms. Proposition 4 applies with term-independent exterior states.</p><p>At a reflection cut, conjugation inverts charge and path reversal inverts it again, so joined chains continue with the same charge and no new endpoint. Other intact stencils retain their high or low type, proving compatibility with repeated extraction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “Local deformation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The primitive is invariant, pairing by pairing and for arbitrary outside spins, under simple-path isotopies continuing its initial lift and keeping endpoints in their regions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the primitive of Proposition 5, in the positive winding expansion, and keep other insertions outside its deformation corridor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The primitive is invariant, pairing by pairing and for arbitrary outside spins, under simple-path isotopies continuing its initial lift and keeping endpoints in their regions. A pure diagonal string obeys the analogous source-free deformation rule.</p>", "proof": "<p>Draw strands smoothly and disjointly. Orthogonalize each path-crossing direction with rotation less than \\(\\pi/2\\). Sliding a source while its strand tangent changes by \\(b\\) changes the two departing-piece turns by \\(-2b\\); the factor \\(e^{-i\\rho b}\\) cancels the marker gauge.</p><p>At a tangency, terms marked elsewhere are unchanged because the two new diagonal factors cancel. The two terms marked at the newly created crossings cancel each other. If \\(c_1\\) is the first-crossing spin when the second crossing is marked, and \\(\\beta\\) is the test strand tangent, then\n\\[\n(\\alpha_2-\\beta_2)-(\\alpha_1-\\beta_1)=-c_1\\pi.\n\\]\nThe marker and winding ratio is \\(e^{-ic_1\\lambda}\\), while the extra prefix factor is \\(Q^{c_1}\\); their product is \\(-1\\). Outside constraints agree. Generic isotopies have only such tangencies and transverse slides. For a pure string only the diagonal cancellation is needed, equivalently ice conservation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Tree evaluation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a fixed pairing, the primitive is the sum over a marked edge on the root-to-\\(z\\)-region tree path, with factors \\(k_e\\) on earlier path edges, \\(c_*a_e\\) on the marked edge, and \\(w_e\\) elsewhere.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In a disk, order boundary ports from a flat gap \\(p\\), using factors \\(b_j(c)\\) from Lemma 2.2.</p><p class=\"li\">A path starts inward from \\(p\\), with initial tangent lift between the positive boundary tangent and that tangent plus \\(\\pi\\), and ends at a lattice vertex \\(z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Boundary arcs form the separator tree, rooted at the region of \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an arc \\(e:i\\to j\\), put\n  \\[\n  m=h_i-h_j,\\quad\n  w_e=\\frac{\\cos((m-1/2)\\lambda)}{\\sin\\lambda},\n  \\quad\n  k_e=-\\frac{\\cos((m+1/2)\\lambda)}{\\sin\\lambda},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  a_e=e^{-i\\lambda(h_i+h_j)},\\qquad\n  c_*=\\frac{e^{-i\\lambda}}{2\\sin\\lambda}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a fixed pairing, the primitive is the sum over a marked edge on the root-to-\\(z\\)-region tree path, with factors \\(k_e\\) on earlier path edges, \\(c_*a_e\\) on the marked edge, and \\(w_e\\) elsewhere. Unmarked internal loops weigh \\(d\\); a marked closed loop contributes zero.</p><p>A pure \\(K(Q)\\) string uses \\(k_e\\) on its entire tree path. An internal loop enclosing \\(z\\) then weighs \\(-2\\) instead of \\(d\\), and its nesting parity records the color change between the boundary-tree region and the lattice color of \\(z\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 5.1 choose a simple path crossing minimally the separating boundary arcs, then nested closed loops. A single outward source on a closed loop violates arrow consistency and gives zero.</p><p>At a parent-to-child crossing, the boundary-ordered arc tangent lift \\(\\beta\\) and path lift \\(\\alpha\\) satisfy exactly\n\\[\n\\beta-\\alpha=\\pi/2.\n\\]\nClose the boundary segment, arc, and reversed path into a simple positive curve; its full turn is \\(\\beta-\\alpha+3\\pi/2=2\\pi\\). There is no extra \\(2\\pi\\) ambiguity.</p><p>The two departing-piece turns sum to \\(\\theta_i+\\theta_j-\\pi-2\\beta\\). Boundary factors and gauge therefore give \\(c_*a_e\\). Summing unmarked orientations gives \\(w_e\\); adding a prefix \\(K(Q)\\) gives \\(k_e\\). A counterclockwise enclosing loop contributes\n\\[\ne^{i\\lambda}Q^{-1}+e^{-i\\lambda}Q=-2.\n\\]\nCrossing a medial loop changes adjacent color, giving the parity assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 6.5, “Identification of a holomorphic subsequential limit,” together with the four-change boundary construction of Section 6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Then \\(0<r<1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), \\(d=2\\cos\\lambda=\\sqrt q\\), \\(\\rho=\\lambda/\\pi\\), and \\(s,t,\\Delta\\) as in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega\\) be a fixed simple orthogonal polygon with counterclockwise flat boundary marks \\(p,b,c,d_0\\), dividing it into intervals \\(A,B,C,D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use whole-tile approximants at mesh \\(n^{-1}\\), shifted by \\(O(n^{-1})\\), with the balanced corner parities of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Intervals \\(A,C\\) designate color \\(\\mathsf A\\), and \\(B,D\\) color \\(\\mathsf B\\). Choose the global assignment for which the deterministic-cap partition completed around \\(A,C\\) is at least its globally reversed counterpart.</p><p class=\"li\">The open-cap law leaves four unmatched ports unpaired and weights all closed loops by \\(d\\), with no extra pairing weights.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_{\\mathsf A}\\) be an actual \\(\\mathsf A\\)-path joining \\(A,C\\), and \\(E_{\\mathsf B}\\) an actual \\(\\mathsf B\\)-path joining \\(B,D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use boundary bases\n  \\[\n  (H_A,H_B,H_C,H_D)=(s+1,1,s,0),\n  \\quad h_i=H_R+\\Delta\\mathbf1_{\\{i\\text{ high}\\}},\\ i\\in R.\n  \\]\n  On the second lap the base decreases by one. Put\n  \\[\n  x=e^{-i\\lambda},\\quad y=e^{i\\lambda},\\quad\n  P_0=x^{s+1},\\quad G=1-y^2,\\quad A_R=x^{2H_R+\\Delta}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_V,D_V\\) be the separator-tree variables of Lemma 6.1 below, and define\n  \\[\n  h_n(z)=\\frac{\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{open}}H_{V(z)}}{P_0G}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(h_n\\to h\\) locally continuously in the interior, \\(h\\) is holomorphic, and\n  \\(\\mathbb P_{\\mathrm{open}}(E_{\\mathsf A})\\to r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Then \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\). With\n\\[\nU_0=r+d(1-r),\\quad V_0=dr+1-r,\\quad T_0=r+y(1-r),\n\\]\nthe function \\(h\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) conformally onto the simple polygon with successive vertices\n\\[\n0,\\ U_0,\\ T_0,\\ yV_0,\n\\]\ntaking \\(p,b,c,d_0\\) to those vertices. If the marks have half-plane coordinates \\(0,\\chi,1,\\infty\\), then\n\\[\nr=\n\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\nu^{\\rho-1}|u-\\chi|^{\\rho-1}|u-1|^{1-3\\rho}\\,du}\n{\\displaystyle\\int_\\chi^\\infty\nu^{\\rho-1}|u-\\chi|^{\\rho-1}|u-1|^{1-3\\rho}\\,du}.\n\\]\nUnder deterministic separate \\(\\mathsf A\\)-caps, the actual crossing probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{r}{r+d(1-r)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fixed actual RSW corridors give both pairing alternatives positive probability, so \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\). Lemmas 6.2–6.3 give boundary limits \\(0,U_0,yV_0\\) at \\(p,b,d_0\\). At every other unmarked point, the line invariant and Lemma 6.4 put \\(h\\) on the corresponding line of direction\n\\(1,-y^{-1},y^2,-y\\). In a conformal half-plane coordinate, harmonic Schwarz reflection extends \\(h\\) across each unmarked interval.</p><p>On \\(B,C\\), the bounded corrected variable has only the two values of Lemma 6.2; averaging therefore puts the traces on the actual segments \\([U_0,T_0]\\) and \\([T_0,yV_0]\\). Shielding at \\(c\\) makes their trace limits \\(T_0\\).</p><p>For interior continuity at \\(c\\), use a half-plane coordinate \\(\\zeta\\) with \\(c=0\\). Polynomial growth and the two boundary directions imply\n\\[\ne^{-2i\\lambda}\\zeta^{3\\rho-1}(h(\\zeta)-T_0)\n\\]\nhas real values on both sides. Reflection gives a punctured-disk holomorphic function with at most a pole. Its leading power after undoing the multiplier is positive because both boundary traces tend to zero. Thus \\(h\\to T_0\\) from the interior.</p><p>The target polygon is counterclockwise and simple; \\(T_0\\) is reflex for \\(d&gt;1\\), and lies on the triangle side at \\(d=1\\). Straightening boundary traces in their lines preserves winding about points off those lines. The argument principle gives exactly one preimage of generic interior points and none of exterior points. The open mapping theorem then places the image inside the polygon and the remaining traces on their actual segments. This proves conformal bijectivity.</p><p>The target angles are \\(\\rho\\pi,\\rho\\pi,(2-3\\rho)\\pi,\\rho\\pi\\). Schwarz–Christoffel gives derivative proportional to\n\\[\n\\zeta^{\\rho-1}(\\zeta-\\chi)^{\\rho-1}(\\zeta-1)^{1-3\\rho}.\n\\]\nThe \\(B,C\\) side vectors have lengths \\(1-r,r\\), giving the integral ratio. Completing caps reweights alternatives by \\(d,d^2\\), yielding \\(r/(r+d(1-r))\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, “Tree normalization and connection alternatives.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The number \\(I\\) of loops closed by local caps is the number of unwired tree vertices.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the setting of Proposition 6, cut along all raw boundary arcs, ignoring internal closed loops when defining regions.</p><p class=\"li\">The region adjacency tree is rooted at the region incident to \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A tree vertex is wired if it visits a gap of its own color on an interval designating that color.</p><p class=\"li\">For a boundary arc \\(e:i\\to j\\), \\(i&lt;j\\), define \\(m_e,w_e,k_e,a_e\\) as in Lemma 5.2, and let \\(P(V)\\) be the root-to-\\(V\\) path.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The number \\(I\\) of loops closed by local caps is the number of unwired tree vertices. Exactly one of \\(E_{\\mathsf A},E_{\\mathsf B}\\) occurs. Completing around \\(A,C\\) adds respectively one and two loops.</p><p>The tree variables are\n\\[\nD_V=d^{-I}\\prod_{e\\in P(V)}k_e\\prod_{e\\notin P(V)}w_e,\n\\]\n\\[\nH_V=d^{-I}\\sum_{e\\in P(V)}\na_e\\prod_{\\substack{f\\in P(V)\\\\f\\text{ before }e}}k_f\n\\prod_{\\substack{f\\text{ not before }e\\\\f\\ne e}}w_f.\n\\]\nIf \\(e\\) joins parent \\(u\\) to child \\(v\\), with \\(D_e^\\circ\\) the common product omitting \\(e\\), then\n\\[\nH_v-H_u=a_eD_e^\\circ,\\qquad\nD_u=w_eD_e^\\circ,\\qquad D_v=k_eD_e^\\circ.\n\\]\nThe primitive contraction divided by open-cap weight is \\(c_*H_{V(z)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Attach a thin exterior annulus containing the caps and extend unmatched ports to its outer boundary. Remaining annular regions connect exactly the designated wire-color visits. Two noncrossing open strands cut a disk into three boundary regions, with one more region for each boundary loop. Precisely the unwired tree regions are cut off, giving the loop count and the opposite-pair alternative.</p><p>These are actual connection alternatives: switch arcs are perimeters between thickened diagonal graphs; same-color visits in one boundary region belong to the same actual component. Internal loops do not split the boundary class. The two noncrossing four-port pairings agree or disagree with the deterministic pairing, adding two or one loops as stated. Lemma 5.2 gives the formulas, and isolating the term marked on \\(e\\) gives their increments.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, “Marked values and corrected observable.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a typical tree, \\( D_p=D_b=D_{d_0}=0, \\) \\( \\frac{H_b}{P_0G}= \\begin{cases}1&E_{\\mathsf A},\\\\d&E_{\\mathsf B},\\end{cases} \\qquad \\frac{H_{d_0}}{P_0G}= y\\begin{cases}d&E_{\\mathsf A},\\\\1&E_{\\mathsf B}.\\end{cases} \\) On …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A tree is typical if its four terminal marked legs have positive length.</p><p class=\"li\">For an arc \\(e\\), let \\(L(e)\\) count \\(b,c,d_0\\) in its child interval.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma(\\mathsf A)=P_0y^2,\\qquad\n  \\Gamma(\\mathsf B)=-P_0y,\\qquad\n  H_V^\\circ=H_V-\\Gamma(\\operatorname{col}V)D_V,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  T=\\mathbf1_{E_{\\mathsf A}}+y\\mathbf1_{E_{\\mathsf B}}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a typical tree,\n\\[\nD_p=D_b=D_{d_0}=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{H_b}{P_0G}=\n\\begin{cases}1&amp;E_{\\mathsf A},\\\\d&amp;E_{\\mathsf B},\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\frac{H_{d_0}}{P_0G}=\ny\\begin{cases}d&amp;E_{\\mathsf A},\\\\1&amp;E_{\\mathsf B}.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nOn gap visits of interval \\(R\\),\n\\[\n\\Re(H_V/A_R)-c_R(\\operatorname{col}V)D_V\n\\]\nis constant, where\n\\[\nc_R(\\text{wire color})=-\\cos\\lambda,\\qquad\nc_R(\\text{other color})=\\cos(2\\lambda).\n\\]\nThe variables \\(H_V^\\circ\\) are uniformly bounded over all trees and vertices. On a typical tree they equal \\(P_0GT\\) along the \\(c\\)-leg and its descendants. Along \\(B\\) their only values are \\(H_b,P_0GT\\), and along \\(C\\) their only values are \\(H_{d_0},P_0GT\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The exact edge table is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nL&amp;w_e&amp;k_e\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;d,1&amp;-1,-d\\\\\n1\\ (b\\text{ or }d_0)&amp;1&amp;0\\\\\n1\\ (c)&amp;1&amp;-d\\\\\n2&amp;1,d&amp;0,-(d^2-1)\\\\\n3&amp;0&amp;1.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nPaired entries correspond to high and low first ports.</p><p>The marked subtree has four terminal legs and either one common branch node or an \\(L=2\\) chain. Type \\(X\\) encloses \\(c,d_0\\) with ports on \\(B,D\\); type \\(Y\\) encloses \\(b,c\\) with ports on \\(A,C\\). Write \\(\\mathsf C=\\mathsf B,\\mathsf A\\), respectively, and chain colors \\(c_0,\\ldots,c_m\\). Leg and branch nodes are wired. Internal chain nodes are unwired exactly when their color differs from \\(\\mathsf C\\). If \\(I_0\\) counts high-starting \\(L=0\\) edges, then\n\\[\nI=I_0+\\#\\{0&lt;j&lt;m:c_j\\ne\\mathsf C\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF=d^{-I_{\\mathrm{mid}}}\\prod_{L(e)=2}w_e\n=\n\\begin{cases}\nd^{\\mathbf1_{\\{c_0\\ne\\mathsf C\\}}}&amp;m&gt;0,\\\\\n1&amp;m=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nOff-path \\(L=0\\) factors cancel their cap contributions. Within an \\(L=0\\) branch in \\(R\\), put \\(g_R=\\tfrac12\\) on wire color and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) otherwise. Then\n\\[\nk_e/w_e=-d^{g_R(\\operatorname{col}v)-g_R(\\operatorname{col}u)},\n\\qquad a_e=A_R,\n\\]\nso two successive \\(H\\)-increments cancel.</p><p>An unpassed \\(L=3\\) edge kills all \\(H\\)-terms; only its last edge can mark. The first \\(b\\)- or \\(d_0\\)-leg edge has \\(k=0\\), killing later marks. At chain node \\(0\\),\n\\[\nD=F,\\qquad H/P_0=\n\\begin{cases}F&amp;c_0=\\mathsf A,\\\\-y^3F&amp;c_0=\\mathsf B.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe \\(b\\)-leg coefficients are \\(-y^2,y^{-1}\\), and the \\(d_0\\)-leg coefficients \\(-y^4,y\\). Middle-chain amplitudes are \\(y\\) in type \\(X\\) and \\(-y^2\\) in type \\(Y\\). A zero \\(k\\) terminates the sum; otherwise the chain and target contribution is \\(2-d^2\\) times that amplitude. The identities\n\\[\nd+(2-d^2)y=yG,\\qquad\n-dy^3-(2-d^2)y^2=G\n\\]\ngive the marked values.</p><p>At either endpoint of an arc, product-to-sum gives the line invariant through the increment formulas of Lemma 6.1. For \\(R=B,C\\),\n\\[\n\\Re(\\Gamma/A_R)=c_R,\\qquad\nd\\Gamma(\\text{wire color})+\\Gamma(\\text{other color})=-A_R.\n\\]\nThus \\(H^\\circ\\) is unchanged on their \\(L=0\\) branches and on the \\(c\\)-leg. Other table entries kill a prefix within at most two chain steps; only the uncorrected \\(c\\)-leg can grow. Hence \\(H^\\circ\\) is uniformly bounded.</p><p>The two line invariants meeting at a \\(c\\)-leg node give \\(P_0GT\\); at \\(d=1\\) use continuity for each finite tree. The boundary line directions, after dividing by \\(P_0G\\), are\n\\[\n1,\\ -y^{-1},\\ y^2,\\ -y\n\\quad\\text{on }A,B,C,D.\n\\]\nAt chain node \\(0\\), \\(H^\\circ/(P_0G)\\) is \\(F\\) or \\(yF\\). If the first \\(k\\) is nonzero, the first jump already gives \\(T\\); otherwise the far value is immediate. This proves the two-value assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.3, “Compactness and boundary shielding.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every fixed positive moment of \\(|H_{V(z)}|+|D_{V(z)}|\\) is bounded by a power of \\(\\min(1,|z-c|)^{-1}\\), uniformly farther than mesh distance from \\(c\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the four-change observable of Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every fixed positive moment of \\(|H_{V(z)}|+|D_{V(z)}|\\) is bounded by a power of \\(\\min(1,|z-c|)^{-1}\\), uniformly farther than mesh distance from \\(c\\). The expectations \\(h_n\\) are asymptotically equicontinuous on interior compact sets; all lattice parities have the same locally continuous subsequential limits.</p><p>Typicality has probability tending to one. Approaching an ordinary boundary point, including a corner, the tree region visits its interval with probability tending to one after the mesh limit. Near \\(b,c,d_0\\), the point lies beyond an edge of the corresponding leg with high probability; near \\(p\\), an unpassed \\(L=3\\) edge remains with high probability.</p>", "proof": "<p>The table and two-step cancellation bound the variables by \\(Ce^{C\\ell}\\), where \\(\\ell\\) counts passed \\(c\\)-leg edges. A passed \\(c\\)-leg disk containing \\(z\\) cannot lie wholly inside a sufficiently small fixed multiple of \\(\\min(1,|z-c|)\\) around \\(c\\). Dyadic passage bands between that scale and macroscopic scale count such arcs. Conditional passage moments, with disjoint-buffer coloring, give the stated polynomial moments. Open caps are a bounded reweighting of deterministic caps.</p><p>Different tree regions for two nearby interior vertices require a boundary-reaching raw arc between them. Bulk circuits make this uniformly unlikely. Hölder and the moments give equicontinuity, including across parities.</p><p>For shielding, in separated wall-sector buffers impose opposite-color actual wall-to-wall crosscuts, one inside the other. Up-to-wall RSW gives uniform conditional probability. A medial separator lies between them: the bank gaps have opposite colors, hence an odd number of ports between the crosscut ends, and the color paths prohibit radial escape. A raw arc must join the banks and cut off the deeper box. This works in corner sectors as well. Iteration proves all boundary and typicality assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, “Diagonal residual cancellation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\lim_{z\\to u}\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{open}} [\\gamma(\\operatorname{col}V(z))D_{V(z)}] \\right|=0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\gamma\\) be any bounded function of the two tree-region colors.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u\\in\\partial\\Omega\\setminus\\{p,b,c,d_0\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Interior locations \\(z\\to u\\) are taken after the mesh limit.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\lim_{z\\to u}\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{open}}\n[\\gamma(\\operatorname{col}V(z))D_{V(z)}]\n\\right|=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First replace the primitive by a full \\(K(Q)\\) string to fixed interior \\(z\\). If \\(N\\) counts internal loops surrounding \\(z\\), their ratio is \\(-2/d\\). Choose opposite color signs \\(\\sigma\\). Nesting parity and equality of winding expansions give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{open}}\n[\\sigma(\\operatorname{col}V)D_V(2/d)^N]=o(1).\n\\]</p><p>Indeed, the wrong expansion relative to complementary caps is bounded absolutely by\n\\[\nK_*^{1+X_*}\n\\prod_{\\nu=p,b,d_0}\\mathbf1_{\\{N_\\nu=0\\}}\\,\nd^{N_c}\n\\prod_{v\\text{ corner}}f_{\\mathrm{loc}}(g_v)^{N_v/2},\n\\qquad g_v=\\pm\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nAt \\(b,d_0,c\\), wrong drops \\(-s,-s,-t\\) give \\(0,0,d\\). At \\(p\\), the locally forward arc intersects the exiting string with net intersection \\(-1\\); changing winding gives effective drop \\(s+1\\), again zero. Internal loops around \\(z\\) have absolute weight \\(2|\\cos2\\lambda|\\le d\\). Nonlocal factors are charged to \\(X_*\\).</p><p>Without the passage factor, the logarithmic upper bound is\n\\[\n3P+D_*+\\sum C_{i(v)}+\\sum L_{i(v)}+O(1).\n\\]\nIts second moment is polynomial. Hölder and first/second-moment interpolation absorb \\(K_*^{1+X_*}\\) with \\(O(\\eta\\log n)\\) loss, without requiring a lower first-moment bound. Proposition 3 and strict arm decay make the total strictly negative. The chosen global color assignment bounds the complementary-cap normalization by the correct one.</p><p>To remove \\((2/d)^N\\), let \\(\\mu\\) be deterministic caps and\n\\(R_*=d^{\\ell_{\\mathrm{open}}-\\ell_{\\mathrm{det}}}\\). For the interval \\(R\\) containing \\(u\\), set\n\\[\nB_*=R_*\\sigma(\\operatorname{col}V)d^{-g_R(\\operatorname{col}V)}D_V,\n\\quad\nT_*=d^{g_R(\\operatorname{col}V)}(2/d)^N.\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n|z-u|\\ll r_1\\ll r_2\\ll r_3\\ll1\n\\]\nwith diverging ratios as \\(z\\to u\\). Actual screens confine changing arcs near \\(u\\), so \\(B_*\\) is exterior-readable outside \\(r_2\\), except on a rare event. Affected arcs are \\(L=0\\); their on-path \\(k/w\\) suffix telescopes, canceled by the color factor. Denote the exterior approximation by \\(B_{\\mathrm{out}}\\).</p><p>Truncate \\(T_*\\) to loops inside \\(r_1\\), reconstructing tree color from loop parity and lattice color; call it \\(T_{\\mathrm{in}}\\). Failure requires a raw path from scale \\(O(|z-u|)\\) to \\(r_1\\). Let \\(U_z=(2/d)^{N_0}\\), with \\(N_0\\) counting nests in a ball of radius \\(\\operatorname{dist}(z,\\partial\\Omega)/8\\). Under the \\(U_z\\)-tilt, these failures remain rare. Tilt-edge passage estimates give bounded moments for \\(B_*,T_*/U_z,T_{\\mathrm{in}}/U_z\\), uniformly as \\(z\\to u\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu T_{\\mathrm{in}}\\asymp\\mathbb E_\\mu U_z\\ge1.\n\\]\nThus product truncation errors are \\(o(\\mathbb E_\\mu U_z)\\).</p><p>The one-sector relative comparison between \\(r_1,r_2\\) yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[B_{\\mathrm{out}}T_{\\mathrm{in}}]\n-\\mathbb E_\\mu B_{\\mathrm{out}}\\mathbb E_\\mu T_{\\mathrm{in}}\n=o(\\mathbb E_\\mu U_z).\n\\]\nUse the fixed-\\(z\\) cancellation first and then \\(z\\to u\\), obtaining \\(\\mathbb E_\\mu B_*\\to0\\). Apply the same separation without tilt to bounded functions of the reconstructed tree color. Choosing the inverse bounded color factors and dividing by \\(\\mathbb E_\\mu R_*\\) proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 7.2, “Complete-wall factorization.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For sufficiently large fixed \\(L\\), there are probe-independent complex numbers \\(a_n(R_0,L)\\), bounded above and away from zero in modulus, such that \\( \\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{full}}(z,u) =a_n(R_0,L)h_n(z)J_n(u)+o(1) \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the polygon and four-change interior observable of Proposition 6. Put \\(O=\\mathbb C\\setminus\\Omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Replace contractions at used wall ports by products of boundary factors on the two banks. Exterior wire color is opposite interior wire color; high statuses agree.</p><p class=\"li\">The exterior order from \\(p\\) is \\(D,C,B,A\\), its physical normal is \\(\\theta_O=\\theta_\\Omega+\\pi\\), and its raw bases are \\(H_R-\\tfrac12\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put a primitive from \\(p\\) to \\(z\\in\\Omega\\) and one from \\(p\\) to \\(u\\) in a simply connected exterior strip along \\(D\\), approaching \\(p,d_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Add short exterior interval strings with charges\n  \\[\n  P_D=0,\\quad P_C=-2s,\\quad P_B=-2(s+t),\\quad P_A=-2(2s+t).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Add two finite lists of distant charge endpoints, each with admissible positive magnitudes summing to one. Their charge-\\(-a_j\\) strings share a cut from \\(p\\), then split at scales \\(r_j\\asymp R_0L^{j-1}\\), never returning near the polygon. Ordered parallel lanes put pure strings on the non-probe side.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu\\) be deterministic separate primal caps on each wall component, \\(Z^{\\mathrm{cap}}\\) its vacuum-relative partition, and \\(\\Xi(z,u)\\) the spin contraction. If \\(N_j\\) counts endpoint nests in disjoint buffered cutoff patches, put\n  \\[\n  U=\\prod_j u_+(a_j)^{N_j},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{full}}(z,u)\n  =\\frac{\\Xi(z,u)}{Z^{\\mathrm{cap}}\\mathbb E_\\mu U}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(J_n(u)\\) by the exterior disk-tree formula on \\(O\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\), using inverted boundary order \\(D,C,B,A\\), bases \\((s+1,1,s,0)\\), and normalization \\(P_0G\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large fixed \\(L\\), there are probe-independent complex numbers \\(a_n(R_0,L)\\), bounded above and away from zero in modulus, such that\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{full}}(z,u)\n=a_n(R_0,L)h_n(z)J_n(u)+o(1)\n\\]\nuniformly on compact probe sets, with mesh limit first and \\(R_0\\to\\infty\\) second. Every locally uniform limit of \\(J_n\\) is continuous and nonconstant on the connected probe strip.</p>", "proof": "<p>The exterior tree is finite because no raw boundary arc escapes to infinity. Its table gives local boundedness and equicontinuity. Shielding gives limiting value zero at \\(p\\) and a value bounded away from zero at \\(d_0\\); hence every continuous limit \\(J\\) is nonconstant.</p><p>Slide the short strings onto the complete wall without crossing the source. The profile \\(H_R-\\tfrac12+P_R\\) differs from the ideal exterior profile by the constant \\(-s-\\tfrac32\\), contributing one fixed source phase. For inversion about \\(z_*\\), use the chosen lift\n\\[\n\\psi=\\arg\\left(\\left(\\frac1{z-z_*}\\right)'\\right).\n\\]\nThe inverted normal gains \\(\\psi\\); an arc’s turn gains the endpoint \\(\\psi\\)-difference minus \\(4\\pi\\) times signed intersections with the outward cut. Boundary factors cancel endpoint terms, and the marker gauge cancels marked-arc start terms. The common total charge \\(-2\\) supplies the remaining cut multiplier. Thus bounded open-arc weights agree with the ideal exterior weights up to one common phase before strings split.</p><p>For absolute estimates, temporarily slide pure-string launch endpoints along the solid non-probe bank, recording the corresponding diagonal port phases. Choose regular source tubes with \\(p\\) inside a flat entrance face. Deform each source path to cross minimally its tube separators. A sufficiently small separator is an entire launch-straddling arc. Its effective drop is \\(-s\\) before the prefix and \\(-s-1\\) after it: its weight is zero while unpassed and unit in absolute value while passed, so only the last nested candidate can mark. Other small flips have unit magnitude, ordinary disks cancel caps, and all remaining factors are bounded per macroscopic passage. Hence\n\\[\n|\\text{normalized source sum}|\n\\le |Y/U|C^{1+X_0},\n\\]\nwith all fixed passage moments.</p><p>RSW circuits between the wall and first endpoints make open-arc escape to splitting scales unlikely under \\(\\nu_U\\). Lemma 7.1 and Hölder remove this exceptional contribution. Choose\n\\(1\\ll R_1\\ll R_2\\ll R_0\\). Truncate the ideal tree integrand to data inside \\(R_1\\) and \\(Y/U\\) to data outside \\(R_2\\). Their errors vanish by the same bounds. Relative bulk collar comparison factors expectations and replaces the inner tilted expectation by its unweighted one. Complete-wall interior/exterior independence then gives the displayed product. The coefficient is the common phase times \\(\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}(Y/U)\\) and bounded cap normalizations, so Lemma 7.1 bounds it away from zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, “Distant normalization.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large fixed \\(L\\), \\( \\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}(Y/U)\\ge c_L>0, \\qquad \\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}|Y/U|^p\\le C_{p,L} \\) for every fixed \\(p>0\\), uniformly for large \\(R_0\\) and fine mesh.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the complete-wall exterior law and endpoint tilt \\(\\nu_U\\propto U\\mu\\) of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">For an exterior closed loop \\(\\gamma\\), let \\(A_\\gamma\\) be the total endpoint magnitude in its physically bounded inside.</p><p class=\"li\">Its ordinary-weight ratio is\n  \\[\n  v(A_\\gamma)=\\frac{\\cos((A_\\gamma-1)\\lambda)}{\\cos\\lambda}\n  \\quad\\text{if it surrounds the wall},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  u(A_\\gamma)=\\frac{\\cos((1+A_\\gamma)\\lambda)}{\\cos\\lambda}\n  \\quad\\text{otherwise}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y\\) be the product of all nontrivial such ratios.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large fixed \\(L\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}(Y/U)\\ge c_L&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}|Y/U|^p\\le C_{p,L}\n\\]\nfor every fixed \\(p&gt;0\\), uniformly for large \\(R_0\\) and fine mesh.</p>", "proof": "<p>Between endpoint scales use corridor annuli \\(Kr_j&lt;|z-z_*|&lt;r_{j+1}/K\\). Winding loops wholly in corridor \\(j\\) have ratio\n\\(v(\\sum_{i\\le j}a_i)\\ge1\\), since total magnitude is two. Let \\(M_j\\) be their product and \\(M=\\prod_jM_j\\).</p><p>All remaining nontrivial loops, except the local endpoint nests, are counted by a finite family \\(X\\) of bordering-scale passages. Tilt-edge bounds give all fixed exponential moments uniformly, independently of sufficiently large \\(L\\). Thus\n\\[\n|Y/U|\\le MC^X,\\qquad\nY/U\\ge MC^{-X}\\quad\\text{off }\\mathcal B,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal B\\) means a loop excluding \\(z_*\\) encloses at least two endpoints. Dyadic passage bands in the corridors give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}M^b\\le C_bL^{C_b}.\n\\]</p><p>If a bad loop has smallest enclosed endpoint index \\(j\\), it traverses corridor \\(j\\) radially and excludes every winding loop counted by \\(M_j\\), so \\(M_j=1\\). Successive actual circuit blockers give conditional probability \\(CL^{-\\alpha}\\), even given endpoint patches and other \\(M_i\\). Apply this to \\(\\prod_{i\\ne j}M_i\\), then Hölder with exponent close to one and arbitrary exponential rates for \\(X\\). For some \\(\\alpha'&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}[MC^X\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal B}]\n\\le C'L^{-\\alpha'}\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}M.\n\\]\nThe same interpolation gives, for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}[MC^{-X}]\n\\ge c_\\varepsilon L^{-\\varepsilon}\\mathbb E_{\\nu_U}M.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\varepsilon&lt;\\alpha'\\), then \\(L\\) large. Since \\(M\\ge1\\), the positive lower bound follows. Hölder and the polynomial \\(M\\)-moments give all fixed absolute moments.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 8.1, “Gap closure.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are positive probe-independent numbers \\(b_n\\), bounded above and below, such that \\( \\mathcal H_g(z,u)=b_n\\mathcal H_f(z,u)+o(1) \\) uniformly on compact probe sets, with mesh limit first and \\(h\\to0\\) second.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the paired-wall geometry, fixed distant charges, source corridors, and normalized spin contraction of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Remove finitely many separated wall intervals of lengths comparable to \\(h\\), away from marks and source/string corridors. At a corner remove comparable intervals on both adjacent sides.</p><p class=\"li\">At each tip let \\(N_{\\mathrm{tip}}\\) count raw arcs joining the two banks around that tip, wholly inside a small cutoff box of scale \\(h\\), with disjoint buffers.</p><p class=\"li\">For a gapped wall \\(g\\), put\n  \\[\n  T_g=\\prod_{\\mathrm{tips}}(\\cot\\lambda)^{N_{\\mathrm{tip}}},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal H_g(z,u)=\n  \\frac{\\Xi_g(z,u)}\n  {Z_g^{\\mathrm{cap}}\\mathbb E_{\\mu_g}[UT_g]}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\) be obtained by closing the specified \\(h\\)-gaps, retaining any other fixed gaps and their tip factors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are positive probe-independent numbers \\(b_n\\), bounded above and below, such that\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_g(z,u)=b_n\\mathcal H_f(z,u)+o(1)\n\\]\nuniformly on compact probe sets, with mesh limit first and \\(h\\to0\\) second. A second, smaller collection of gaps can be closed before the first.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the same switches in both cut conventions. Put\n\\[\nS=UT_f,\\qquad T=T_g/T_f,\\qquad\n\\nu_g\\propto ST\\mu_g,\\quad \\nu_f\\propto S\\mu_f.\n\\]\nTheir \\(S\\)-supports avoid closing gaps.</p><p>In collars \\(h\\ll r_1\\ll1\\), impose a primal screen joining primal wire portions in one sector and a dual screen in the other. Repeated conditional RSW makes this inner good event likely, even with remote tilts and deeper tip data. It confines affected paths and makes transmitted outer primal connectivity the same in both conventions. Euler’s formula makes \\(\\ell_f-\\ell_g\\) local. On this event,\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_g=B\\mathcal O_f\n\\]\nwith an exact positive local multiplier.</p><p>For positivity, same-bank arcs have ordinary staggered \\(d,1\\) weights. Opposite-bank arcs have factor\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos(\\rho(W+\\theta_i-\\theta_j)/2)}{\\sin\\lambda}.\n\\]\nFor ports on the same ray, the turn-minus-normal difference is \\(\\pm2\\pi\\), giving \\(\\cot\\lambda\\); for different rays it is zero, giving \\(1/\\sin\\lambda\\). Bending a ray through a right angle rotates its normal equally. All local factors are positive.</p><p>Uniformly as \\(h\\to0\\),\n\\[\nK^{-(1+X_h)}T\\le B\\le K^{1+X_h}T,\n\\]\nwhere \\(X_h\\) has all fixed exponential moments under \\(\\nu_g\\). To see this without a cost per intermediate scale, eliminate affected arcs of diameter below a small fraction of \\(h\\), with descendants, in both conventions. Filled arcs lie on homogeneous intervals; gapped short affected arcs lie at a single tip. Ordinary cap increments cancel, while a single-tip arc transports one unmatched end and leaves exactly \\(\\cot\\lambda\\). Common descendants are removed identically. The difference between removed tip arcs and the tilt count is \\(O(X_h)\\). Surviving affected arcs have slot-scale passages; residual cap pairings differ at only \\(O(1+X_h)\\) ports. New local closed loops cancel their ordinary weights.</p><p>The same elimination counts, without confinement, give uniformly bounded fixed moments of\n\\(\\mathcal O_g/(ST)\\) and \\(\\mathcal O_f/S\\) under the required tilted laws. Use common regular source corridors and the launch rule from Proposition 7. Non-short affected arcs cost bounded factors per \\(X_h\\); other nonordinary pieces are fixed-scale passages \\(X_0\\). Local endpoint nests and retained tips are divided out. Very large loops enclosing all data have ratio one. Thus the absolute normalized sums are bounded by \\(K^{1+X_0+X_h}\\).</p><p>Choose \\(r_1\\ll r_2\\ll r_3\\ll1\\), with diverging ratios. Construct an exterior-readable approximation \\(F_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) to \\(\\mathcal O_f/S\\), exact unless a raw passage traverses the common collar. On the good event, every changed filling is removed by neutral homogeneous-interval cap elimination, leaving common adjacent reconnections. Moment bounds control errors. Put\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{in}}=(B/T)\\mathbf1_{\\{\\text{inner good}\\}}.\n\\]\nIt is nonnegative, local, has bounded moments, and expectation in \\([c,C]\\).</p><p>The outward two-sector relative comparison makes the outer law, conditional on an inner filling under \\(\\nu_g\\), relatively \\(o(1)\\)-close to that under \\(\\nu_f\\). Iterate over gaps. Uniform relative estimates survive the remote tilt \\(S\\), including its normalization. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_g}\\frac{\\mathcal O_g}{ST}\n=\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_g}[D_{\\mathrm{in}}F_{\\mathrm{out}}]+o(1)\n=\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_g}D_{\\mathrm{in}}\\,\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_f}\\frac{\\mathcal O_f}{S}+o(1).\n\\]\nThese outer expectations are \\(\\mathcal H_g,\\mathcal H_f\\). The positive first factor is \\(b_n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 9.2, “Mixed derivative saving.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For suitable finite secondary gaps and some \\(c>0\\), \\( \\left| (\\delta_{1,z}^{(2)}+i\\delta_{2,z}^{(2)}) \\delta_{j,u}^{(2)}\\mathcal H_g(z,u) \\right| =O(n^{-2-c}),\\qquad j=1,2, \\) uniformly on eligible compact probe sets.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a rational orthogonal polygon \\(\\Omega\\) and the paired-wall functions \\(\\mathcal H_g\\) of Propositions 7–8.</p><p class=\"li\">True vertical wall levels lie in \\(X_*\\!+\\!G_*\\mathbb Z\\) for fixed \\(G_*&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Probe neighborhoods have distinct heights, avoid horizontal feature levels and vertical wall levels, and no probe pair lies on a common axis diagonal. Distant endpoints avoid their height bands.</p><p class=\"li\">Primary gaps, of fixed width comparable to \\(h\\), include true corners and vertical-wall openings in each probe-height band.</p><p class=\"li\">For probe box \\(i\\), choose an anchor height \\(y_{*,i}\\) and \\(H_i&gt;0\\), with \\(2H_i\\) dividing \\(G_*\\), so that\n  \\[\n  l_i=\\frac{y_i+y_{*,i}-H_i}{2},\n  \\qquad y_i\\in(l_i,l_i+H_i)\n  \\]\n  with uniform margins.</p><p class=\"li\">Secondary gaps of fixed width \\(\\sigma\\ll h\\) are chosen by a finite reflection template.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\delta_{k,v}^{(2)}F=F(v+2n^{-1}e_k)-F(v).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For suitable finite secondary gaps and some \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\n(\\delta_{1,z}^{(2)}+i\\delta_{2,z}^{(2)})\n\\delta_{j,u}^{(2)}\\mathcal H_g(z,u)\n\\right|\n=O(n^{-2-c}),\\qquad j=1,2,\n\\]\nuniformly on eligible compact probe sets. Constants and template size may depend on the fixed gaps.</p>", "proof": "<p>The mixed stencil is one high insertion at \\(z\\) and one low insertion at \\(u\\). Choose the extraction deficit \\(\\varepsilon\\) in Lemma 9.3 small relative to \\(c_0\\) in Lemma 9.2. Extracted angular costs contribute\n\\[\n-(1+\\rho)(1-\\varepsilon)\\log n\n-(1-\\rho)(1-\\varepsilon)\\log n\n=(-2+2\\varepsilon)\\log n.\n\\]\nThe averaged baseline cancels exactly the normalization of \\(\\mathcal H_g\\), and the remaining feature cost is \\(-c_0\\log n+o(\\log n)\\). Since the tree is finite, choose all Hölder allowances with total less than \\(c_0/4\\), and \\(2\\varepsilon&lt;c_0/4\\). This yields \\(O(n^{-2-c})\\). All geometry is fixed before the mesh; no uniformity in shrinking-template size is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Axis cuts give Cauchy–Schwarz children with multiplicative weights \\(1/2\\).", "statement": "<p>Reflection and extraction, Section 9.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A fixed finite schedule uses axis-parallel reflection cuts through vacant tile-vertex lines and diagonal extraction slabs.</p><p class=\"li\">Local high and low stencil boxes remain intact.</p><p class=\"li\">A copy is favorable when its slope-\\(+1\\) line has a clear slab of fixed positive thickness, free of solids, endpoints, other stencils, and other features, with distinct diagonal-time projection.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Axis cuts give Cauchy–Schwarz children with multiplicative weights \\(1/2\\). A favorable high copy can be extracted at cost \\(O(n^{-1-\\rho})\\), and a favorable low copy at cost \\(O(n^{-1+\\rho})\\), retaining the exterior reflection norms. A finite median-extraction schedule removes all but an arbitrarily small expected residual favorable count.</p>", "proof": "<p>The plane Gram construction gives reflection inequalities. Diagonal chains can be rerouted transversally through cuts; conjugation and joined path reversal preserve flux, creating no endpoint.</p><p>Choose each source box smaller than all fixed cut distances. Every retained image contains the same connected local drawing. When extracting it, keep the final incoming segment fixed; the rerouting difference is outside the connected box and has one face coefficient throughout it. Proposition 5 therefore gives one phase for all stencil terms, including reflected \\(L_+,Q^{-1}\\) types and backward increments rewritten forward.</p><p>The two norms are reflected diagrams with the extracted middle band omitted and the positive square-layer metric at the cuts. Other favorable images remain favorable for sufficiently narrow bands.</p><p>If \\(k\\) favorable copies remain, extract one at median projection. If \\(k_-,k_+\\) are on its sides, the children contain \\(2k_-,2k_+\\), each at most \\(k\\), with half-average \\(k-1\\). Thus expected time to zero is at most the initial count. Truncating at a sufficiently large finite depth leaves arbitrarily small expected residual count.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The averaged baseline logarithmic cost is \\( \\log\\!\\left(Z_g^{\\mathrm{cap}}\\mathbb E_{\\mu_g}[UT_g]\\right)+O(1). \\) The additional averaged cost is at most …", "statement": "<p>Complete norm-diagram cost, Section 9.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a finite reflection/extraction tree from Lemma 9.1, with at least one preliminary split.</p><p class=\"li\">Complete the retained disjoint straight fragments by positive deterministic caps.</p><p class=\"li\">Assign even-reflection copies correct local weights and odd-reflection copies absolute wrong-expansion weights.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The averaged baseline logarithmic cost is\n\\[\n\\log\\!\\left(Z_g^{\\mathrm{cap}}\\mathbb E_{\\mu_g}[UT_g]\\right)+O(1).\n\\]\nThe additional averaged cost is at most\n\\[\n3P+D_*+\\frac12\\sum_j\\log\\frac{F_-(a_j)}{F_+(a_j)}\n\\le P+o(\\log n)\n\\le-c_0\\log n+o(\\log n)\n\\]\nfor some \\(c_0&gt;0\\). Unextracted insertions contribute no asserted decay, only bounded local sums and macroscopic passage errors.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a final leaf take absolute winding weights. Reflection plus conjugation gives the wrong expansion on odd copies. Ordinary disks have their cap weights. On each bank, correct flat-flip magnitudes are one; wrong local straddles have weight zero at \\(p,b,d_0\\) and \\(d\\) at \\(c\\). At \\(p\\), count the exiting diagonal prefix and effective drop \\(-s\\). Straight-fragment tip-cross factors are \\(\\cot\\lambda\\) in either absolute convention. A charge endpoint has \\(u_+\\) on an even copy and \\(u_-\\) on an odd copy. There are no true corners on retained solids.</p><p>An unextracted source must connect to a solid or another source; it cannot be a singly broken closed component, so it is macroscopic. All other nonordinary arcs or loops are also counted by fixed-scale passages. Total diagonal divergence is zero, and loops enclosing all finite data retain ordinary weight. Cap elimination bounds the leaf by its local factors times \\(C^{1+X}\\). Hölder and local tilts absorb this at arbitrarily small logarithmic loss.</p><p>Two banks at a mark are separate half-sector patches. Tip expectations compare to original normalizations; complementary tips are handled by reflection, polarity exchange, and collar comparison. Lemma 1.8 factors segment partitions and compares reversed caps. Thus each feature carries its original baseline, with extra wrong costs \\(2P\\) for a colocated \\(s\\)-flip pair, \\(2D_*\\) for a \\(t\\)-flip pair, and \\(\\log(F_-/F_+)\\) for an endpoint.</p><p>Under half-weights every original feature has expected copy count one. Every child pairs retained features with opposite-parity mirrors, so expected even and odd counts are each \\(1/2\\). Baselines therefore average to the precise original normalization. There are three \\(s\\)-marks, one \\(t\\)-mark, both banks, and two charge lists each of total magnitude one. Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n3P+D_*+\\tfrac12\\sum_j\\log(F_-/F_+)\n\\le2P+D_*+o(\\log n)\\le P+o(\\log n).\n\\]\nStrict arm decay gives the saving.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.1, “Finite reflection templates.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), finitely many reflections, extractions, and secondary gaps can be chosen so the expected extracted count of each original marker is at least \\(1-\\varepsilon\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed primary-gap geometry and eligible probe pairs of Proposition 9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), finitely many reflections, extractions, and secondary gaps can be chosen so the expected extracted count of each original marker is at least \\(1-\\varepsilon\\). All cuts have strict macroscopic clearances. Finitely many templates cover an eligible compact probe set, and their secondary gaps may be united before choosing sufficiently small fixed \\(\\sigma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Partition a bounded vertical interval containing all data into finitely many distinctly labeled cells, including the two marker cells. A horizontal fold splitting a word after \\(j\\) of \\(m\\) cells produces\n\\[\nWW^{\\mathrm{rev}},\\qquad V^{\\mathrm{rev}}V\n\\]\nof lengths \\(2j,2(m-j)\\).</p><p>Under size-biased weights \\(j/m,1-j/m\\), label frequencies form a bounded martingale. Terminal expected frequency times initial length equals terminal expected count under ordinary half-weights. Choose a near-middle split maximizing frequency variance. Away from pure frequencies, some admissible consecutive split has\n\\[\n\\|\\mathrm{counts}_{\\le j}-j\\pi\\|\\ge c,\n\\]\nbecause consecutive differences are a single-label vector minus \\(\\pi\\). Conditional variance is at least \\(c'/m^2\\). Along the size-biased chain, \\(\\sum m^{-2}=\\infty\\): lengths change by bounded amounts, and drift and variance of \\(\\log m\\) are \\(O(m^{-2})\\); finite sum would force \\(\\log m\\) to converge and \\(m\\) to remain bounded, contradicting summability. A bounded martingale cannot accumulate this variance at a mixed limit. Frequencies therefore become pure, making expected impurity arbitrarily small at finite depth.</p><p>Outer-end folds increase length arbitrarily without losing size-biased mass. A sufficiently pure majority-marker branch has all but a small fraction of markers deep inside long pure runs. Adjacent copies alternate orientation. Their projection is a triangular wave of period \\(2H_i\\), sending marker ordinates to \\(y_i\\) and a displacement \\(H_i\\) to \\(y_{*,i}\\).</p><p>Before choosing horizontal depth, choose a finite vertical-cut schedule for each target marker. Horizontal folds do not enlarge the original horizontal support. Starting from its abscissa \\(x_0\\), retain the child containing all columns at cuts just beyond the rightmost copy, using the farther wall-free side. If the cut is \\(c'\\), the rightmost copy and tail projection are\n\\[\na'_{\\max}=2c'-x_0,\\qquad\ns'\\mapsto a'_{\\max}+x_0-s'.\n\\]\nDrift residues modulo \\(2H_i\\) toward \\(A_*=X_*-H_i\\). For lifted mean \\(\\mu\\), choose \\(c'\\equiv\\mu+v\\pmod{H_i}\\). Reflected lifts are \\(2(\\mu+v)-a\\), so mean advances by \\(v\\) and variance by \\(v^2\\). Choose \\(Nv\\) to realize the bounded positive desired displacement and then \\(N\\) large. Chebyshev makes all but a small fraction of residues lie within the primary-gap tolerance of \\(A_*\\), and\n\\[\na_{\\max}+x_0\\equiv2A_*\\pmod{2H_i}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose pure-run depth exceeding folded horizontal span divided by cell width. The slope-\\(+1\\) line stays in the marker-height band, misses horizontal features, and meets projected vertical walls at horizontal displacement \\(H_i\\) modulo \\(2H_i\\); the triangular wave sends these encounters into primary gaps. The central projection is wall-free, and both tails obey the residue identity. Successful copies have clear slabs.</p><p>Perturb finitely many cuts generically, preserving strict clearances and making copy time projections distinct. Add secondary gaps at preimages of transverse solid intersections, away from marks and corners. Each template works on a neighborhood of its probe pair; compactness gives a finite cover. Unite all secondary gaps, fix sufficiently small \\(\\sigma\\), round cuts to vertex lines, and apply Lemma 9.1’s finite median extraction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Theorem 9.3, “Holomorphic disk limit.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every locally continuous subsequential limit of \\(h_n\\) is holomorphic in the ordinary complex coordinate.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use a rational orthogonal polygon with four flat marks, balanced approximants, and observables \\(h_n\\) from Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every locally continuous subsequential limit of \\(h_n\\) is holomorphic in the ordinary complex coordinate. Consequently Proposition 6’s boundary identification and crossing integral hold without a holomorphicity premise for these polygons.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take simultaneous locally convergent subsequences \\(h_n\\to h\\), \\(J_n\\to J\\). Their compactness did not require holomorphicity. On each product lattice coset for step-two differences, multiply Proposition 9’s estimate by a compactly supported smooth test \\(\\varphi(z,u)\\), sum with normalized mesh weights, sum by parts twice, and divide by \\((2/n)^2\\). Local bounds give\n\\[\n\\int\\mathcal H_g(z,u)\n(\\partial_1+i\\partial_2)_z\\partial_{j,u}\\varphi(z,u)\n\\,d^2z\\,d^2u=o(1).\n\\]</p><p>Take limits in the prescribed order:\n\\[\nn\\to\\infty,\\quad \\sigma\\to0,\\quad h\\to0,\\quad R_0\\to\\infty,\n\\qquad L\\text{ fixed}.\n\\]\nProposition 8 closes secondary gaps and then primary gaps. Proposition 7 factors the complete wall. Their probe-independent scalars have bounded inverses at each fixed stage, so no scalar convergence is needed. Obtain\n\\[\n\\int h(z)J(u)\n(\\partial_1+i\\partial_2)_z\\partial_{j,u}\\varphi(z,u)\n\\,d^2z\\,d^2u=0.\n\\]</p><p>Fix eligible interior \\(z\\). Some generic exterior box has a nonzero distributional coordinate derivative of \\(J\\); otherwise both derivatives vanish off finitely many excluded lines, and continuity makes \\(J\\) constant on the connected strip, contradicting its boundary values. Choose a test \\(\\beta\\) with \\(\\langle\\partial_jJ,\\beta\\rangle\\ne0\\), and use \\(\\varphi(z,u)=\\alpha(z)\\beta(u)\\). Division gives\n\\[\n\\langle(\\partial_1+i\\partial_2)h,\\alpha\\rangle=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(h\\) is holomorphic off finitely many feature lines. Continuity and Morera, splitting triangles along those lines, remove them. Proposition 6 applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 10.1, “Jordan-quad transfer,” and Corollary 10.2, “The prescribed bond-percolation crossing.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The probability of an actual open path between the separately wired arcs tends to \\(g(\\chi)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), \\(d=\\sqrt q=2\\cos\\lambda\\), \\(\\rho=\\lambda/\\pi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  I_B(\\chi)=\\int_\\chi^1\n  u^{\\rho-1}(u-\\chi)^{\\rho-1}(1-u)^{1-3\\rho}\\,du,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  I_C(\\chi)=\\int_1^\\infty\n  u^{\\rho-1}(u-\\chi)^{\\rho-1}(u-1)^{1-3\\rho}\\,du,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  f(\\chi)=\\frac{I_C(\\chi)}{I_B(\\chi)+I_C(\\chi)},\n  \\qquad\n  g(\\chi)=\\frac{f(\\chi)}{f(\\chi)+d(1-f(\\chi))}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((D_n;a_n,b_n,c_n,d_n)\\) uniformly approximate a marked Jordan quadrilateral as in Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Keep every polygon-graph edge random and wire the closed arcs \\(a_nb_n\\) and \\(c_nd_n\\) separately, with no other primal identifications.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\chi=\\psi(b)\\), where \\(\\psi(a,c,d)=(0,1,\\infty)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The probability of an actual open path between the separately wired arcs tends to \\(g(\\chi)\\). At \\(q=1\\), the partition does not change the edge law, and the limit for the freely sampled crossing event of Theorem 2 is\n\\[\nf(\\chi)=\n\\frac{\\int_0^\\chi[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,dt}\n{\\int_0^1[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,dt}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 6 and 10 prove the formula for rational tile-orthogonal polygons. If the designated color is dual, translate the entire tile domain and caps one tile step. It then lies on the prescribed primal grid, with only \\(O(\\delta_n)\\) geometric displacement. Retain this tile test graph; it need not be the full graph of a primal polygon. Each primal edge belongs to one tile, absent incident edges attach only at its boundary, and every eligible electrode vertex belongs to its cap wire.</p><p>Use a plane Schoenflies homeomorphism taking the limiting quadrilateral to the square, electrodes left and right. Uniform marked convergence gives strict inset and outset containments. Pull back and rationally orthogonally polygonize\n\\[\n[\\varepsilon,1-\\varepsilon]\\times[-\\varepsilon,1+\\varepsilon]\n\\]\nas an easier test and\n\\[\n[-\\varepsilon,1+\\varepsilon]\\times[\\varepsilon,1-\\varepsilon]\n\\]\nas a harder test, with polygonization error below \\(\\varepsilon/10\\) and marks in flat sides. Such approximants are obtained by analytic Jordan approximation, polygonal approximation, and thin-corridor staircase replacements.</p><p>For the upper bound, restrict target and easier law to common primal edges. Nonshared target pieces can attach only to easier electrodes, not its distant free sides, and cannot join the two electrode zones externally. Thus the induced target partition is weaker than separate easier wiring. FK monotonicity couples easier common edges above target edges. Every actual target crossing contains a shared crossing between easier electrodes, by last exit and first entry into the two zones.</p><p>For the lower bound interchange roles with the harder quad. Nonshared harder pieces attach only to target electrodes and cannot link opposite sides externally. The target contains every prescribed lattice edge in its closure on the same grid, so its wires dominate the induced harder partition. Every harder crossing contains an actual shared crossing of target arcs.</p><p>Uniform Jordan convergence gives uniform local connectedness and marked conformal convergence of test quads. Their cross-ratios tend to \\(\\chi\\). First take mesh limits, then \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\), using continuity of \\(g\\).</p><p>At \\(q=1\\), \\(d=1\\), \\(\\rho=1/3\\), and separate wiring has no effect. The substitution \\(v=\\chi/u\\) in the four-change integral gives Cardy’s integral. All comparisons used actual paths; no external wire counts as a crossing.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Theorem 11.3, “Moving four-change test.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For arbitrary mesh-dependent switch-revealing stopping rules satisfying this localization, \\( \\mathbb P_{\\mathrm{open}}(E_{\\mathsf P}\\mid\\mathcal F_n) -f(\\chi_{U_n})\\longrightarrow0 \\) in probability on the localized …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), and use the four-open-change experiment and \\(f\\) of Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Reveal a switch in full at first encounter. A stopping time may occur partway through the already-revealed tile.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U_n\\) be the remaining simple slit disk with the tip as starting mark.</p><p class=\"li\">Localize to histories for which a fixed basepoint has a fixed positive disk in \\(U_n\\), and the four prime-end marks in its normalized disk chart remain mutually separated by a fixed positive amount.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_{\\mathsf P}\\) be the primal pairing event and \\(\\chi_{U_n}\\) the current ordered cross-ratio.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For arbitrary mesh-dependent switch-revealing stopping rules satisfying this localization,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_{\\mathrm{open}}(E_{\\mathsf P}\\mid\\mathcal F_n)\n-f(\\chi_{U_n})\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nin probability on the localized histories.</p>", "proof": "<p>Open caps and separate deterministic closure differ by a bounded density depending only on pairing, with conditional odds change\n\\[\np\\mapsto\\frac{p}{p+d(1-p)}.\n\\]\nIt suffices to prove a primal upper bound under separate closure; exchanging colors gives the complementary bound.</p><p>Use Lemma 12.6’s finite-list \\(H\\), with rectangle abscissas \\(u\\) in \\(U_n\\) and \\(v\\) in \\(H\\). Test the trimmed quad \\(a_*&lt;v&lt;1-a_*\\). Proposition 11 identifies its crossing, uniformly over the finite list, close to \\(g(\\chi_{U_n})\\) after trimming and kernel errors.</p><p>Align coordinates by finitely many abscissa bins. Their central vertical-fiber pieces match under kernel convergence. Their tails fit in matched small balls by length–area: for inverse rectangle map \\(\\Phi\\),\n\\[\n\\int_I\\operatorname{length}\\Phi(\\{x\\}\\times J)\\,dx\n\\le (|I||J|)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int_{I\\times J}|\\Phi'|^2\\,dA\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe area is uniformly bounded. Let deleted height fractions decrease slowly and choose levels controlling both charts’ tails. Charge their endpoint balls by Lemma 12.4.</p><p>Delete central uncertain edges failing containment in \\(H\\) with clearance, or whose endpoint abscissas disagree by more than \\(a_*/10\\). Such deletions have negligible attachment cost. Indeed, an actual electrode path avoiding flagged/protected/fiber-end balls enters the central band through a matched fiber, cannot leave \\(H\\) by candidate clearance, and cannot develop chart drift without crossing a fiber in a mismatched bin.</p><p>On surviving common edges, nonshared pieces attach only on designated test sides, not free sides, and cannot connect the two sides externally. History identifications lie in separate groups near \\(u=0,1\\). The original induced partition is dominated by separate test wiring; every modified original crossing contains a shared test crossing. FK monotonicity gives the upper bound.</p><p>Choose trims, chart precision, excursion and block cutoffs, protection radii, \\(R_1\\), and finally \\(\\ell\\), then the mesh. If convergence in probability failed, select failing histories outside all small exceptional sets and take a kernel subsequence; matched fibers and conditional deletion estimates contradict failure. Six-arm summation was under the original law before this selection. Convert through the pairing tilt to obtain \\(f\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 11.1, “A compatible simple drawing.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They admit a tile-and-collar representation in a Jordan polygon \\(V_n\\) with the same marked uniform limit, a simple rounded strand at curve distance \\(O(\\delta_n)\\) preserving all ordered pieces, and a simple slit disk …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the original polygon graph and its Dobrushin strand, with the boundary convention of Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They admit a tile-and-collar representation in a Jordan polygon \\(V_n\\) with the same marked uniform limit, a simple rounded strand at curve distance \\(O(\\delta_n)\\) preserving all ordered pieces, and a simple slit disk at every revealing stop. Unrevealed edges have the original conditional FK law, with known open edges contracted and known closed edges deleted. Primal and dual prefix banks physically access corresponding known edges and wire vertices within \\(O(\\delta_n)\\), and actual completion paths embed without crossing the past. The construction can be unchanged away from an interval carrying additional boundary changes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Make one tile for each edge, including boundary edges. Glue triangles toward each interior square center. Give each exterior boundary triangle its own tab and outer dual vertex; do not glue distinct exterior tabs along radial sides. Flatten tabs and add a thinner Jordan collar, all within \\(O(\\delta_n)\\) of the original boundary.</p><p>Draw switches and caps as disjoint arcs, with local wires behind caps. At a wired boundary edge, its outer dual cap makes either switch state equivalent on inside ports; declaring that edge open substitutes only a microscopic detour and possibly removes a microscopic loop. On a free side, caps around primal vertices give the complementary dual-wire turn. Matching ordered pieces gives the \\(O(\\delta_n)\\) curve bound, including repeated medial occurrences.</p><p>The Euler loop identity gives the conditional FK law. At an open queried tile the bank uses its actual diagonal; at a closed turn it remains at one corner. Caps stay at a vertex or its wire interval. Thus bank accesses are physical and local. At partial-tile stops the full tile state is already known, so continuing its arc reveals nothing further. Projection to the physical color grid moves points by \\(O(\\delta_n)\\), duplicates no edge, and may identify virtual copies of a vertex.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Lemma 11.2, “Small-interval behavior.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( f(1-\\chi)=1-f(\\chi), \\qquad f(\\chi)=c_q\\chi^h(1+o(1)) \\quad(\\chi\\downarrow0), \\qquad c_q>0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(f\\) from Proposition 11 and put \\(h=1-2\\rho\\in[1/3,1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nf(1-\\chi)=1-f(\\chi),\n\\qquad\nf(\\chi)=c_q\\chi^h(1+o(1))\n\\quad(\\chi\\downarrow0),\n\\qquad c_q&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The substitutions \\(u^{-1}\\) in \\(I_C\\) and \\(u=1-(1-\\chi)t\\) in \\(I_B\\), followed by Euler transformation, give\n\\[\nI_B(\\chi)=\\left(\\frac{1-\\chi}{\\chi}\\right)^hI_C(1-\\chi).\n\\]\nBoth \\(I_C(0)\\) and \\(I_C(1-)\\) are finite and positive: endpoint exponents are \\(-2\\rho&gt;-1\\) and \\(-1-\\rho&lt;-1\\) at infinity. Substitution into \\(f\\) gives the claims, with\n\\(c_q=I_C(0)/I_C(1-)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "Lemma 11.4, “Physical paths and chart oscillation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The oscillation of \\(u_n\\) on a connected path of vanishing physical diameter, including prime-boundary accesses, has a common modulus tending to zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use separate deterministic primal closure in the localized remaining disk.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_n\\) be its rectangle abscissa, with primal electrodes at \\(0,1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A possible primal path opens every still unknown primal edge but uses no identification as an edge.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The oscillation of \\(u_n\\) on a connected path of vanishing physical diameter, including prime-boundary accesses, has a common modulus tending to zero. Thus a possible path starting in \\(\\beta\\le u_n\\le1-\\beta\\) cannot reach a known primal-open edge or electrode inside a sufficiently small physical ball.</p>", "proof": "<p>Away from the basepoint, Brownian motion from it hits a connected set in a ball of radius \\(r\\) before exit with probability tending uniformly to zero, by comparison with a fixed enclosing annulus. In the normalized disk a connected set of fixed positive diameter has hitting probability bounded below, using a crosscut and Beurling. Rectangle maps form a compact family because marks remain separated. Hence a small physical continuum cannot have macroscopic rectangle oscillation. Near the basepoint use distortion, and approximate boundary accesses from the interior.</p><p>Known open edges and wire attachments access abscissa \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) within \\(O(\\delta_n)\\) by Lemma 12.1. A short possible central connection would contradict the modulus.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.4", "orig": "Lemma 11.5, “Deletion and protected-ball costs.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The change in actual crossing probability is at most the undeleted conditional probability that an endpoint of a deleted edge reaches either electrode or its revealed primal bank by an actual open path, allowing one …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a history with separate deterministic primal closure.</p><p class=\"li\">Delete a set of still random edges.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The change in actual crossing probability is at most the undeleted conditional probability that an endpoint of a deleted edge reaches either electrode or its revealed primal bank by an actual open path, allowing one initial test step over the deleted edge. Contact at a wire vertex may have length zero.</p><p>For a central-band deletion set inside a ball of radius \\(r\\), this cost tends uniformly to zero as \\(r\\downarrow0\\), with fixed required outer radius and sufficiently fine mesh. The same holds for a fixed finite number of balls.</p>", "proof": "<p>Couple deleted and original laws monotonically. Explore actual open clusters from deletion endpoints in the stronger law, querying corresponding edges in both laws. Partition monotonicity persists. Once closed cuts enclose those clusters, the outer conditional laws agree and can be coupled identically. If no explored cluster reaches an electrode, the crossing decision agrees.</p><p>Choose the outer radius using Lemma 12.3. Relevant possible components have no accessible wire or known-open attachment inside that ball; uncertain connections occur only at its rim. Project to the physical lattice, fill missing edges, and wire the rim. Splitting vertex copies only weakens the original partition. The full-lattice wired-rim law dominates the relevant arm. Buffered dual circuits make its probability from radius \\(r\\) to fixed outer radius vanish. Use a finite union bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.5", "orig": "Lemma 11.6, “Flagged cells and their cost.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If a nearby candidate actually attaches to an electrode, then six alternating actual arms occur in a cropped annulus \\(200\\ell\\) to \\(R_1/2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In a fixed bulk block let \\(\\pi\\) be a simple known primal bank path.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\ell\\ll R_1\\), away from its endpoints and the original boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">A candidate is a central-band vertex with a possible path to an electrode.</p><p class=\"li\">Flag an \\(\\ell\\)-cell if the prefix already supplies four alternating actual arms in its annulus \\(100\\ell\\) to \\(R_1\\), with its two primal arms known wired bank paths.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If a nearby candidate actually attaches to an electrode, then six alternating actual arms occur in a cropped annulus \\(200\\ell\\) to \\(R_1/2\\). Summed over a bounded deterministic grid region, the averaged conditional attachment cost is\n\\[\nC\\ell^{-2}(\\ell/R_1)^{2+c_0}=O_{R_1}(\\ell^{c_0}),\n\\qquad c_0&gt;0.\n\\]\nOne initial test step and bounded open-cap changes are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 12.3 forbids the extra primal arm from bridging laterally to known primal legs in the wider annulus, even in the optimistic completion. In intervening strips, planar crossing alternatives supply dual radial separators. Together with the old dual separator, this gives three primal and three dual alternating arms after cropping.</p><p>Thicken paths into tile territories. Absence of an optimistic primal transversal forces a known-closed dual territorial separator, shadowing actual physical edges. Thus no auxiliary partition creates these arms.</p><p>Use the original finite-law six-arm estimate\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(A_6(r,R))\\le C(r/R)^{2+c_0},\n\\]\ntransferred from the infinite-plane result by a bulk collar with fixed outer scale. Sum over all \\(O(\\ell^{-2})\\) deterministic cells before selecting histories. Bounded cap densities preserve the averaged estimate. Markov then makes conditional cost small outside a small-probability set of histories. No arbitrary-maze conditional six-arm bound is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.6", "orig": "Lemma 11.7, “Directed obstacle.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After discarding histories of arbitrarily small probability and allowing arbitrarily small conditional deletion cost, a Jordan polygon \\(H\\) can be chosen from a finite list at each fixed accuracy so that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use localized histories, central candidates, protected balls, and flagged cells from Lemmas 12.3–12.5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After discarding histories of arbitrarily small probability and allowing arbitrarily small conditional deletion cost, a Jordan polygon \\(H\\) can be chosen from a finite list at each fixed accuracy so that:</p><p class=\"li\">its boundary follows the original boundary and prefix to that accuracy;</p><p class=\"li\">outside finitely many protected balls and flagged cells it has positive clearance from all central candidates;</p><p class=\"li\">as accuracies improve, \\(H\\) and \\(U_n\\) have the same pointed kernel limits;</p><p class=\"li\">artificial marks in \\(H\\) have disk angles arbitrarily close to those in \\(U_n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Retain prefix excursions reaching depth \\(r\\) from the limiting boundary, including complete segments between returns to depth \\(h_b\\ll r\\) and the last incomplete segment. Their number is tight by a finite bulk annulus cover. Bound that number, then choose \\(h_b\\). Join boundary-near endpoints to the exterior through protected \\(O(h_b)\\)-balls, charged by Lemma 12.4.</p><p>Break retained excursions into short bulk blocks of diameter \\(\\varepsilon\\ll h_b\\), with tight block number. Snap endpoints to a finer deterministic grid and protect them by radius \\(e\\ll\\varepsilon\\). Loop erase the known bank path to a simple \\(\\pi\\). Choose \\(\\ell\\ll R_1\\ll e\\). Forbid closed \\(\\ell\\)-cells within \\(3\\ell\\) of candidates, except flagged or protected cells.</p><p>A connector avoiding forbidden cells exists. Otherwise cell separation gives a circuit separating protected endpoints. Choose candidate witnesses in successive cells. They can be joined avoiding \\(\\pi\\) in \\(O(R_1)\\)-balls: failure would give a proper crosscut of \\(\\pi\\) separating two witness arms; optimistic no-bridge tests then produce four old alternating arms and flag the cell, a contradiction. Push the separating circuit through witnesses and these avoiding joins, preserving its winding distinction. It becomes disjoint from \\(\\pi\\), impossible.</p><p>Choose connectors for all finite patterns. Their union with exterior attachments is an exterior-attached obstacle \\(K\\). The basepoint complement is simply connected, possibly with slits and pinches. An interior radial Riemann-map approximation and polygonal perturbation give a Jordan domain \\(H\\) within \\(\\ell/10\\) of obstacle or outer wall, preserving candidate clearance. There are finitely many choices at fixed scales and count cutoffs.</p><p>On Hausdorff subsequences of boundary and prefix, every compact path in the limiting basepoint component eventually belongs to both domains, and complements approach every limiting boundary point. Thus pointed kernels and normalized interior charts agree. Transfer marks by finite-grid disk-angle approximations. No local connectedness of the limiting slit boundary is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 12.2, “Driving convergence.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On every compact capacity interval, \\( W^{(n)}\\Longrightarrow\\sqrt\\kappa B \\) uniformly in law, where \\( \\kappa=\\frac8{1+h} =\\frac{4\\pi}{\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), \\(\\rho=\\lambda/\\pi\\), \\(h=1-2\\rho\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Map the compatible Jordan drawing \\(V_n\\) to \\(\\mathbb H\\), with Dobrushin endpoints \\(0,\\infty\\), positive real side primal-free, and convergent interior normalization.</p><p class=\"li\">Its simple rounded strand has chordal maps and driver\n  \\[\n  \\partial_tg_t(z)=\\frac2{g_t(z)-W_t},\\quad g_0(z)=z,\\quad W_0=0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On every compact capacity interval,\n\\[\nW^{(n)}\\Longrightarrow\\sqrt\\kappa B\n\\]\nuniformly in law, where\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\frac8{1+h}\n=\\frac{4\\pi}{\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Localize as in Lemma 13.1. Put\n\\[\nX_t(x)=g_t(x),\\qquad A_t(x)=x^hg_t'(x)^h,\\qquad\nM_t(x)=A_t(x)(X_t(x)-W_t)^{-h}.\n\\]\nThe \\(X,A\\) are uniformly bounded and Lipschitz, with \\(A\\) bounded below. Two solutions \\(x_1&lt;x_2\\) remain separated because their gap solves\n\\[\n\\partial_t(X_t(x_2)-X_t(x_1))\n=-\\frac{2(X_t(x_2)-X_t(x_1))}\n{(X_t(x_1)-W_t)(X_t(x_2)-W_t)}.\n\\]</p><p>Freeze \\(X,A\\) at a stop \\(\\sigma\\), writing \\(F_j(w)=A_\\sigma(x_j)(X_\\sigma(x_j)-w)^{-h}\\). The derivative ratio \\(F_1'/F_2'\\) has uniformly positive derivative. For\n\\(a_\\sigma=F_1'(W_\\sigma)/F_2'(W_\\sigma)\\),\n\\[\nF_1(w)-F_1(W_\\sigma)\n-a_\\sigma(F_2(w)-F_2(W_\\sigma))\n\\ge c(w-W_\\sigma)^2.\n\\]\nFreezing costs \\(C(\\tau-\\sigma)\\). Approximate martingale identities give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(W_\\tau-W_\\sigma)^2\n\\le C\\mathbb E(\\tau-\\sigma)+o(1).\n\\]\nStopping-time tightness gives continuous limits.</p><p>At inner continuity localization levels, ODE continuity passes the \\(M(x)\\) martingale identities. Since\n\\[\nW=X-(A/M)^{1/h},\n\\]\n\\(W\\) is a continuous semimartingale. Write its finite-variation part \\(B^{\\mathrm{fv}}\\), and set \\(Z=X-W\\). Then\n\\(dX=2Z^{-1}dt\\), \\(d\\log A=-2hZ^{-2}dt\\). Itô and the martingale property yield\n\\[\n(X-W)\\,dB^{\\mathrm{fv}}-4\\,dt+\\frac{1+h}{2}\\,d[W]=0.\n\\]\nSubtract for \\(x_1,x_2\\). Their positive separation gives \\(dB^{\\mathrm{fv}}=0\\), and\n\\(d[W]=8(1+h)^{-1}dt\\). Lévy identifies Brownian motion. Since\n\\(\\arccos(-d/2)=\\pi-\\lambda\\), the parameter is the asserted \\(\\kappa\\). Brownian exit bounds remove localization.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 12.1, “Bounded martingale tests.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These processes and their reciprocals are uniformly bounded on the stopped range.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Stop at time \\(T&lt;\\infty\\) or first exit from \\(|W_t|&lt;R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(x&gt;2R+1\\), and define\n  \\[\n  M_t(x)=\\left(\\frac{xg_t'(x)}{g_t(x)-W_t}\\right)^h.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These processes and their reciprocals are uniformly bounded on the stopped range. For arbitrary stopping rules \\(\\sigma_n\\le\\tau_n\\) there and uniformly bounded \\(\\mathcal F_{\\sigma_n}\\)-measurable tests \\(F_n\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[F_n(M_{\\tau_n}(x)-M_{\\sigma_n}(x))]\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nunder the original Dobrushin law.</p>", "proof": "<p>Insert a small separately wired primal interval with half-plane endpoints \\(x,x+s\\) and use four open changes. Its current cross-ratio is\n\\[\n\\chi_t=\\frac{g_t(x+s)-g_t(x)}{g_t(x+s)-W_t}.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(s\\), all marks remain separated. A sufficiently high point \\(iM\\) supplies an unaffected physical basepoint disk. The conditional pairing probability \\(Z_t\\) is an exact bounded martingale, and Proposition 12 gives \\(Z_t-f(\\chi_t)\\to0\\) in mean at every allowed stop.</p><p>At fixed \\(s\\), divide by \\(f(s/(x+s))\\). Lemma 12.2 and ODE estimates give, uniformly,\n\\[\n\\frac{f(\\chi_t)}{f(s/(x+s))}\n=M_t(x)+o_s(1).\n\\]\nThe right side and its reciprocal are bounded independently of small \\(s\\), since the real solutions stay separated from the driver and\n\\[\ng_t'(x)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\int_0^t\\frac2{(g_u(x)-W_u)^2}\\,du\\right).\n\\]</p><p>Only after replacing the normalized probabilities by these bounded expressions compare laws. Couple separate four-change closure to Dobrushin by exploring clusters from the small added interval in the stronger law. Their probability of leaving a fixed neighborhood vanishes as \\(s\\downarrow0\\), by wired-rim arm domination and dual circuits; outside it configurations agree. Open and deterministic four-change laws also have vanishing total variation distance, since their density depends only on a pairing of probability \\(g(s/(x+s))\\to0\\). ODE bounds keep the stopped strand away from the insertion neighborhood. Apply identical history stopping rules in the coupled experiments. No coupling error is divided by the vanishing initial probability.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Theorem 12.9, “Dobrushin convergence for \\(1\\le q<4\\).”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The strands converge along the full sequence in the Euclidean oriented-curve topology modulo increasing reparametrization to chordal \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_{\\kappa(q)}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the marked Jordan approximations, boundary convention, and ordered medial strands of Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The strands converge along the full sequence in the Euclidean oriented-curve topology modulo increasing reparametrization to chordal \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_{\\kappa(q)}\\). The conclusion includes the complete ordered traversal, not merely the driver or hulls.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition 13 and Lemma 14.2 to couple a subsequence with uniform physical curve convergence and compact driving convergence. Write \\(Y(t)\\) for the physical Brownian \\(\\mathrm{SLE}\\) trace and \\(\\gamma(s)\\) for the full limit. Before first target visit, let \\(c(s)\\) be initial-segment hull capacity. Kernel convergence makes it continuous, nondecreasing, and identifies the accessible remaining domain with that of \\(Y\\) at time \\(c(s)\\). As the target is approached, \\(c(s)\\to\\infty\\), since bounded-driver finite-time hulls remain bounded in the initial half-plane.</p><p>Whenever \\(c\\) changes, newly added boundaries in the shared accessible domain contain common trace points. Shrinking such intervals and using continuity give\n\\[\n\\gamma(s)=Y(c(s))\n\\]\noutside plateau interiors and at their active endpoints.</p><p>A nonconstant plateau is an extra excursion based at \\(Y(t)\\), lying in the old hull or original boundary. Lemma 14.6 excludes open swallowed components. Lemma 14.2 excludes a varying interval entirely on the boundary. A varying interior subarc away from its basepoint must therefore lie on the old trace. Every point there has a unique old time: two old visits plus the extra visit would violate the no-triple-visit conclusion. The old-time inverse is continuous by compactness and uniqueness. Its composition with the varying subarc sweeps a nontrivial time interval, contradicting Lemma 14.5’s double visit in every interval. Thus all plateaus are constant, including at capacity zero.</p><p>Lemma 14.4 excludes post-target extra motion. The capacity change of time identifies the entire ordered limit. Lemma 12.1 transfers from the rounded drawing back to the prescribed medial strand, preserving repeated midpoint occurrences. All subsequences agree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 12.3, “Territorial arm counting.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They force at least \\(m\\) alternating actual primal/dual arms in a further cropped annulus.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>In a buffered bulk annulus, take \\(m\\) disjoint radial medial sublegs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They force at least \\(m\\) alternating actual primal/dual arms in a further cropped annulus. If only one color is kept, there is a crossing in a distinct intervening strip per two assignments of that color to successive separators.</p>", "proof": "<p>Rounded medial arcs divide tiles into primal and dual territories. A territorial path shadows an actual path of its color: crossing between opposite corners uses exactly the diagonal supplied by the switch, with one-tile error.</p><p>Perturb rims within buffers. Thin crosscuts on both sides of ordered medial separators give opposite-color territorial crossings. Compress successive equal-color runs; every separator creates a transition, leaving at least \\(m\\) alternating crossings. Shadow in the central crop, retaining disjoint territorial tails until splicing. Same-color arms lie in strips separated by opposite-color paths and cannot meet. Last-inner-to-first-outer cropping preserves order. Ignoring one color proves the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma 12.4, “Curve compactness and no triple visits.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They are tight in uniform Euclidean curve topology modulo increasing reparametrization.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the physical Dobrushin strands in uniformly approximated Jordan domains.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They are tight in uniform Euclidean curve topology modulo increasing reparametrization. Every limit has no nonconstant interval on the original boundary and no interior point with three visits separated by nonconstant excursions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Bulk passage counts in a fixed annulus cover are tight. Boundary-confined macroscopic displacement is unlikely: in Schoenflies coordinates it crosses one of finitely many homogeneous boundary subarcs. Its nonwired-color bank is an actual path. Place it in a thin Jordan ribbon, fill that color’s lattice, and wire the ribbon boundary to dominate the crossing. Choose any \\(N\\) disjoint transverse blocker quads with strict margins. Their extremal distances are bounded, and up-to-boundary RSW gives conditional blocker probability at least \\(c\\). Thus longitudinal crossing probability is at most \\((1-c)^N\\). Let \\(N\\) grow, then narrow the ribbon.</p><p>Outside such boundary travel, excess successive displacements force many passages through a finite bulk annulus cover. Their tight counts give the usual reparametrized Arzelà–Ascoli compactness, and also rule out a varying boundary interval.</p><p>Three genuine interior visits give six medial legs to a fixed outer scale. Lemma 14.1 gives six alternating actual arms. A grid of scale \\(\\ell\\) and the exponent \\(2+c_0\\) six-arm estimate give \\(O(\\ell^{c_0})\\). Take mesh first, then \\(\\ell\\downarrow0\\), then countable interior and excursion cutoffs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "Lemma 12.5, “Macroscopic avoidability.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The conditional probability of a future radial traversal in any avoidable component tends uniformly to zero as \\(r/R\\downarrow0\\), with \\(r,R\\) fixed before the mesh limit and \\(\\delta_n\\ll r\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>At a revealing stop, call a component of \\(U_n\\cap A(z;r,R)\\) avoidable if it does not separate the tip access from the target access.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The conditional probability of a future radial traversal in any avoidable component tends uniformly to zero as \\(r/R\\downarrow0\\), with \\(r,R\\) fixed before the mesh limit and \\(\\delta_n\\ll r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>An avoidable component cannot access both designated primal and dual boundary arcs between the rims: a joining path would separate tip from target. Choose a color whose designated boundary is absent. A future strand traversal supplies an actual arm of that color’s unqueried edges. A known edge or wire jump would locally access the missing designated boundary, a contradiction.</p><p>Keep all relevant edges of this color in a cropped annulus. Away from rims, induced attachments are only ordinary physical-vertex attachments, using Lemma 12.1’s local accesses. Delete known opposite-state edges, fill to the full lattice, and wire both rims. This dominates all relevant arms simultaneously. Buffered dual circuits give a vanishing bound. Sum over the two possible missing colors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.4", "orig": "Corollary 12.6, “No return from a small target cap.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After first entrance into such a cap, the probability of a later fixed positive physical displacement tends to zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use shrinking initial conformal caps around the Dobrushin target.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After first entrance into such a cap, the probability of a later fixed positive physical displacement tends to zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>At first entrance, the untouched cap gives a tip-to-target route in a small physical ball. Stop just before entrance, or use the entering bank with one-step buffer. Outside that ball, all relevant annular components are avoidable; apply Lemma 14.3. Initial inverse disk maps converge uniformly to the Jordan boundary, so cap diameters shrink uniformly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.5", "orig": "Lemma 12.7, “Interior double visits in every time interval.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Almost surely every nonempty open capacity interval contains both visits to an interior self-double point.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(Y\\) be ordinary chordal \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa\\), \\(4&lt;\\kappa&lt;8\\), parametrized by capacity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Almost surely every nonempty open capacity interval contains both visits to an interior self-double point.</p>", "proof": "<p>The double-point and boundary-hit dimensions are\n\\[\n1+\\kappa/8-6/\\kappa,\\qquad 2-8/\\kappa,\n\\]\nwhose difference is \\((\\kappa-4)^2/(8\\kappa)&gt;0\\). Hence an interior double point exists almost surely. If \\(E_T\\) means both visits occur before \\(T\\), scaling makes \\(\\mathbb P(E_T)\\) independent of \\(T&gt;0\\); their union over finite \\(T\\) has probability one, so each has probability one. Restart at deterministic times by domain Markov and map interior double points back into the remaining domain. Take all rational intervals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.6", "orig": "Lemma 12.8, “Small gates exclude swallowed excursions.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The physical curve cannot enter an open component of the Loewner hull disjoint from its \\(\\mathrm{SLE}\\) trace and the original boundary.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Take a joint subsequential limit of the physical strand and its driver, whose latter law is identified by Proposition 13.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The physical curve cannot enter an open component of the Loewner hull disjoint from its \\(\\mathrm{SLE}\\) trace and the original boundary.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose otherwise and choose a closed ball \\(J\\), with a larger buffered neighborhood disjoint from trace and original boundary, inside an allegedly visited swallowed component at bounded capacity and driver. Before the buffered hit, the discrete prefix stays a fixed distance from \\(J\\), but its basepoint kernel excludes \\(J\\).</p><p>For normalized inverse disk maps \\(\\Phi_n\\), put \\(z_n=\\Phi_n^{-1}(\\operatorname{center}J)\\). Then \\(|z_n|\\to1\\). With \\(\\varepsilon_n=1-|z_n|\\), consider disk crosscuts centered at \\(z_n/|z_n|\\), of radii \\(2\\varepsilon_n\\) to \\(\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_n}\\). Their image lengths satisfy\n\\[\n\\int_{2\\varepsilon_n}^{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_n}}\nL_n(t)^2\\,\\frac{dt}{t}\n\\le2\\pi\\,\\operatorname{area}(U_n).\n\\]\nThus one crosscut has diameter tending to zero and separates all of \\(J\\) from the basepoint. The target is on the basepoint side, by a high-half-plane route avoiding bounded hulls. A vanishing outer-wall gate cannot separate fixed interior balls under uniform Jordan convergence.</p><p>For fixed small \\(\\ell\\), stop at the first history admitting a gate of diameter at most \\(\\ell\\) separating \\(J\\) from basepoint and target, while preserving spare distance from \\(J\\). At first occurrence a gate endpoint lies on the latest microscopic step; otherwise the gate and separation already existed. The tip is consequently within \\(\\ell+O(\\delta_n)\\) of its target-side bank.</p><p>In an annulus from \\(4\\ell\\) to fixed outer radius, trapped-side components are avoidable because the target route crosses the gate inside the inner rim. A later hit of \\(J\\) requires traversal there. Lemma 14.3 makes its probability vanish as \\(\\ell\\downarrow0\\), without summing over gate locations.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 13.4, “Tests away from coincidence.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(x>0\\) beyond the localization bounds, \\( M_t(x)=\\left(\\frac{xg_t'(x)}{g_t(x)-W_t^{\\mathbb H}}\\right)^h, \\qquad h=1-2\\rho, \\) satisfies approximate martingale identities between arbitrary causal stops.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), \\(4&lt;\\kappa=\\kappa(q)\\le6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Start in a square whose boundary primal edges are deterministically open. Homogeneous walls consist of actual open edges of their color and deterministic inward half-tile triangles.</p><p class=\"li\">A target branch is obtained by opening one wall triangle and following switches, cutting off homogeneous pockets. Retain the target component; at a pocket switch open the next old-wall triangle in counterclockwise order for primal and clockwise order for dual.</p><p class=\"li\">In an active component the counterclockwise boundary consists of the tip side, an actual dual wall, the force side, and an actual primal wall.</p><p class=\"li\">Use radial time\n  \\[\n  t=\\log\\frac{\\operatorname{crad}(U_0,z)}\n  {\\operatorname{crad}(U_t,z)},\n  \\]\n  with normalized disk map \\(\\Phi_t(z)=0\\). Let tip and force prepoints be \\(e^{iW_t},e^{iO_t}\\), with \\(\\theta_t=O_t-W_t\\in[0,2\\pi]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At a causal completed state with \\(\\theta\\) in a compact interior subinterval, map the active domain to \\(\\mathbb H\\), tip \\(0\\), force \\(\\infty\\), target imaginary part one, and positive real side dual-designated. Stop a short subsequent segment at fixed driver and chordal-capacity bounds before resets or loss of target/force access.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(x&gt;0\\) beyond the localization bounds,\n\\[\nM_t(x)=\\left(\\frac{xg_t'(x)}{g_t(x)-W_t^{\\mathbb H}}\\right)^h,\n\\qquad h=1-2\\rho,\n\\]\nsatisfies approximate martingale identities between arbitrary causal stops. Tests may include the full earlier history of finitely many targets. Errors vanish after the mesh limit in averaged history law, not uniformly over deterministic mazes.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed small chart scale \\(s\\), cut a notch near \\(x\\), with dual flanks and a separately wired primal middle interval away from its ends. Approximate by deterministic openable diagonals and transition sides in unqueried territory. Length–area chooses notch terminal tails with vanishing physical diameter, while compact interior parts have positive clearance.</p><p>Conditionally at segment start, sample the notched experiment. Its exact pairing probability is a martingale; the complementary comparison uses the bounded pairing tilt. The local segment cannot reach or swallow notch data by chordal ODE bounds. Homogeneous discarded pockets contain no notch or force data. Other targets retain original conditional independence by Lemma 15.1, and identities may be stopped at frozen clocks.</p><p>For fixed \\(s\\), Lemma 15.3 compares exterior histories by bounded density. For vanishing coupling error as \\(s\\downarrow0\\), bracket notch partitions, expose stronger-law attachment clusters, and block them by dual crossings in dyadic chart half-collars, away from the old primal wire. This last coupling uses separate primal closure, not its pairing tilt.</p><p>Apply Proposition 12’s moving-domain comparison with additional obstacles. Truncate branch and generation counts by Lemma 15.4. In each generation retain excursions deep from the initial boundary and earlier obstacles, then short bulk blocks. Their two actual banks give known color paths. Choose accuracies backward through generations so earlier obstacles are much more precise than descendant endpoint protections. Route the notch on its removed side, place physical tails in protected balls, and use finite lists after all cutoffs. The directed-obstacle and matched-fiber arguments then apply.</p><p>The flag estimate must remain an original six-arm estimate. Test optimistic bridges in the unnotched ambient completion. A path leaving the old domain first meets its designated actual wall, and opposite-color diagonals block it. Chart modulus excludes local central shortcuts to known edges. Near the notch, primal central paths remain near the old dual-wall patch, away from old primal history. Dual central vertices relevant to flags stay in an interior patch beside the notch’s primal interval, physically separated from history. Thus territorial separators are original known edges.</p><p>For dual extra arms, collar comparison transfers narrow pieces outside the notch collar to the original law. For primal arms, restore the notch and wire only new middle primal vertices, which dominates the modified law; a vacant bulk collar separates this wiring from flagged local arms. Bounded-density comparison again transfers to the original conditional law. Summing deterministic cells therefore costs a bounded multiple of the original annealed six-arm sum. Protected balls are controlled directly conditionally.</p><p>Consequently the stopped pairing probability \\(Z_t^{(s)}\\) differs in mean from \\(g(\\chi_t^{(s)})\\) at fixed \\(s\\). Divide the exact identity by its positive segment-start value only at fixed \\(s\\). Analyticity near the small notch, bounded derivatives, rescaled Jordan shape convergence, and \\(g(\\chi)\\sim c\\chi^h\\) yield\n\\[\ng(\\chi_t^{(s)})/g(\\chi_0^{(s)})=M_t(x)+o_s(1)\n\\]\nuniformly on the stopped segment. Replace by this bounded ratio before returning to the original law by the vanishing coupling error. No such error is divided by a vanishing probability.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 13.1, “Exact pocket law.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conditional on finite exploration history, unqueried switches in remaining components have the positive cap laws of their boundaries, and distinct components are conditionally independent.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the actual-wall peeling rule of Proposition 15, processing finitely many targets in fixed priority order and retaining all cut-off components.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Conditional on finite exploration history, unqueried switches in remaining components have the positive cap laws of their boundaries, and distinct components are conditionally independent. Targets have identical explorations before their components split. Between pocket switches, the strand is part of one original closed loop.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a cut, each matching connection is either the consumed switch pair or a local cap on one side. Loop factors and the eventual current-strand closure factor therefore have no remaining dependence involving both components. They factor. A two-change component has one open strand; external closure gives its cap law. Deterministic triangles implement exactly these identifications, hiding no switch.</p><p>Until entering a homogeneous pocket, traversed pairs belong to the loop through the opened cap. At the switch that loop stays on the other side of the actual diagonal; the new cap belongs to an untraced loop in the pocket. Fixed-priority processing preserves the factorization and common ancestry.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Individual update capacities, boundary-angle jumps, and within-update angular oscillations vanish uniformly on bounded horizons.", "statement": "<p>Radial clocks and resets, Section 13.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use bounded radial horizons and the interpolation prescribed in Section 13.2: grow the actual diagonal and triangle-clipping legs; boundary relocations take zero growing time; at a primal pocket \\(\\theta=0\\), advance the force access to the next counterclockwise old-wall edge, and reverse at a dual pocket \\(\\theta=2\\pi\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Individual update capacities, boundary-angle jumps, and within-update angular oscillations vanish uniformly on bounded horizons. Between designated resets,\n\\[\ndO_t=\\cot(\\theta_t/2)\\,dt.\n\\]\nAt a collision retaining the force side, continuation uses the access beside that side, with finite one-sided improper integral and no extra forcepoint jump.</p>", "proof": "<p>Koebe gives a fixed positive target ball on every bounded horizon. The chart modulus from Proposition 12 applies to short physical paths and their accesses. Every update lies in boundedly many adjacent tiles, so its chart footprint is small; kernel convergence makes its capacity small.</p><p>In its starting chart, the update is confined to a vanishing sector. New normalized maps converge on interior compact sets and, by reflection, on boundary arcs away from that sector. The new tip lies in the small sector; the force access is either regular or in the same collapsing sector. Thus whole-update oscillations vanish.</p><p>The usual radial boundary equation gives \\(dO=\\cot(\\theta/2)dt\\). At a collision truncate the retained access by short circular gates; convergence in that sector gives the same one-sided equation afterward. The improper motion has one sign and a finite angular limit, so it is finite and introduces no new jump.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.3", "orig": "Lemma 13.2, “Wall crossings and screening.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "An actual crossing of either specified color \\(X\\) has uniformly positive conditional probability over exploration histories and partitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In a normalized disk chart, fix a buffered bounded-aspect corridor with terminal intervals strictly inside homogeneous wall arcs; one terminal interval may be an interior bar.</p><p class=\"li\">Work after the mesh limit at this fixed chart scale.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>An actual crossing of either specified color \\(X\\) has uniformly positive conditional probability over exploration histories and partitions. At an \\(X\\)-wall it reaches an actual wall vertex; at an opposite wall it reaches the \\(X\\)-corner of an inward triangle, and can be completed to the wall midpoint by a triangle segment meeting an actual \\(X\\)-path only at its start.</p><p>The bounded-density collar comparison also holds for buffered homogeneous-wall chart collars with boundedly many through-wire blocks.</p>", "proof": "<p>Retain the basepoint edge-connected component of square faces at distance more than \\(k\\delta\\), \\(k=20\\), from the complement. Failing faces are edge-connected by rasterized complement paths; assign other passing islands to the exterior. Both face families are edge-connected, excluding alternating interior/exterior faces at a pinch, so the retained boundary is simple. It eventually contains every compact chart core. Every exterior neighbor is originally failing and has an \\(O(k)\\)-step access to the actual wall. Chart modulus makes the perimeter approach the unit circle with correct cyclic accesses.</p><p>Approximate longitudinal sides by color-grid crosscuts, first on compact interior portions and then in separated terminal neighborhoods. Loop erase. These crosscuts and retained perimeter arcs bound a simple lattice quad. Leave a narrower positive-width smooth family of reference curves clear. Crop them at first perimeter or bar hits. For any density transferred to the chart and extended by zero, Fubini and Cauchy–Schwarz bound one cropped weighted length by a constant times its \\(L^2\\)-area. Hence continuum extremal distance is bounded, exactly as required by the square-FK up-to-boundary theorem.</p><p>Do not query terminal perimeter edges. From a reached vertex, extend over \\(O(k)\\) fresh edges along the failing-face access; finite energy gives positive cost. Stop at first wall contact, or at the specified-color corner of an opposite-wall triangle. Add the prescribed triangle segment.</p><p>For collars, select the screen nearest the unknown side by minimizing its unknown-side area, using pruned paths and ordered spokes. Any closer open excursion would replace part of the path, so selection reads only that side. The other side retains an FK law. Actual same-color crossings attach to the screen; opposite-color paths cannot bypass it except through boundedly many through-wire blocks. Split or join those at bounded cost and repeat the searches of Lemma 1.3.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.4", "orig": "Lemma 13.3, “Disjoint passages and reference walls.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The number of edge-disjoint actual monochromatic cycles surrounding a fixed macroscopic ball, using no deterministic initial wall edge, is tight.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in the initial wired square.</p><p class=\"li\">A generation begins with a homogeneous pocket whose wall is its reference wall. Continue while the retained same-color wall arc has a prefix of reference edges; begin a new generation when no reference edge remains, including every opposite-color switch.</p><p class=\"li\">Use finitely many targets and bounded radial horizons.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The number of edge-disjoint actual monochromatic cycles surrounding a fixed macroscopic ball, using no deterministic initial wall edge, is tight. Reference-wall generation counts are jointly tight. For the corresponding fixed-aspect rectangle passage count \\(N\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(N\\ge m)\\le(1-c)^{m-1},\n\\qquad\n\\sup_\\delta\\mathbb E e^{aN}&lt;\\infty\n\\quad(0&lt;a&lt;-\\log(1-c)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Surrounding cycles supply disjoint vertical passages in a fixed-height middle band. Bounded degree and Menger reduce edge-disjointness to vertex-disjoint passages in an ordinary color-lattice rectangle with induced FK law.</p><p>Prune terminal returns, then choose the leftmost open passage and repeat to its right. Auxiliary exterior spokes and minimizing left area show that selection reads only the selected path and its left side: an eligible path entering left gives a replacement excursion. If many passages existed, all but the selected one remain to its right.</p><p>Conditionally, another passage is blocked with uniform positive probability. If the selected path is within ten mesh steps of the right wall, force a short opposite-color connection at bounded finite-energy cost. Otherwise its right-side face-center shadow, loop-erased and terminally pruned, bounds a fresh opposite-grid simple quad with horizontal rows giving bounded extremal distance. The wall-crossing theorem gives a transversal blocker, completed at the path by a local segment and beyond the outer wall by bounded extension. It blocks every further vertex-disjoint vertical path. Thus selections have a geometric tail.</p><p>During a generation the same-color arc is a prefix of remaining reference edges followed by newly discovered actual edges. Clipping shortens it; fresh diagonals extend it or return to an earlier occurrence. Consumed edges are not restored. New reference cycles therefore consist of newly discovered diagonals and are edge-disjoint, separately by color. They surround the horizon’s target ball. Extract a surrounding cycle from any repeated wall walk. The first assertion bounds generations; sum over finite branches, allowing shared ancestral generations to be counted repeatedly.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Proposition 14.3, “The endpoint convention is determined.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The gap is the unique instantaneous reflected diffusion on \\([0,2\\pi]\\) with interior generator \\( \\mathcal Af(\\theta)= \\frac\\kappa2 f''(\\theta) +\\frac{\\kappa-4}{2}\\cot(\\theta/2)f'(\\theta). \\) The forcepoint has no …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use a limiting target branch from actual-wall peeling, parametrized by its own radial clock, with \\(\\theta=O-W\\in[0,2\\pi]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The gap is the unique instantaneous reflected diffusion on \\([0,2\\pi]\\) with interior generator\n\\[\n\\mathcal Af(\\theta)=\n\\frac\\kappa2 f''(\\theta)\n+\\frac{\\kappa-4}{2}\\cot(\\theta/2)f'(\\theta).\n\\]\nThe forcepoint has no singular push:\n\\[\nO_t=O_0+\\int_0^t\\cot(\\theta_s/2)\\,ds.\n\\]\nGlobally, including collisions,\n\\[\ndW_t=\\sqrt\\kappa\\,dB_t+\\frac{6-\\kappa}{2}\\cot(\\theta_t/2)\\,dt,\n\\qquad\ndO_t=\\cot(\\theta_t/2)\\,dt,\n\\]\nwith locally integrable drift.</p>", "proof": "<p>For smooth functions constant near endpoints, local interior identities glue into the \\(\\mathcal A\\)-martingale problem. Extend to smooth \\(f\\) with \\(f'(0)=f'(2\\pi)=0\\) by cutoffs with bounded second derivatives: \\(f'\\cot(\\theta/2)\\) stays bounded, and endpoint time is zero. Apply also to \\(f^2\\) for brackets.</p><p>Then \\(X=\\cos(\\theta/2)\\) solves\n\\[\ndX_t=\\frac{\\sqrt\\kappa}{2}\\sqrt{1-X_t^2}\\,dB_t\n-\\frac{3\\kappa-8}{8}X_t\\,dt.\n\\]\nIts coefficient is one-half Hölder and drift Lipschitz, so one-dimensional square-root uniqueness fixes its law. Zero endpoint time excludes stickiness.</p><p>Near either endpoint, scaled distance is a bounded stopped perturbation of a Bessel process of dimension\n\\[\n\\mathfrak d=3-\\frac8\\kappa\\in(1,2).\n\\]\nStopped Girsanov transfers Bessel regularity: \\(\\theta\\) is locally Hölder of every exponent below \\(1/2\\), and endpoint sets have Hausdorff dimension at most\n\\(d_0=1-\\mathfrak d/2&lt;1/2\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nd\\nu=dO-\\cot(\\theta/2)\\,dt.\n\\]\nChoose \\(p=1/2-\\eta&gt;d_0\\), \\(u_m=2^{-m}\\), \\(r_m=u_m^p\\). Hölder regularity makes every sufficiently fine endpoint-meeting dyadic block stay in gap zone \\(r_m/32\\). Lemma 16.2 and a union bound give probability at most\n\\[\nC(1+T/u_m)e^{-cu_m^{-\\eta}}\n\\]\nthat some such block has one-signed \\(O\\)-variation above \\(Cr_m\\). This is summable. Borel–Cantelli bounds variation by \\(Cu_m^p\\) on all sufficiently fine endpoint-meeting blocks.</p><p>Near each endpoint, \\(dO\\) and the cotangent time measure have one sign; the latter gives no mass to the endpoint set. Thus endpoint-supported \\(|\\nu|\\) is bounded by that variation. Cover the endpoint set by variable-length intervals with arbitrarily small sum of \\(p\\)-th powers, replace them by boundedly many comparable dyadic intervals, and conclude \\(\\nu=0\\).</p><p>A Bessel process of dimension greater than one is a semimartingale with integrable inverse-distance drift and no ordinary-coordinate local-time term. Undo the cosine coordinate near endpoints and use the interior equations elsewhere. This yields the global equations and the asserted instantaneous reflection.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "Lemma 14.1, “Interior characteristics.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The limiting local characteristics are \\( dW_t=\\sqrt\\kappa\\,dB_t+\\frac{6-\\kappa}{2}\\cot(\\theta_t/2)\\,dt, \\qquad dO_t=\\cot(\\theta_t/2)\\,dt. \\) Martingale identities retain tests of the entire causal history.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use finitely many deterministic targets and bounded radial horizons, localizing away from \\(\\theta=0,2\\pi\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The limiting local characteristics are\n\\[\ndW_t=\\sqrt\\kappa\\,dB_t+\\frac{6-\\kappa}{2}\\cot(\\theta_t/2)\\,dt,\n\\qquad dO_t=\\cot(\\theta_t/2)\\,dt.\n\\]\nMartingale identities retain tests of the entire causal history. The gap processes are continuously tight, and their numbers of fixed-size alternating swings are tight.</p>", "proof": "<p>Two ordered tests from Proposition 15 and the relative-convexity argument of Proposition 13 identify stopped chordal driver and bracket, with stopped second-moment bounds. On a short segment starting in an interior angular range, chordal/radial clock changes and coordinates are bounded above and below. Crossing a fixed interior angular interval in vanishing radial time is therefore unlikely at causal stops. A macroscopic increment from near an endpoint must cross such an interval. Aldous and vanishing within-update oscillations give continuous gap tightness and swing tightness.</p><p>After the full \\(W,O\\) tightness supplied in Lemma 16.3, localize on rational start ranges and inner continuity levels to pass identities jointly. The stopped chordal-to-radial target change, valid while the force access survives, gives exactly the displayed equations. No endpoint continuation is inferred from this coordinate change.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "Lemma 14.2, “Fast motion near coincidence.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The probability that \\(W\\) advances counterclockwise by \\(r\\) within radial time \\(u\\), while \\(\\theta<r/16\\) until that advance, is at most \\( C\\exp(-cr/\\sqrt u)+o(1). \\) The error vanishes in mesh at fixed \\(r,u\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Start at a causal stop with \\(\\theta&lt;r/16\\), where \\(r&gt;0\\) is small. Let \\(0&lt;u\\ll r^2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The probability that \\(W\\) advances counterclockwise by \\(r\\) within radial time \\(u\\), while \\(\\theta&lt;r/16\\) until that advance, is at most\n\\[\nC\\exp(-cr/\\sqrt u)+o(1).\n\\]\nThe error vanishes in mesh at fixed \\(r,u\\); limiting constants are uniform. Near \\(2\\pi\\), reverse motion direction and exchange colors.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the starting disk chart, radial ODE confines time-\\(u\\) growth to boundary depth \\(C\\sqrt u\\). A driver advance \\(r\\) forces positive traversal of a buffered angular corridor from \\(W_0+r/5\\) to \\(W_0+r/2\\). Otherwise an untouched arc near \\(W_0+5r/6\\) retains prepoint image within \\(O(\\sqrt u)\\) of its old angle: check ODE at depth a large multiple of \\(\\sqrt u\\), then use reflected analytic maps and a harmonic gradient bound. The tip cannot pass that untouched access, a contradiction.</p><p>Place \\(O(r/\\sqrt u)\\) disjoint buffered dual bars across the corridor, each at scale \\(\\sqrt u\\), from a dual corner of the primal-wall triangle to deeper than the permitted strip. Lemma 15.3 gives uniform conditional success. Complete each bar to the primal-base midpoint.</p><p>A successful bar prevents positive traversal before a dual reset. Follow traversed pairs and microscopic primal-restart connectors. They avoid actual dual paths. With primal on the left, traversing the fixed cap crosses its added segment only negatively; choosing that triangle for restart reaches its earlier port without crossing the segment. Other connectors avoid it, and consumed triangles are never reused. Positive crossing is impossible. While \\(\\theta&lt;r/16\\), no dual reset occurs. Multiply conditional failures. Negative motion of order \\(r\\) is impossible there since \\(O\\) is nondecreasing, including primal resets, and \\(W=O-\\theta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The \\(W,O\\) processes are continuously tight, and \\( \\operatorname{Var}_{[0,T]}(O), \\qquad \\int_0^T|\\cot(\\theta_t/2)|\\,dt \\) are tight.", "statement": "<p>Global tightness and possible endpoint measure, Section 14.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use bounded radial horizons and the interpolated peeled data \\(W,O,\\theta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The \\(W,O\\) processes are continuously tight, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}_{[0,T]}(O),\n\\qquad\n\\int_0^T|\\cot(\\theta_t/2)|\\,dt\n\\]\nare tight. In every limit the endpoint sets have zero radial Lebesgue measure, \\(O\\) has locally finite variation, and its deviation from the cotangent time integral can only be a measure supported on endpoint sets, nonnegative at \\(0\\) and nonpositive at \\(2\\pi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Away from endpoints use Lemma 16.1. In an endpoint zone, rapid exit to the interior is controlled by gap tightness; rapid motion staying there is controlled by Lemma 16.2 and the sign of \\(O\\). This gives continuous \\(W,O\\) tightness at variable stops.</p><p>Inside a sufficiently small endpoint zone, continuous forcepoint motion and all resets have the same sign, so variation during a visit is bounded by angular oscillation. Count visits by alternations between nested endpoint zones, whose number is tight. Outside them cotangent is bounded. This gives both displayed tight quantities.</p><p>The integral tightness forces zero time at endpoints. The boundary equation holds away from them. Any remaining finite-variation measure is therefore endpoint-supported, with signs prescribed by the reset rule.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Proposition 14.4, “Joint target tree.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The joint radial driving data converge to the target-invariant branching radial \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa(\\kappa-6)\\) tree.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use actual-wall peeling toward a finite family of deterministic interior targets, with bounded individual radial horizons.</p><p class=\"li\">Process separated active components in fixed priority order.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the instantaneous reflected endpoint convention of Proposition 16.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The joint radial driving data converge to the target-invariant branching radial \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa(\\kappa-6)\\) tree. Branches agree until their target kernels separate and have independent continuation noises afterward, conditional on the common causal history at separation. Successive full turns reverse the force side.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use common parameter \\(v\\), the sum of accrued target clocks, freezing each at its horizon. Every \\(t_i(v)\\) is nondecreasing and \\(1\\)-Lipschitz; flat clocks have constant states. Shared targets run together, separated components serially. Lemma 15.1 ensures no advance information is gained by working elsewhere. All tightness and martingale identities retain full causal tests.</p><p>Take joint limits. In each own clock, Proposition 16 gives the branch equation. Its martingale part \\(M_i\\), in common time, satisfies\n\\[\nd\\langle M_i\\rangle=\\kappa\\,dt_i.\n\\]\nWhile two targets share a domain, with \\(\\xi=\\Phi_i(z_j)\\),\n\\[\ndt_j=P^2dt_i,\\qquad\nd\\log|\\xi|=Pdt_i,\\qquad\nP=\\frac{1-|\\xi|^2}{|e^{iW_i}-\\xi|^2}.\n\\]\nDisk Möbius maps relate marked prepoints. On \\(|\\xi|&lt;1\\), these regular identities pass to the limit. Kernel convergence preserves interior connecting paths, giving shared growth up to kernel separation.</p><p>If limiting kernels separate before discrete components split, then \\(|\\xi|\\to1\\). On subintervals where both gaps are interior, set \\(w=e^{iW_i},o=e^{iO_i}\\). The identity\n\\[\n|F_\\xi(w)-F_\\xi(o)|\n=\\frac{(1-|\\xi|^2)|w-o|}{|w-\\xi||o-\\xi|}\n\\]\nshows one of \\(w,o\\) is \\(O(1-|\\xi|)\\) from \\(\\xi\\). The choice cannot switch on a connected strict-angle interval. Then \\(P\\) is respectively of order \\((1-|\\xi|)^{-1}\\) or \\(1-|\\xi|\\), forcing one limiting clock flat. After exact splitting, serial clocks have disjoint time interiors. Exhaust the strict-angle open sets; endpoint sets have zero own-clock mass. Covariance domination gives\n\\[\nd\\langle M_i,M_j\\rangle=0\n\\]\nafter kernel separation.</p><p>To identify independence, choose the first target as representative in each accessible active group. Its evolution determines other states and clock changes until the next intrinsic separation or freeze stage. Accumulate its representative suffix\n\\[\nN_i(v)=\\int_0^v\n\\mathbf1_{\\{i\\text{ active representative}\\}}\\,dM_i.\n\\]\nThese martingales are pairwise orthogonal. At deterministic terminal common time \\(V=\\sum_iT_i\\), divide by \\(\\sqrt\\kappa\\) and append independent Brownian motions, independent of the terminal sigma-field. The completed martingales have infinite brackets. Knight’s theorem gives independent Brownian motions in their respective bracket clocks, whose restrictions supply the original representative noises.</p><p>For conditional independence, restart at any separation or freeze stage \\(S\\), localize brackets, and apply the same argument under \\(\\mathbb P(\\cdot\\mid E)\\) for every positive-probability \\(E\\in\\mathcal G_S\\). Product Wiener law is unchanged, hence continuation noises are independent of \\(\\mathcal G_S\\). No terminal-bracket or future-separation conditioning is used.</p><p>Pathwise uniqueness of the cosine-gap equation and forcepoint integral determines evolution through each next stage. Induct over finitely many target groups. The resulting equations and side reversals are the target-invariant \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa(\\kappa-6)\\) tree. Increase finite horizons.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Proposition 15.7, “Identification away from the square wall.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every positive lattice-limit loop with an interior point has a canonical label.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in a fixed primal-wired square with actual wall triangles.</p><p class=\"li\">Draw paired tile turns disjointly within midpoint disks of radius less than \\(\\delta/100\\). All discrete loops are then simple, disjoint, and primal-left oriented.</p><p class=\"li\">Couple subsequential loop limits with the target tree of Proposition 17 for a countable dense deterministic interior set \\(\\mathcal Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let canonical nested \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_\\kappa\\) loops be constructed from this tree. For target \\(z\\), write \\(b_k\\) for alternating endpoint hits, \\(a_k\\) for the last starting-endpoint visit before \\(b_k\\), and \\(\\widetilde L_z^k\\) for the successful full-turn front completed by the tree.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L_z^k\\) be the limit of the \\(k\\)-th actual discrete winding-loop label.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every positive lattice-limit loop with an interior point has a canonical label. Matched elements have identical absolute winding indicators on \\(\\mathcal Z\\). If either matched element stays away from the square wall, they are equal as curves modulo reparametrization and orientation, with collection multiplicity retained. Without that restriction, the canonical traversal is still contained in cyclic order in its lattice-limit partner.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 18.3 and convergence of true turn times exhaust all positive winding labels around every dense target: positive target clearance bounds the required horizon, while full-turn counts on it are tight.</p><p>Every positive lattice-limit loop with an interior point has a winding-one disk by Lemma 18.1, hence contains a dense target and is one of its labels. Lemma 18.6 makes the dense-target winding indicators identical in matched collections.</p><p>If an interior lattice trace point lay outside its canonical trace, a small avoiding ball would have constant canonical winding. Lemma 18.1 supplies disks of both lattice winding statuses there, each containing a dense target, contradiction. Ordered containment gives reverse interior trace inclusion. If either element is wall-away, connectedness prevents the other from reaching the wall without adding an unmatched interior trace point. Full traces are equal.</p><p>The cyclic representative selection from Lemma 18.5 has a continuous nondecreasing degree-one generalized inverse. Its plateaus correspond to possible inserted traversal portions. If one varied, choose a varying subarc away from its anchor. Each visited trace value has a unique canonical parameter; otherwise two canonical visits plus the inserted visit violate Lemma 18.1. Compactness makes this inverse assignment continuous, so it sweeps a nontrivial canonical interval consisting of unique visits. Lemma 18.2 contradicts that. All inserted portions are constant, proving curve equality with multiplicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.1", "orig": "Lemma 15.1, “Positive-law loop geometry.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They are tight for one-to-one matching of uniform loop curves; constant limits may be discarded.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use complete loop collections in the fixed wired square and the disjoint drawing of Proposition 18.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They are tight for one-to-one matching of uniform loop curves; constant limits may be discarded. Positive limits almost surely satisfy:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. every deterministic interior point avoids all loops, and absolute winding about any avoided point is \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) and stable under convergence;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. no interior point has three distinct visits in total across all collection elements, counting multiplicity and repeated traversal;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. every neighborhood of an interior trace point contains open disks of both absolute winding statuses for that loop.</p>", "proof": "<p>In a fixed vertical band, each radial medial passage has an actual dual bank. Cropping the band, separators divide it into strips; accompanying dual paths occupy distinct actual crossing components, with at most two assignments per strip. The internal dual rectangle has arbitrary induced boundary data and no crossing through the deterministic primal wall. Lemma 15.4’s blocker argument bounds component counts. Horizontal bands work likewise.</p><p>Finite band covers bound both the number of loops above a diameter cutoff and their successive displacement counts. Subdivide loops at first-displacement times at scales \\(2^{-j}\\), preserving older marks. Their numbers are tight. Diagonalize positions and cyclic orders and assign nested parameter intervals with mesh tending to zero. This proves curve and collection tightness while retaining multiplicities.</p><p>RSW circuits obstruct an arm from a deterministic point to fixed positive distance, proving avoidance. Winding of simple approximants is \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) and stable away from their trace. Three positive visits, even on different elements, give six medial passages; territorial ordering gives six alternating arms. The bulk six-arm bound and grid union exclude such points.</p><p>For both-side visibility, fix a rational buffered core rectangle and color \\(X\\). With high probability as \\(m\\to\\infty\\), every actual macroscopic crossing cluster acquires an \\(X\\)-circuit enclosing a disk of radius \\(c/m\\). First label crossing components up to a middle row; their count is tight. In \\(m\\) fresh transverse slabs, whenever a label survives to a new row, select its leftmost simple path from its sources. Spokes and left-area minimization reveal only its left side. At middle slab height choose a right-side point distance \\(h/16\\), \\(h\\asymp1/m\\), from the path. The fresh right graph dominates a free comparison in a buffered ball, since omitted attachments go through the actually connected selected path. RSW and FKG give two connected concentric circuits, the outer meeting that path and the inner enclosing a disk on its right. Conditional failure costs \\(1-c'\\) per slab. Truncate initial labels and iterate.</p><p>A varying limiting loop portion crosses one of countably many rational core tests. Its two actual color banks acquire attached disk-circuits on opposite sides. Choose the patch smaller than loop diameter so neither circuit contains the whole loop. Their enclosed disks give the two winding statuses, with positive clearance preserved under subsequences. Countable tests prove the assertion at every interior trace neighborhood.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.2", "orig": "Lemma 15.2, “Default continuation and local double visits.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The remaining traversal of \\(\\widetilde L_z^k\\) is the default strand from \\(W_s\\) to \\(O_s\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the canonical tree of Proposition 17.</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(s\\in(a_k,b_k)\\), tip and force are distinct.</p><p class=\"li\">The established branching-SLE/CLE construction is used for \\(4&lt;\\kappa&lt;8\\); here \\(\\kappa-6&gt;-2\\), and reversal weights \\(\\kappa-6,0\\) are at least \\(\\kappa/2-4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The remaining traversal of \\(\\widetilde L_z^k\\) is the default strand from \\(W_s\\) to \\(O_s\\). In a chart sending them to \\(0,\\infty\\), every finite part is ordinary chordal \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa\\), recorded by branches to targets in \\(\\mathcal Z\\) remaining accessible sufficiently high above it. Every nonempty nonconstant traversal interval of a canonical loop contains two visits to a square-interior point.</p>", "proof": "<p>The stopped chordal equivalence applies while force access survives. After the original target is cut off, continue through the branch retaining that access. A bounded finite chordal portion has bounded hull and driver; an open set of sufficiently high chart targets remains accessible and has separated tip/force angles. Density supplies such a target in \\(\\mathcal Z\\). Target invariance makes these finite continuations consistent and exhausts the default strand.</p><p>The continuum continuity and reversal inputs complete Sheffield’s exploration-tree construction to continuous, conformally and anticonformally invariant nonsimple CLE loops in the stated parameter range. At finite radial times the branch and original square boundary form a locally connected continuum; Torhorst gives locally connected complementary boundaries, so prime-access maps extend continuously.</p><p>Lemma 14.5 supplies chordal double visits in every rational time interval. Apply stopped chordal equivalence simultaneously over the countable targets, rational own-clock starts, and strict localizations. Every full-turn interval contains such a start. For a default-completion interval, choose a sufficiently high dense target recording a finite portion containing it, then a rational start in that target’s regular clock. This uses one universal countable probability-one event and never applies Markov at a postselected excursion start.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.3", "orig": "Lemma 15.3, “Discrete winding order.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "An episode succeeds exactly when its terminal switch changes wall polarity.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">An episode begins by opening a homogeneous wall cap and ends at the next switch retaining a homogeneous pocket.</p><p class=\"li\">Its color is the starting wall color.</p><p class=\"li\">It is successful if its original loop winds around the target.</p><p class=\"li\">The target is macroscopically separated from consumed tiles and stars.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>An episode succeeds exactly when its terminal switch changes wall polarity. Polarity changes enumerate all surrounding loops from outside inward. A surrounding loop with positive target clearance is reached before a finite radial horizon depending on that clearance. A successful switch on a bounded horizon has macroscopic target clearance.</p>", "proof": "<p>At the opened cap the starting-color side is outside the current loop, and the other side inside. Actual banks preserve connections to those sides. At a same-color wall hit, the retained pocket connects along its bank to the cap. Past pairs are removed, the new actual diagonal blocks crossing, and the old-loop continuation stays on the other side. Comparing retained and starting colors proves the polarity criterion.</p><p>All other unstarted surrounding loops remain intact in the retained simply connected component: actual diagonals cannot cross them, including deterministic wall pairs. A loop entered at the new cap is outermost among remaining surrounding candidates. Thus successful episodes enumerate them.</p><p>Before and during its episode, growth stays outside its enclosed target ball, so conformal radius stays at least that radius, bounding radial time. Conversely Koebe gives a positive surviving target ball on any bounded horizon, and the successful loop lies outside it up to vanishing step errors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.4", "orig": "Lemma 15.4, “Endpoint resets cannot be skipped.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "From \\(\\theta\\to0\\), returning to a fixed positive angle before a primal-pocket reset has probability tending to zero, mesh first and endpoint tolerance second.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use completed peeled states on bounded radial horizons.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>From \\(\\theta\\to0\\), returning to a fixed positive angle before a primal-pocket reset has probability tending to zero, mesh first and endpoint tolerance second. The analogous assertion holds near \\(2\\pi\\) for dual resets. True discrete polarity-change times \\(b_k^n\\) converge in probability to \\(b_k\\) on sufficiently large finite horizons.</p><p>In a bounded-horizon successful episode, after a fixed interior advance, retreat arbitrarily close to its starting endpoint before success has vanishing probability in the same order of limits.</p>", "proof": "<p>Near zero the dual wall and end sides lie in a tiny stopping-chart neighborhood. Until a primal reset, the growing dual bank remains connected to it. In successive buffered dyadic chart half-annuli impose actual primal wall-to-wall paths. Each has positive conditional probability by Lemma 15.3. One confines the connected dual bank and slit to a small sector. Such a sector has vanishing new angular footprint, by interior convergence and reflection away from it, and cannot produce a fixed angular advance. Iterate. Exchange colors at \\(2\\pi\\).</p><p>True polarity changes approach the opposite endpoint, so uniform angle convergence prevents premature limiting switch times. After a limiting endpoint hit, instantaneous reflection makes a positive excursion in any additional time almost surely. The reset estimate prevents such an excursion without the corresponding reset. Induct over turns, taking tolerances to zero.</p><p>For retreat, stop at approaches to the starting endpoint after a fixed advance. A later-successful episode must then advance without its same-color reset, since that reset would end it. The first estimate controls each test, and angular tightness bounds the number of fixed-size swing tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.5", "orig": "Lemma 15.5, “The canonical traversal is contained in order.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The full traversal of \\(\\widetilde L_z^k\\) occurs in cyclic order within \\(L_z^k\\), using at most one turn and allowing inserted traversal portions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use joint limits of the tree and actual winding labels \\(L_z^k\\), with equality indicators for finite label lists stabilized along the subsequence.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The full traversal of \\(\\widetilde L_z^k\\) occurs in cyclic order within \\(L_z^k\\), using at most one turn and allowing inserted traversal portions. Equal canonical labels have equal lattice-limit collection elements.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 18.4 puts every compact subinterval of \\((a_k,b_k)\\) in the prelimit successful episode. Fix rational \\(s\\) in it. Between any \\(t&lt;t'\\) in the front, the target kernel strictly shrinks. A new boundary point in the earlier kernel belongs to the canonical trace. Every small ball there must meet new prelimit growth; otherwise it stays clear of boundary and, meeting a later internal patch, would extend the limiting kernel through its alleged boundary point. New prelimit pieces trace the successful original loop up to vanishing local errors.</p><p>Apply to finite ordered interval lists, moving completed stops inside them. For finitely many default-continuation intervals after \\(s\\), choose a dense target sufficiently high above their bounded chordal portion. It shares the domain at \\(s\\), and interior connecting paths prevent earlier discrete splitting. Its tip/force angles stay separated on the selected portion, so its discrete branch continues the same loop without reset. The new-boundary test applies in its clock.</p><p>Shrinking intervals and increasing continuation ranges gives ordered representatives for every finite canonical point list. Compactness and diagonalization on dense parameter values give a nondecreasing cyclic selection, completed by continuity. It uses at most one discrete turn because the episode and no-reset continuation do not pass the released cap twice. Nonconstant canonical intervals cannot collapse to one representative.</p><p>If equal canonical labels had distinct lattice-limit elements, each would contain the same canonical double visit from Lemma 18.2, violating Lemma 18.1’s bound of two total visits. Thus equal labels give the same element.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.6", "orig": "Lemma 15.6, “The converse label identification.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Almost surely, simultaneously for all \\(z,w\\in\\mathcal Z\\) and \\(k,j\\ge1\\), \\( L_z^k=L_w^j \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\widetilde L_z^k=\\widetilde L_w^j. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the labels and coupling of Proposition 18, with equality understood as collection-element equality.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Almost surely, simultaneously for all \\(z,w\\in\\mathcal Z\\) and \\(k,j\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nL_z^k=L_w^j\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\widetilde L_z^k=\\widetilde L_w^j.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Only the forward implication remains. For shared discrete labels, the targets cannot split before entering that common intact loop. Choose horizons including both successful switches.</p><p>While domains are shared, put \\(\\xi=\\Phi_z(w)\\), and stop at first \\(|\\xi|\\ge1-e\\), within \\(|\\xi|\\le1-e/2\\) up to negligible update error. Exact splitting cannot precede this regular range.</p><p>An unstarted loop surrounding both targets at that stop becomes unlikely as \\(e\\downarrow0\\). Place many dyadic chart half-annular bars around the boundary direction of \\(\\xi\\), discarding boundedly many scales meeting marked accesses. Each actual-color transversal, completed at an opposite-color triangle if necessary, separates the target-near sector from the \\(z\\)-sector and a remote wall portion. An unstarted simple loop surrounding both targets must cross it twice; it cannot cross the actual path and can use the one added cap segment only once. Thus one screen precludes it. Lemma 15.3 and iteration make failure small.</p><p>If the common loop has already progressed, Lemma 18.4 prevents a near-starting-endpoint state from later succeeding without resetting. Therefore, except on a small event, the two successful episodes share a pre-separation state with both angles bounded inside. The chart correspondence is regular there. Their successful fronts cannot retreat to starting endpoints before the switches. Choose horizons, separation threshold, interior margins, and retreat tolerance in order. In the limit their endpoint histories, successful excursion start, front, and default force-access continuation agree. Their canonical elements coincide. Let errors vanish and use countability.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Theorem 15.8, “Nested full-plane convergence for \\(q<4\\).”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The loop collections converge along every mesh sequence to nested whole-plane \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_{\\kappa(q)}\\), with all nesting generations, multiplicities, and traversals retained.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(1\\le q&lt;4\\), and use the complete medial loop collection of the specified free infinite-volume critical square-lattice FK law, with the spherical one-to-one matching topology of Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The loop collections converge along every mesh sequence to nested whole-plane \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_{\\kappa(q)}\\), with all nesting generations, multiplicities, and traversals retained. The limiting law is Möbius invariant.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a compact disk \\(K\\), actual monochromatic circuits in successive buffered annuli show that the probability of any loop visiting \\(K\\) and reaching a distant circle tends to zero, uniformly for fine mesh and larger wired squares.</p><p>Couple the free plane law and a growing wired square identically on a growing intermediate disk. Bracket exterior partitions by free and wired, expose actual boundary-attachment clusters in the stronger sample, and use dual RSW screens to prevent them reaching the deep interior. Once separated, conditional laws agree and switches can be identical. The escape bound then makes entire loops visiting \\(K\\) agree.</p><p>Proposition 18 identifies all wall-away macroscopic square limits with canonical CLE curves. Possible discrepancies reach the square wall; the escape bound makes this unlikely for loops visiting \\(K\\). The same holds for canonical loops because they are contained in their lattice partners. Thus large-square CLE and free-plane lattice configurations have arbitrarily close matchings in fixed windows.</p><p>The established large-domain nested-CLE limit agrees, for \\(4&lt;\\kappa&lt;8\\), with whole-plane branching CLE and is invariant under inversion and Möbius maps. Its comparisons use conformal identifications approaching identity on compact sets, not equality at unchanged coordinates. Squares and disks have the same normalized local limit.</p><p>For spherical matching accuracy \\(\\varepsilon\\), choose \\(K\\) with spherical exterior diameter less than \\(\\varepsilon\\). Loops wholly outside \\(K\\) are irrelevant. Escape tails control those visiting \\(K\\) but leaving a larger disk. The preceding comparisons make large-square CLE laws Cauchy in spherical matching, via the same free-plane lattice law. Any limit has the established local whole-plane CLE law, which together with these tails determines the full spherical collection. Every lattice subsequence has that limit. Labels included all \\(k\\) with unbounded horizons, so no nesting generations are lost.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For fixed \\(a/b<1/16\\), \\( \\tfrac12p_0(a/(64b)) \\le\\liminf_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\pi_{1,\\delta}^\\square(a,b) \\le\\limsup_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\pi_{1,\\delta}^\\square(a,b) \\le p_0(4a/b), \\) and \\(p_0(t)\\asymp t^{5/48}\\) as …", "statement": "<p>Buffered annular arm comparisons, Sections 16.1–16.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set \\(q=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi_{1,\\delta}^\\square(a,b)\\) be the critical square-bond primal one-arm probability across the circular annulus from radius \\(a\\) to \\(b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For even \\(m\\), let \\(\\pi_{m,\\delta}^\\square(a,b)\\) and \\(\\pi_{m,\\delta}^\\triangle(a,b)\\) be cyclically alternating arm probabilities for square bonds and triangular sites.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma\\) have whole-plane \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_6\\) law. Let \\(Z(\\Gamma;a,b)\\) mean that no loop contained in \\(a&lt;|z|&lt;b\\) has nonzero absolute winding about \\(0\\), and put\n  \\[\n  p_0(t)=\\mathbb P(Z(\\Gamma;t,1)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g_m(t)\\) be the triangular fixed-annulus alternating-arm limit in the parallelogram convention used in the cited arm theorem.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For fixed \\(a/b&lt;1/16\\),\n\\[\n\\tfrac12p_0(a/(64b))\n\\le\\liminf_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\pi_{1,\\delta}^\\square(a,b)\n\\le\\limsup_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\pi_{1,\\delta}^\\square(a,b)\n\\le p_0(4a/b),\n\\]\nand \\(p_0(t)\\asymp t^{5/48}\\) as \\(t\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>For each fixed even \\(m\\), one fixed \\(K\\ge64\\) gives\n\\[\ng_m(t/K)\n\\le\\liminf_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\pi_{m,\\delta}^\\square(t,1)\n\\le\\limsup_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\pi_{m,\\delta}^\\square(t,1)\n\\le g_m(Kt),\n\\]\nfor \\(0&lt;t&lt;1/K\\), with\n\\[\ng_m(t)=t^{(m^2-1)/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThese are fixed-annulus envelopes, not an assertion of an unbuffered square-annulus probability limit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 20.1 and scale invariance reduce either-color probabilities to \\(p_0\\). The established triangular full-limit identification of the no-surrounding-loop event, and the triangular one-arm exponent, give\n\\(p_0(t)\\asymp t^{5/48}\\). Either-color probability lies between a one-color probability and twice it; square duality is primal law translated by half a mesh, absorbed by collars. This gives the one-arm envelope.</p><p>Lemma 20.2 compares even square arms with triangular arms at buffered radii in both directions. The triangular fixed-annulus arm theorem gives exponent \\((m^2-1)/12\\) in parallelogram annuli. Deterministic circle/parallelogram inclusions dilate rims by fixed factors. Absorb these and the factor-eight buffers into one fixed \\(K\\), yielding the stated envelopes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(0<a<b/4\\) and fine mesh, \\( B_\\delta(a/2,2b)\\subset Z(\\Gamma_\\delta;a,b) \\subset B_\\delta(2a,b/2). \\) The matching-topology open/closed comparisons imply, for \\(16a<b\\) …", "statement": "<p>One arm from surrounding loops, Section 16.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_\\delta(a,b)\\) mean a square-bond radial arm of either color.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_o(\\Gamma;a,b)\\) and \\(H_c(\\Gamma;a,b)\\) mean existence of a nonzero-winding loop contained respectively in the open and closed annulus.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For fixed \\(0&lt;a&lt;b/4\\) and fine mesh,\n\\[\nB_\\delta(a/2,2b)\\subset Z(\\Gamma_\\delta;a,b)\n\\subset B_\\delta(2a,b/2).\n\\]\nThe matching-topology open/closed comparisons imply, for \\(16a&lt;b\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(Z(\\Gamma;a/4,4b))\n\\le\\liminf_\\delta\\mathbb P(B_\\delta(a,b))\n\\le\\limsup_\\delta\\mathbb P(B_\\delta(a,b))\n\\le\\mathbb P(Z(\\Gamma;2a,b/2)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A surrounding complete loop separates rims, and actual paths of either color cannot cross it. Conversely, if neither color crosses a cropped annulus, planar cutset duality gives nested primal and dual circuits between its rims. The inner-color cluster is confined by the opposite outer circuit, and its outer medial boundary is a complete surrounding loop. Fixed collars absorb mesh displacement.</p><p>The event \\(H_o\\) is open: a witness has positive rim clearance and positive diameter, and close matching preserves winding. The event \\(H_c\\) is closed: witness diameters are bounded below, so local finiteness matches a subsequence to one fixed limit loop, preserving containment and winding. Use\n\\[\nH_o(a,b)\\subset H_c(a,b)\\subset H_o(a/2,2b)\n\\]\nand Portmanteau with Proposition 19. Combine at rescaled radii to obtain the stated envelope. No indicator continuity is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For either lattice \\(X=\\square,\\triangle\\), \\( A^X_{m,\\delta}(a/2,2b)\\subset T_m(\\Gamma_\\delta^X;a,b), \\) \\( T_m(\\Gamma_\\delta^X;a/2,2b)\\subset A^X_{m,\\delta}(a,b). \\) The event \\(T_m(\\cdot;a,b)\\) is open, and for …", "statement": "<p>Even alternating arms from loop traversals, Section 16.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For even \\(m\\), let \\(T_m(\\Gamma;a,b)\\) mean that \\(m\\) parameter intervals on loop elements, with disjoint interiors on each individual loop, each visit both \\(|z|&lt;a\\) and \\(|z|&gt;b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Intervals on distinct collection elements count separately. The definition retains traversal and is independent of roots and orientations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For either lattice \\(X=\\square,\\triangle\\),\n\\[\nA^X_{m,\\delta}(a/2,2b)\\subset T_m(\\Gamma_\\delta^X;a,b),\n\\]\n\\[\nT_m(\\Gamma_\\delta^X;a/2,2b)\\subset A^X_{m,\\delta}(a,b).\n\\]\nThe event \\(T_m(\\cdot;a,b)\\) is open, and for \\(4a&lt;b\\),\n\\[\n\\overline{T_m(\\cdot;a,b)}\\subset T_m(\\cdot;2a,b/2).\n\\]\nSquare and triangular matching limits are both \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_6\\), and for \\(64r&lt;R\\),\n\\[\n\\limsup_\\delta\\pi_{m,\\delta}^\\square(r,R)\n\\le\\liminf_\\delta\\pi_{m,\\delta}^\\triangle(8r,R/8),\n\\]\nwith the same inequality after interchanging lattices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Alternating simple arm subpaths cut the annulus into opposite-colored strips. Each strip supplies an interface crosscut belonging to a complete-loop interval, and arms separate these intervals. Conversely crop witnessing loop intervals to radial crosscuts and apply territorial arm counting. The same strip argument applies to triangular hexagonal boundaries. Buffers separate shared endpoints and absorb square drawing errors.</p><p>Witness loops have diameters bounded below. Close one-to-one matching transports witnessing parameter intervals, their order, and disjoint interiors, proving openness. For closure, match witnesses to finitely many relevant limit loops and pass to interval-endpoint subsequences. Separated rim visits prevent collapse; enlarged rims make limiting visits strict.</p><p>For triangular sites, RSW circuits reduce local matching to a fixed disk. The established full scaling-limit construction has a tight number of steps to find all loops above a cutoff, with joint tightness of every fixed number of ordered exploration curves and boundaries. Each loop is a bounded concatenation of subcurves, so subcurve endpoint compactness gives ordered-curve matching tightness. Forgetting multiplicity gives the known CLE limit.</p><p>There are no duplicate positive elements: each positive CLE loop has a double visit by Lemma 18.2. Two copies would create at least three total visits and hence six radial sublegs; the uniform triangular six-arm estimate excludes such points by a grid union. Thus triangular matching has precisely CLE multiplicities.</p><p>Apply Portmanteau to \\(T_m\\) and its buffered closure in the common CLE law. The two deterministic inclusions yield the factor-eight comparison and its reverse without requiring zero-probability rim tangencies.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 2, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [6, 11, "cite"], [6, 56, "cite"], [7, 3, "cite"], [8, 3, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [9, 3, "cite"], [10, 3, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 3, "cite"], [11, 33, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [12, 23, "cite"], [12, 33, "cite"], [13, 12, "part"], [14, 22, "cite"], [15, 23, "cite"], [15, 27, "cite"], [16, 20, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 16, "cite"], [20, 32, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [23, 1, "cite"], [23, 28, "cite"], [23, 35, "cite"], [23, 36, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [26, 23, "cite"], [27, 23, "cite"], [28, 30, "cite"], [28, 31, "cite"], [28, 35, "cite"], [29, 28, "cite"], [30, 31, "cite"], [30, 35, "cite"], [31, 35, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [32, 34, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 31, "cite"], [35, 1, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [36, 1, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [36, 39, "cite"], [37, 45, "cite"], [37, 53, "cite"], [37, 55, "cite"], [38, 40, "cite"], [38, 46, "cite"], [38, 49, "cite"], [39, 45, "cite"], [40, 41, "cite"], [40, 42, "cite"], [40, 43, "cite"], [41, 37, "cite"], [41, 43, "cite"], [42, 43, "cite"], [43, 37, "cite"], [44, 46, "cite"], [44, 52, "cite"], [44, 59, "cite"], [45, 44, "cite"], [46, 0, "cite"], [47, 48, "cite"], [48, 46, "cite"], [49, 50, "cite"], [49, 52, "cite"], [50, 46, "cite"], [51, 46, "cite"], [51, 65, "cite"], [52, 46, "cite"], [53, 0, "cite"], [53, 59, "cite"], [54, 53, "cite"], [54, 62, "cite"], [55, 53, "part"], [56, 53, "cite"], [56, 60, "cite"], [56, 67, "cite"], [56, 69, "cite"], [57, 53, "cite"], [57, 64, "cite"], [58, 0, "cite"], [58, 62, "cite"], [59, 58, "part"], [60, 58, "cite"], [60, 61, "cite"], [61, 59, "cite"], [62, 0, "cite"], [62, 63, "cite"], [62, 65, "cite"], [63, 0, "cite"], [63, 70, "cite"], [64, 63, "cite"], [64, 68, "cite"], [65, 63, "cite"], [65, 68, "cite"], [65, 73, "cite"], [66, 63, "cite"], [67, 68, "cite"], [67, 69, "cite"], [68, 63, "cite"], [69, 63, "cite"], [70, 0, "cite"], [70, 72, "cite"], [71, 2, "cite"], [72, 71, "cite"], [73, 71, "cite"]]});
